\"period\": \"CY2026\"},\n   348\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n   349\t      \"basis\": \"third_party\",\n   350\t      \"value\": 800,\n   351\t      \"low\": null,\n   352\t      \"high\": null,\n   353\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   354\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e2759\",\n   355\t      \"quote_number\": \"$800 billion\",\n   356\t      \"qualifier\": \"nearly\",\n   357\t      \"inputs\": null,\n   358\t      \"operation\": null,\n   359\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n   360\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   361\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   362\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   363\t    },\n   364\t    {\n   365\t      \"id\": \"F20\",\n   366\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"Microsoft, Alphabet, Amazon, Meta and Oracle\", \"measure\": \"capital expenditure\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\n   367\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n   368\t      \"basis\": \"third_party\",\n   369\t      \"value\": 795,\n   370\t      \"low\": null,\n   371\t      \"high\": null,\n   372\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   373\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e5055\",\n   374\t      \"quote_number\": \"$795 billion\",\n   375\t      \"qualifier\": \"roughly\",\n   376\t      \"inputs\": null,\n   377\t      \"operation\": null,\n   378\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n   379\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   380\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   381\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   382\t    }\n   383\t  ],\n   384\t  \"answers\": [\n   385\t    {\n   386\t      \"view_id\": \"v6\",\n   387\t      \"shape\": \"forecast\",\n   388\t      \"rung\": 4,\n   389\t      \"reference_unavailable\": null,\n   390\t      \"reasons\": \"Reference: outside tallies put the four companies' combined 2026 capital spending at roughly $720-745bn (Zacks, e5014; about $745bn tracking, e1856), inside rung 4 ($700-800bn). Build: the three companies with full-year guidance add to $540-570bn (F4): Alphabet $195-205bn after raising from $180-190bn (e1254), Meta $125-145bn after a raise (e1549), Amazon $220bn after raising from $200bn (e1649). Microsoft gives no calendar-year guidance in the evidence; its January-March 2026 quarter was $30.88bn, up 84% (e922), and a continued ramp of about 16% a quarter gives about $156bn, while the tally less the other three implies $150-205bn; I use $165bn (F10, my estimate). Total capital expenditure is therefore about $720bn (F11; $705-735bn across the guidance ranges). The question asks for data-center spending, which is narrower than total capex, so I apply a 98% data-center share (F14, my estimate): the companies indicated more than $730bn of AI-infrastructure spending (e1947), at or above the total tally; Meta's $130bn of AI capex (e3686) sits inside its $125-145bn guidance; Amazon's raise was mostly memory chips; outside estimates put spending on data centers near $750bn (e1416, e4082). The share is bounded at about 96-100% by these passages, giving data-center capex of about $706bn (F15), rung 4; the supported floor from e1947 (more than $730bn of AI infrastructure) also lies in rung 4. Rung 5 is not supported: the $795-800bn figures (NVIDIA, e2759; BofA, e5055) cover five companies, Oracle among them in BofA's list, so the four sit below $800bn, and the highest four-company tally is $760bn (e2458). Pushing up: three mid-year guidance raises, rising memory costs that lift the dollar cost of the same build (e1649), and strong cloud demand (Azure backlog $678bn, e4405; Google Cloud +82%, e4406; AWS's best quarter in four years, e4404). Pushing down: about $130bn of US data-center projects blocked or delayed this year (e122, not specific to these four), and grid-connection and electrician constraints (e5292, e1545); the memory shortage (e4566) limits volume but raises price, so it cuts both ways. The forecast sits close to the $700bn edge, so the main risk is rung 3 if Microsoft's ramp or the data-center share comes in lower; the repeated raises make an undershoot of guidance less likely. The many reprints of NVIDIA's top-five figure count as one claim (e4564's '$1.3 billion' for 2027 is a misprint of $1.3 trillion).\",\n   391\t      \"why\": null,\n   392\t      \"cites\": [\"e5014\", \"e1856\", \"e1254\", \"e1549\", \"e1649\", \"e922\", \"e4405\", \"e1947\", \"e3686\", \"e1416\", \"e4082\", \"e2458\", \"e2759\", \"e5055\", \"e4406\", \"e4404\", \"e122\", \"e5292\", \"e1545\", \"e4566\"],\n   393\t      \"pushes\": [\n   394\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e1254\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   395\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e1549\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   396\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e1649\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   397\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e922\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   398\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5014\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   399\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e1856\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   400\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e1947\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   401\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e2458\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   402\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e1416\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   403\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4082\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   404\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e2759\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   405\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5055\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   406\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4405\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   407\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4406\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   408\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4404\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   409\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e3686\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n   410\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e122\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n   411\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5292\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n   412\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e1545\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"},\n   413\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4566\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n   414\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e2079\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n   415\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4790\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n   416\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4567\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n   417\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5081\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n   418\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5105\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n   419\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5114\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n   420\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5358\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n   421\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5376\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n   422\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4766\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n   423\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4514\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n   424\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4564\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n   425\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5170\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n   426\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e2081\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n   427\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4807\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n   428\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4417\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n   429\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e3142\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n   430\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5150\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n   431\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5293\", \"push\": \"none\"},\n   432\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5200\", \"push\": \"none\"}\n   433\t      ],\n   434\t      \"theme_pushes\": []\n   435\t    }\n   436\t  ]\n   437\t}\nS/system.txt\n     1\tYou are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging the current rungs of a family of related number theses: one company's quantities that overlap, such as a total and its segment, a level and its growth, or a later year that rests on an earlier year's base. Use only the supplied evidence and themes in force. Each rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n     2\t\n     3\t- Judge every member from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently) and the themes in force, as if for the first time. Earlier forecasts are not shown: whether a conclusion changed is compared afterwards.\n     4\t- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate whose pace you forecast, informed by guidance, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted subject to the evidence requirement below; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. Build the family's figures once and read every member's outcome from them: a part cannot exceed its whole, a figure that spans several periods or products gives a question only its share, and a quantity the family shares has one forecast. Compare each outcome with its rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n     5\t- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n     6\t- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n     7\t- \"Themes in force\" lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on a member and report it in that member's theme_pushes with the direction it pushes that member (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \"updated since this thesis was last judged\" may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n     8\t- Your conclusions must be consistent with each other and with the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open.\n     9\t- Return the family's figures and one answer per member, as the JSON schema defines them. Every number an answer rests on is a figure; a number you judge rather than read or compute is an estimate, stated as yours. If the chosen rung depends on a share, deduction or discount, show what in the passages supports its size or gives a bound that justifies the rung. If the evidence cannot support a forecast, answer unresolved and explain the gap. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\nS/user.txt\n     1\t[Family] members:\n     2\tThesis v6 (ai-capex, theme, focus \"Combined data-center capital expenditures of the four largest AI hyperscalers\", 2026, due 2027-02-28): How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?\n     3\tLadder: rung 1 Lowest range: Combined spending is below $500 billion. / rung 2 Lower-middle range: Combined spending is at least $500 billion and below $600 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Combined spending is at least $600 billion and below $700 billion. / rung 4 Upper-middle range: Combined spending is at least $700 billion and below $800 billion. / rung 5 Highest range: Combined spending is at least $800 billion.\n     4\t\n     5\tRelations: none.\n     6\tShared quantities, one forecast figure each; on every arithmetic path from an outcome, through rates and multiples too, the first figure of that quantity reached is its forecast: none.\n     7\tOutcome keys (population | measure | scope | period), in USD bn, to be used exactly: v6 = four largest AI hyperscalers | data-center capex | combined | CY2026.\n     8\t\n     9\t[Evidence in view for the family, 50 items]\n    10\t- e122 [third_party_view; Autonocion.com; original source Tom’s Hardware ; from gnews:417f6244f0aaa27123023deb 2026-07-06] Tom’s Hardware’s running tally said more than 75 U.S. data-center projects worth around $130 billion had been blocked or delayed so far in 2026.\n    11\t    Quote: More than 75 of these projects, worth around $130 billion, have been blocked or delayed across the US so far this year, according to a running tally at Tom’s Hardware .\n    12\t- e922 [fact; 247wallst.com; original source Microsoft ; from gnews:ba97f512051bcb3ff34f5b05 2026-07-22] Microsoft reported Q3 FY2026 capital expenditures of $30.88 billion, up 84% year over year.\n    13\t    Quote: Microsoft’s Q3 FY26 capex hit $30.88 billion, up 84%.\n    14\t- e1254 [party_statement; fool.com (Harsh Chauhan); original source Alphabet 2026-07-22; from gnews:ac966c48ae5afea2a79acaf3 2026-07-27] Alphabet raised its 2026 capital expenditure guidance to $195 billion–$205 billion, from $180 billion–$190 billion previously.\n    15\t    Quote: Alphabet released second-quarter results on July 22 and announced that it is raising its 2026 capex guidance to a range of $195 billion to $205 billion. The company had earlier projected $180 billion to $190 billion in capex for 2026.\n    16\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1174, e1446, e1648; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    17\t- e1416 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-29; from gnews:d67077a64d61e214e5d9ac06 2026-07-29] The author estimates that tech giants will spend $750 billion on data centers in calendar 2026 and says their spending could increase in calendar 2027.\n    18\t    Quote: Tech giants will spend an estimated $750 billion this year and could increase that spending next year.\n    19\t- e1545 [party_statement; Larry Fink, as quoted by Preston Fore, Fortune; original source Larry Fink ; from gnews:79bbaae99cc8279528469e6e 2026-08-01] BlackRock CEO Larry Fink said the U.S. would run short of electricians needed to build AI data centers.\n    20\t    Quote: “I’ve even told members of the Trump team that we’re going to run out of electricians that we need to build out AI data centers,” Fink said to an audience at CERAWeek, an S&P Global energy conference hosted in Houston. “We just don’t have enough.”\n    21\t- e1549 [party_statement; 247wallst.com, as reported by Joel South; original source Meta Platforms ; from gnews:c5ba7d4f41d0388a171eebcc 2026-08-01] Meta raised its FY26 capital expenditure guidance to $125 billion–$145 billion.\n    22\t    Quote: Meta Platforms ( NASDAQ:META ) raised its FY26 capex guidance to $125 billion to $145 billion\n    23\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e414; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    24\t- e1649 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source Amazon ; from gnews:275ccde6edc6d12657c32c3e 2026-08-06] Amazon raised its capital expenditure projection from $200 billion to $220 billion; the article says the increase was mostly due to higher memory chip costs.\n    25\t    Quote: Amazon (NASDAQ: AMZN) is doing the same, going from an estimated $200 billion to a new projection of $220 billion, although, in fairness, the increase was mostly due to higher memory chip costs.\n    26\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4567; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    27\t- e1856 [third_party_view; 247Wallst.com, by Gerelyn Terzo; original source 247Wallst.com, by Gerelyn Terzo ; from gnews:61ba718265df905f5dcc9dad 2026-08-11] Combined 2026 capital spending by the four largest hyperscalers was tracking near $745 billion.\n    28\t    Quote: Combined 2026 capital spending across the four largest hyperscalers is tracking near $745 billion\n    29\t- e1947 [party_statement; fool.com (Patrick Sanders); original source Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet ; from gnews:c8ac167e2356a75994ebc26f 2026-08-13] Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure in calendar 2026.\n    30\t    Quote: hyperscalers Amazon , Meta Platforms , Microsoft , and Alphabet have indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year\n    31\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1738, e2079; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    32\t- e2079 [party_statement; uk.finance.yahoo.com; original source Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers ; from gnews:1219126db2e6631aa1264142 2026-08-16] Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers guided combined 2026 capital spending of roughly $720 billion to $745 billion, about 77% above the prior year.\n    33\t    Quote: Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers whose cloud platforms host most of the world's AI workloads have together guided roughly $720 billion (€624bn) to $745 billion (€646bn) of capital spending in 2026, an increase of about 77% on last year.\n    34\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1947, e2177; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    35\t- e2081 [third_party_view; uk.finance.yahoo.com; original source Moody's ; from gnews:1219126db2e6631aa1264142 2026-08-16] Moody’s warned that hyperscaler-scale spending is reducing technology companies’ free cash flow and pushing them toward more borrowing.\n    36\t    Quote: Moody's has warned that spending on this scale is eating into free cash flow and pushing tech groups into heavier borrowing.\n    37\t- e2458 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source The top four hyperscalers ; from gnews:c8ea23246edff9fbbc981992 2026-08-23] The top four hyperscalers plan to spend $760 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n    38\t    Quote: The top four hyperscalers plan $760 billion in capex in 2026 alone.\n    39\t- e2759 [party_statement; Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26] NVIDIA said the five largest hyperscalers’ capital expenditures are expected to total nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n    40\t    Quote: With cloud industry backlog now greater than $2 trillion, CapEx by the top 5 hyperscalers is expected to reach nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n    41\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e592, e3353, e3556, e3557, e3621, e3647, e3651, e3729, e4224, e5377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    42\t- e3142 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation; original source AWS and NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from nvblog:vera-cpu-delivery 2026-08-27] AWS and NVIDIA announced plans for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs and to bring Vera CPU-based infrastructure to AWS.\n    43\t    Quote: AWS and NVIDIA announced yesterday a major expansion of their 16-year collaboration, including plans for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs and work to bring Vera CPU -based infrastructure to AWS.\n    44\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3139; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    45\t- e3686 [third_party_view; Michael Parekh; original source Meta ; from gnews:8e2fa4cb6d1ac1bfdd389e09 2026-08-30] Meta plans $130 billion of AI capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n    46\t    Quote: Meta plans $130 billion of AI capex this year.\n    47\t- e4082 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) ; from gnews:15f1ea8f70a1fc52417f55b5 2026-09-03] The article cites estimates of about $750 billion in AI data-center infrastructure spending in calendar 2026 and more than $1 trillion in calendar 2027.\n    48\t    Quote: The latest estimates put AI data center infrastructure spending at around $750 billion this year and more than $1 trillion next year .\n    49\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e175; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    50\t- e4404 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Amazon ; from gnews:9c64c0df4ef80362d10066d5 2026-09-06] Amazon said AWS growth had reignited and the cloud platform had its best quarter in more than four years.\n    51\t    Quote: Amazon Web Services' growth has reignited, and it just had its best quarter in more than four years.\n    52\t- e4405 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Microsoft ; from gnews:9c64c0df4ef80362d10066d5 2026-09-06] Microsoft reported a $678 billion backlog for its Azure cloud platform.\n    53\t    Quote: Microsoft is sitting on a $678 billion backlog for its Azure cloud platform\n    54\t- e4406 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Alphabet ; from gnews:9c64c0df4ef80362d10066d5 2026-09-06] Google Cloud reported second-quarter revenue growth of 82% year over year.\n    55\t    Quote: Google Cloud delivered 82% year-over-year revenue growth in the second quarter.\n    56\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1255; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    57\t- e4417 [fact; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source Amazon, Microsoft, Alphabet and Meta Platforms ; from gnews:7578c01e5da9c31eff3455e3 2026-09-07] Amazon, Microsoft, Alphabet and Meta Platforms have signed more than $1.5 trillion of data-center leases since the AI boom began, including commitments not yet effective.\n    58\t    Quote: Amazon, Microsoft ( NASDAQ:MSFT ), Alphabet and Meta Platforms have signed more than $1.5 trillion of data-center leases since the AI boom began, including commitments not yet effective.\n    59\t- e4514 [third_party_view; Jon Quast; original source Jon Quast 2026-08-27; from gnews:b851795e16e32bc54280459b 2026-09-08] Jon Quast estimated that the top five hyperscalers would spend roughly $800 billion on capex this year.\n    60\t    Quote: the top five hyperscalars spending roughly 800 billion in capex this year.\n    61\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4224; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    62\t- e4564 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-09-09; from gnews:33edf25ec336133783ceead0 2026-09-09] The article expects top hyperscalers to spend $800 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026 and $1.3 billion in calendar 2027.\n    63\t    Quote: The top hyperscalers are expected to spend $800 billion this year and $1.3 billion next year.\n    64\t- e4566 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Elon Musk ; from gnews:29261e892efef38906097afb 2026-09-09] Elon Musk said a global memory shortage was the limiting factor for the AI build-out.\n    65\t    Quote: Elon Musk said that the global shortage of memory is \"the limiting factor\" to the AI build-out\n    66\t- e4567 [fact; fool.com (analyst); original source Amazon ; from gnews:29261e892efef38906097afb 2026-09-09] Amazon raised its capital expenditure projections for 2026, citing rising memory costs.\n    67\t    Quote: Amazon ( AMZN +0.56% ) specifically cited rising memory costs when raising its capital expenditure projections for this year.\n    68\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1649; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    69\t- e4575 [third_party_view; Danny Vena; original source Many technology companies ; from gnews:3425a2c7d70bccfb0d0589cb 2026-09-09] The author says many technology companies estimate NVIDIA GPUs’ useful life at six years.\n    70\t    Quote: Many technology companies estimate the useful life of Nvidia's graphics processing units (GPUs) at six years.\n    71\t- e4576 [third_party_view; Danny Vena; original source Michael Burry ; from gnews:3425a2c7d70bccfb0d0589cb 2026-09-09] Michael Burry says AI chips should be expensed over two to three years, rather than over a longer useful life.\n    72\t    Quote: Burry believes the lifespan of these chips should be shorter, saying they should be expensed over two to three years.\n    73\t- e4680 [third_party_view; fool.com author Ryan Vanzo; original source Rich Privorotsky, Goldman Sachs analyst ; from gnews:b5313a0ca53ce1af48ab5c6c 2026-09-10] Goldman Sachs analyst Rich Privorotsky said a breakthrough in AI intelligence could shift demand and spur other AI labs to keep spending on development.\n    74\t    Quote: \"Price decline is one thing, but a real breakthrough in intelligence will once again change the demand curve,\" Privorotsky explains. \"It will force all other labs to catch up, keep the spending cycle energized, and reactivate the belief that 'there is something better worth building.'\"\n    75\t- e4766 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-09-11; from gnews:3f69a17dc0ba27583e1dbd92 2026-09-11] The author estimates that the five largest AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n    76\t    Quote: The AI hyperscalers are spending hundreds of billions right now, and Nvidia estimates that the big five will spend nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures this year alone.\n    77\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4358; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    78\t- e4790 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Alphabet ; from gnews:9c798576399fa62f56cf3145 2026-09-11] Alphabet increased its expected 2026 capital expenditures to $195 billion–$205 billion.\n    79\t    Quote: Alphabet has increased expected 2026 capital expenditures to between $195 billion and $205 billion.\n    80\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1648; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    81\t- e4807 [third_party_view; finance.biggo.com, quoting Pablo Hernández de Cos in a BIS speech; original source Pablo Hernández de Cos 2026-09-10; from gnews:8e160db6032cc34164f45e59 2026-09-11] BIS General Manager Pablo Hernández de Cos said on September 10 that AI investment was increasingly financed by debt, particularly private credit, and warned disappointing AI returns could trigger an investment pullback.\n    82\t    Quote: The Bank for International Settlements went further on September 10. In a speech in Mumbai, BIS General Manager Pablo Hernández de Cos said the AI investment boom is increasingly financed \"not by earnings but through debt and, notably, through private credit,\" and warned that if returns to AI disappoint, a pullback in investment \"could turn today's capital expenditure boom into a bust.\"\n    83\t- e5014 [third_party_view; Zacks.com; original source Zacks.com 2026-09-14; from gnews:1d3bc1ba4c2df5e998884bdb 2026-09-14] Zacks estimated that Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta would spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026, with a large portion directed to AI infrastructure.\n    84\t    Quote: Four top hyperscalers — Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta Platforms — alone are expected to spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in 2026, with a large portion directed toward AI infrastructure.\n    85\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2177; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    86\t- e5055 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com (analyst); original source BofA Global Research ; from gnews:ca2f580191a4509ac38bc1cf 2026-09-15] BofA Global Research estimated that Microsoft, Alphabet, Amazon, Meta and Oracle could spend roughly $795 billion collectively on capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n    87\t    Quote: Reuters cited BofA Global Research estimates showing Microsoft, Alphabet ( NASDAQ:GOOG ), Amazon ( NASDAQ:AMZN ), Meta ( NASDAQ:META ) and Oracle ( NYSE:ORCL ) could collectively pour roughly $795 billion into capital expenditures in 2026\n    88\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4848; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    89\t- e5074 [third_party_view; barchart.com (analyst); original source Jacob Coxon ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Former Anthropic researcher Jacob Coxon resigned and warned that AI developers were racing toward self-improving systems without adequate safeguards.\n    90\t    Quote: former Anthropic researcher Jacob Coxon resigned and warned that AI developers were racing toward self-improving systems without adequate safeguards.\n    91\t- e5075 [third_party_view; barchart.com (analyst); original source Evan Hubinger ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Anthropic alignment-science lead Evan Hubinger said he personally believes there is a greater than 10% chance AI could kill all humans within the next decade.\n    92\t    Quote: Anthropic's alignment-science lead Evan Hubinger publicly said he personally believes there is a greater than 10% chance AI could kill all humans within the next decade.\n    93\t- e5076 [third_party_view; barchart.com (analyst); original source Dario Amodei ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei called for the industry to slow AI-model improvement until safety measures can catch up.\n    94\t    Quote: Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei responded by calling for the industry to \"pace the frontier\"—slowing the improvement of AI models until safety measures can catch up.\n    95\t- e5077 [third_party_view; barchart.com (analyst); original source Dario Amodei ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Dario Amodei proposed independent evaluators with access inside AI companies, coordination among frontier developers, and international cooperation.\n    96\t    Quote: His proposal includes independent evaluators with access inside AI companies, coordination among frontier developers, and international cooperation.\n    97\t- e5078 [fact; barchart.com (analyst); original source Anthropic ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] Anthropic committed to using the independent-evaluator approach.\n    98\t    Quote: Anthropic has committed to the evaluator approach.\n    99\t- e5079 [party_statement; barchart.com (analyst); original source Sam Altman ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] OpenAI CEO Sam Altman said OpenAI would use independent evaluators and agreed that AI-model development should be paced.\n   100\t    Quote: OpenAI CEO Sam Altman backed the idea, saying OpenAI would also use independent evaluators and agreeing that the frontier needs to be paced.\n   101\t- e5081 [party_statement; barchart.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5adb2372d28f9339f479435d 2026-09-15] NVIDIA said the top five hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026 and $1.3 trillion in calendar 2027.\n   102\t    Quote: The company said the top five hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n   103\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4358, e5105, e5114, e5358, e5376; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   104\t- e5105 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:8cdef40aebf24cdc89f530ff 2026-09-16] NVIDIA said the top five hyperscalers’ annual capital spending is on track to reach $800 billion in 2026 and is expected to reach $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n   105\t    Quote: Nvidia points out that the annual capital spending by the top five hyperscalers is on track to reach $800 billion this year. The figure is expected to increase to $1.3 trillion in 2027.\n   106\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   107\t- e5114 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com author; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:3f21db1f9791e20f80909da8 2026-09-16] NVIDIA management pegged top-five hyperscaler capital expenditures at nearly $800 billion in calendar 2026.\n   108\t    Quote: top-five hyperscaler CapEx management pegged at nearly $800 billion in 2026\n   109\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   110\t- e5150 [fact; Zacks Equity Research; original source Amazon Web Services and NVIDIA ; from gnews:fc6c2e31b8c175953657a182 2026-09-16] The expanded AWS partnership calls for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs to be deployed in 2027–2028.\n   111\t    Quote: Its expanded AWS partnership, which calls for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs to be deployed in 2027-28, further underscores the scale and longevity of AI infrastructure investment.\n   112\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4616; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   113\t- e5170 [third_party_view; marketbeat.com; original source marketbeat.com 2026-09-16; from gnews:b98d0a9596b1d1e0a9609645 2026-09-16] MarketBeat estimates that leading hyperscalers' capital-expenditure plans total more than $700 billion; the period is not specified.\n   114\t    Quote: CapEx plans among the leading hyperscalers total over $700 billion, underscoring the spend.\n   115\t- e5200 [third_party_view; Keith Noonan; original source Keith Noonan 2026-09-17; from gnews:d5a540df626501269df8ff8c 2026-09-17] Keith Noonan expects SpaceX, Alphabet, Amazon, Meta, and other AI-focused companies not to reduce capital expenditure plans for the coming year or investments in compute infrastructure further out.\n   116\t    Quote: Of those three companies, only SpaceX is public right now -- but keep an eye on quarterly reports from it and other public, AI-focused businesses such as Alphabet , Amazon , and Meta Platforms , and see if any of them announce a reduction in capital expenditure plans for the coming year or suggest that they expect to reduce their investments in compute infrastructure further out. It's probably not\n   117\t- e5291 [fact; qz.com; original source NVIDIA, Google and Emerald AI 2026-09-18; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] NVIDIA, Google and Emerald AI announced the launch of the AI Energy Management Alliance to promote flexible AI-data-center electricity use in exchange for faster grid connections.\n   118\t    Quote: NVIDIA, Google $GOOGL , and Emerald AI announced the launch of the AI Energy Management Alliance, a coalition aimed at getting AI data centers to dynamically manage their electricity consumption in response to grid conditions, with the goal of securing faster connections to the power grid in return.\n   119\t- e5292 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Emerald AI CEO Varun Sivaram, as quoted by Engadget ; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] Emerald AI CEO Varun Sivaram said grid connections for new data centers can take ten years or longer, while average grid utilization is about 50%, leaving potential headroom for flexible data-center loads.\n   120\t    Quote: Getting a new data center onto the grid can take ten years or longer, Sivaram noted, because utilities are obligated to guarantee sufficient capacity for peak demand periods. Yet average grid utilization hovers around 50%, which means data centers willing to flex their consumption could tap into substantial headroom on the existing network without forcing expensive new buildout.\n   121\t- e5293 [third_party_view; qz.com; original source Data Center Watch, as cited by Fortune ; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] Data Center Watch data cited by Fortune said community opposition stalled or halted 48 data-center projects representing more than $156 billion in planned construction in 2025.\n   122\t    Quote: Community opposition stalled or halted 48 projects representing more than $156 billion in planned construction last year alone, according to Data Center Watch data cited by Fortune.\n   123\t- e5357 [third_party_view; benzinga.com; original source Brad Gerstner ; from gnews:4256ae7ea51b8cf4d4bb371b 2026-09-18] Brad Gerstner said the roughly $1.5 trillion in annual AI infrastructure capital spending he described requires customers to pay for the capacity.\n   124\t    Quote: \"If you're going to build a trillion and a half dollars a year in capex, somebody has to pay for it,\" he said.\n   125\t- e5358 [party_statement; fool.com (Harsh Chauhan); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ad94c78d09fe41fc3f29bbca 2026-09-18] NVIDIA said the five largest U.S. hyperscalers were on track to spend $800 billion on capital expenditure in 2026.\n   126\t    Quote: Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) noted on its August earnings call that the top five U.S. hyperscalers alone are on track to clock $800 billion in capital expenditure this year.\n   127\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   128\t- e5376 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4ee5d8f73b7bcfc2f2111ad1 2026-09-19] NVIDIA forecasts that the five largest AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n   129\t    Quote: Nvidia forecasts that the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures during 2026.\n   130\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5081; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n   131\t\n   132\t\n   133\t\n   134\tAnswer per the JSON schema: one calculation for the family and one answer per member.","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && for f in codex-r61.md codex-r61b.md codex-r62.md; do echo $f; nl -ba $f; done'","aggregated_output":"codex-r61.md\n     1\t**The FAIL is real. Ask Alex what evidence is enough to publish a forecast before requesting the paid re-send.** The decisive case is the $36B Vera forecast. Its writer identified a risk to sales, then assigned that risk a $4B cost. Neither reader found evidence for the amount. That one answer fails the registered test regardless of how the four disagreements are resolved. [E writer’s assumption](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json:94) · [Recorded votes](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:8) · [Registered pass conditions](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:375)\n     2\t\n     3\t1. **Serious — U05 deserves its settled “no.”** Management expected FY2028 CPU revenue to more than double a $20B base. The writer used $40B as its reference and forecast 90% realization. The deployment announcement supports demand. Anthropic’s evaluation and AMD’s benchmarks establish competitive risk. None gives the timing or volume of sales likely to slip. Calling $4B “less than half an average quarter” expresses the same assumption in another unit. It adds no evidence for it. At exactly $40B the answer enters the higher band, so the unsupported cut decides the result. **Wording correction:** the result report says four readers rejected U05. The record has two first readers, in agreement, and no adjudication. [E request and bands](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/user.txt:3) · [Writer’s conclusion](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json:130) · [Codex reading](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U05.codex.json:5) · [Claude reading](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reader-packets/U05.claude.json:1) · [Report wording](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND4-RESULT.md:41)\n     4\t\n     5\t2. **Serious — The four splits have different merits.** I read both initial readings and both adjudications for each. All eight adjudicator votes followed their own family. My readings below leave the recorded splits intact. [Vote tally](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:4)\n     6\t\n     7\t   | Reply | My reading | What decides it |\n     8\t   |---|---|---|\n     9\t   | **U02 · S forecast** | **Codex “no” on support. The range is valid.** | The writer applies 98% to $720B and gets $705.6B. Its own 96% lower share gives $691.2B. Crossing $700B is allowed: the checker actually publishes $690.9–720.3B, and the schema permits selecting an overlapping band with a reason. The weakness is the claimed independent floor. If all of the cited **more than $730B** of AI infrastructure really is data-center capex for these firms, it exceeds even the writer’s **total** spending midpoint of $720B. The writer needed to reconcile those estimates before using one to validate the other. [Writer’s reasoning](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json:390) · [Checked range](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/CHECKED.json:62) · [Schema](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/schema.json:334) · [Both readings](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U02.md:36) · [Codex decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U02.codex.json:5) · [Claude decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U02.claude.json:1) |\n    10\t   | **U04 · S unresolved control** | **Codex “yes” is better supported.** | At $745B total, the four firms cross $700B at a data-center share of about 94%. The control names that boundary and addresses the strongest contrary passage. The $730B figure names the right four companies but measures AI infrastructure. The nearly $800B data-center figure covers five companies. Neither establishes how much of the four firms’ spending qualifies. Claude has a plausible reason to lean upward. Its claim that the first figure supplies a floor requires a relationship the passage leaves open. That makes this a defensible abstention and a poor control for an *undisputed* abstention. [Control](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/controls/K-unresolved-justified.json:1) · [Four-company passage](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:29) · [Five-company passage](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:128) · [Both readings](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U04.md:36) · [Codex decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U04.codex.json:5) · [Claude decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U04.claude.json:1) |\n    11\t   | **U06 · E forecast** | **Codex “no.” The existing definition settles the error in Claude’s justification.** | Keep the writer’s 90% Vera share and run its proposed lower growth rate. **$20B × 1.5 × 90% = $27B**, one band below the claimed $30–40B. Claude effectively substitutes a 100% share to obtain $30B. The definition requires judging the answer with the writer’s own assumptions. Claude is right on one factual detail: e4856 does connect Vera to the $20B potential. That passage still supplies neither the writer’s realized share nor a floor for NVIDIA’s growth equal to the whole market’s. The writer’s $32.4B arithmetic is correct. The reader’s claim about the whole interval is wrong. [Writer’s reasoning and sensitivity](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json:162) · [Vera passage](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/user.txt:27) · [Definition](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_rules.py:15) · [Both readings](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U06.md:36) · [Codex decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U06.codex.json:5) · [Claude decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U06.claude.json:1) |\n    12\t   | **U08 · S forecast** | **Codex “no” on the claimed bound. This is the closest call on the forecast itself.** | At $745B total, spending outside data centers can be at most $45B for the forecast to clear $700B. The writer deducts $25B and explicitly allows some AI infrastructure spending outside data centers. Claude’s comparison implies less than $15B **outside AI**. It leaves the amount **outside data centers** open on the writer’s own terms. Meta’s roughly 4% residual offers a concrete comparison, but the writer gives no reason the combined residual of all four firms stays below $45B. Alex’s open question bears directly on whether that comparison nevertheless supports a most-likely forecast. [Writer’s deduction](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json:226) · [Meta passage](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:45) · [Both readings](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U08.md:36) · [Codex decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U08.codex.json:5) · [Claude decision](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U08.claude.json:1) |\n    13\t\n    14\t3. **Serious — The recurring writer failure is assigning a size to a risk when that size selects the answer. The recurring reader disagreement is how much a comparison can support.** Counting each original reply once, excluding controls, gives the following. For run three I used its last authorized reading. A joint reply counts once when both forecasts were supported. [Third-run votes by reply](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json:92) · [Third-run usable replies by case](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json:198) · [Targeted-run votes](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:4)\n    15\t\n    16\t   | Original replies | Settled yes | Settled no | Split |\n    17\t   |---|---:|---:|---:|\n    18\t   | Third run, 12 | 8 | 2 | 2 |\n    19\t   | Targeted run, 6 | 2 | 1 | 3 |\n    20\t   | **Total, 18** | **10** | **3** | **5** |\n    21\t\n    22\t   Across **eight S and E replies**, one had settled support, three had settled rejection, and four split. All eight forecast a number. The clearest recurrence is the E Codex writer cutting the guidance reference by 15% in run three and 10% after the request explicitly asked for evidence sizing the cut. All eight P-family replies across the runs had settled support. O accounts for the remaining yes and split, on whether $18B described funding of an existing commitment balance. The stored votes therefore establish three rejected replies, not eight writer errors. They also show reader variation: the *same* third-run S Codex answer went **no → yes → no** across its three readings. [Earlier E cut](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread2-packets/T17.codex.json:5) · [Revised request](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/system.txt:9) · [O disagreement](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread2-packets/T02.codex.json:5) · [S original vote](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/TALLY.json:494) · [First re-read](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD-TALLY.json:291) · [Second re-read](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json:123)\n    23\t\n    24\t4. **Serious — Alex settled the duty to justify an adjustment. How strong an indirect comparison must be is still his decision.** D741 requires the deduction to be stated or a supported bound given. D745 accepts an unresolved answer when its reason survives the strongest passages. The writer is also told to choose the most likely outcome and to size estimates from comparable deals, capacity, units, selling time, or price. The frozen examples illustrate the distinction. On q04, the reader used roughly $650B of plans and evidence about demand, costs, and delays to forecast $650–720B. On q19, two selling quarters and a $5,000 CPU price made the four million units implied by guidance assessable. No passage supplied the final discount. Those examples support reasoned estimates. They neither establish S’s category relationship nor provide the missing sales evidence for FY2028 Vera. [D741](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11348) · [D745](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11510) · [Writer’s sizing instructions](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/system.txt:4) · [q04 reasoning](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json:246) · [q19 reasoning](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json:897)\n    25\t\n    26\t5. **Minor — Two details change how to interpret the paid option.** Its third reader is Codex. A published singleton stops the test if a reader-confirmed unsized adjustment decides its band. The free test asked about *support*, so these votes indicate a risk, not a measured paid-stop rate. Also, paid S asks about **2027** while targeted S asks about **2026**. Finally, the report’s statement that an unresolved singleton is automatically held is inaccurate. I replayed all nine stored answers and controls in memory. Both unresolved controls returned `publish` with no numeric outcome. Such a card has no chosen band, but the paid verdict does inspect published singleton cards for defects. [Paid reading and stop rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:262) · [Paid cases](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:43) · [Targeted cases](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:166) · [Singleton publication rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:782) · [Paid verdict code](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:772)\n    27\t\n    28\t**One recommendation.** Put this question to Alex: **“When a related figure makes one band more likely, but the evidence still leaves the next band plausible, may we publish the forecast if we explain why the comparison applies, or should we say ‘unresolved’ until the evidence bounds the quantity on the deciding side?”** I favor allowing the forecast when the writer explains why the comparison applies to the companies, period, and quantity asked about, and why it favors that side of the cutoff. U05 fails even that reading.\n    29\t\n    30\tThat ruling costs no provider spend. A fresh read under unchanged instructions would sample the same disagreement. A code or request change now would spend engineering and review time implementing one side before Alex chooses it. Closing S and E wholesale costs both forecasts whenever the evidence later becomes sufficient. The 36-call paid re-send would measure Luna and the benefit of judging related questions together. It cannot settle what Alex wants published. The earlier plan estimated **$0.17** for first attempts at typical output length under a **$0.50** ceiling; a new send requires separate authorization. [Registered sequence](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:375) · [Paid cost assumptions](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:29)\n    31\t\n    32\t**One test.** After Alex rules, register one **$0 blinded contrast**. Give fresh writers the same spending question twice. One version has only the indirect comparison Alex ruled on. The other adds a clearly labeled test passage that explicitly bounds the four firms’ data-center spending above $700B and below $800B. Fix the expected decisions from Alex’s answer before showing either version. Mix in the saved U05 as the unsupported-cut control. The result moves the decision only if both reader families apply Alex’s rule to the indirect case, accept a forecast on the explicitly bounded case, and reject U05’s unsized cut. Agreement would justify returning to writer verification under a settled standard. Another split would keep the paid request closed. The original D745 FAIL stands either way.codex-r61b.md\n     1\t**Ask Alex whether an indirect comparison can justify publishing the more likely forecast. I lean yes. Keep the paid re-send on hold.** The $36B Vera answer shows why these are separate decisions. Its writer acknowledged that the passages gave no order size or shipment pace, then used a $4B shortfall to put sales below $40B. Both first readers rejected that answer. Resolving every disagreement about cloud spending would still leave D745 failed. [Vera answer:94](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json:94) · [Recorded votes:8](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:8) · [Registered pass conditions:375](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:375)\n     2\t\n     3\t1. **Agree with Claude’s revised lean. I dispute two of the reasons given for it.** The writer is asked for the most likely outcome. The frozen examples make that concrete. For q19, the price per CPU tells the reader that the target implies four million units in roughly two selling quarters. That supports a discount even though no passage supplies the final $8–18B range. For q04, the reader weighs spending plans, demand and delays. D741 still applies when a writer starts from total spending to forecast a narrower amount. It must state the deduction or give a supported bound. The open choice is whether an indirect comparison can support the stated deduction strongly enough to pick a side. Alex has not already approved that threshold. D725 does not promise that code catches every invented or mislabelled figure. And §8’s “no data to verify” concerns checking outcomes afterward. It gives no permission to publish an unsupported estimate today. [Writer instructions:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/system.txt:1) · [q19:897](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json:897) · [q04:246](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json:246) · [D741:11359](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11359) · [Limits of the code checks:11265](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11265) · [AGENTS.md:416](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:416)\n     4\t\n     5\t2. **Agree on asking one general question. Disagree with wording that assumes the related number already points to the answer.** The four companies said they expected to spend more than $730B on *AI infrastructure* in 2026. The question asks about *data-center capital spending*. A writer who starts from $745B of total spending has to explain why spending outside data centers is likely to be at most $45B to reach the $700B cutoff. That gives Alex a concrete choice without asking him to define “AI infrastructure” for all future cases. [Spending passages:27](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:27) · [Two readings of the $45B difference:36](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U08.md:36)\n     6\t\n     7\t   **My question for Alex:** “资料没直接给出所问的数。用一个相关数字作比较，能解释为什么结果更可能在线的这一边，但另一边仍有可能。这时可以发布预测，还是要等到能从资料算出结果落在线的哪一边？”\n     8\t\n     9\t   - **可以发布（我推荐）。** 要讲清这个比较为什么适用于所问的公司、时间和数字，以及按答案自己的假设，为什么这一边更可能。\n    10\t   - **先说‘还判断不了’。** 等资料能直接给出或推算出所问的数在线的哪一边，再发布预测。\n    11\t\n    12\t   This covers using market growth as a comparison for one vendor. It does not make market growth a guaranteed minimum. Both readers accepted the earlier $37B Vera answer, which explained why NVIDIA might outgrow the market. The later answer added a 90% Vera share. At its lower growth estimate, $20B × 1.5 × 90% gives **$27B**. The writer itself acknowledges that sensitivity. The reader who claimed the whole range stayed at $30–40B dropped the writer’s assumption. That error is settled by arithmetic. Whether the writer gave enough reason to prefer its $32.4B forecast was still a split vote. [Earlier Vera reasoning:131](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/claude.json:131) · [T15 votes:169](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json:169) · [Later Vera answer:162](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json:162) · [Adjudicator’s claim:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U06.claude.json:1)\n    13\t\n    14\t3. **Agree with the stored-answer re-read as the one next test.** After Alex rules, keep the answers fixed and change only the shared reading instructions to include his words. The synthetic passage in round 61 would show whether a fresh writer can use an explicit bound. It would tell us little about why two readers looking at the *same* answer disagree. Claude’s list contains **eight distinct answers** because U04 is both a split and a control. I would add T15 as the real comparison for U06. That gives **nine answers, 18 independent first readings, zero new writer calls and $0 in paid sends**. T10 is there for its spending argument only. Its previously confirmed quotation error stands. [Round 61 proposal:32](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r61.md:32) · [U04 and other controls:4](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:4) · [T10’s separate error:111](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md:111)\n    15\t\n    16\t   **What would move the decision:** Before reading, record what Alex’s choice implies for the examples and controls. Move on to writer repair if both families independently apply that choice to the spending forecasts and the “can’t tell” answer, reject the unsupported Vera cuts, and judge T15 and U06 using each writer’s actual assumptions. Matching votes for incompatible reasons do not settle the disagreement. A continuing split leaves the publication question unresolved in practice. Even a clean re-read cannot turn D745 into a pass. [Shared reading rule:15](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_rules.py:15) · [D741’s sequence:11364](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11364)\n    17\t\n    18\t4. **Agree on waiting for an E repair. Disagree that a paid STOP is already predictable.** Of four Vera answers, **two were rejected by both readers, one split, and one was accepted by both**. Three contained a cut or share whose size is in question. Those four answers came from Codex and Claude. They give no measured failure rate for Luna, and the free readers voted on support while the paid readers separately mark whether a gap decided the band. Paid S also asks about **2027**. The stored S answers concern **2026**. [Earlier Vera votes:169](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json:169) · [Later votes:8](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:8) · [Paid cases:15](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:15) · [Free cases:169](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:169)\n    19\t\n    20\t   **The attainable paid verdict is conditional.** On a complete run, one confirmed deciding gap in any of the three published Vera answers forces **STOP**, even if joint judging wins all nine pairs. Codex breaks a reader tie. With zero published defects across all joint and singleton answers, **PASS** remains possible with **8 joint wins and at most 5 separate wins**, or **9 joint wins and at most 6 separate wins**, with at least two usable joint pairs per snapshot. A properly explained “can’t tell” is published for a singleton and is still inspected. So the 36 calls could measure Luna and the benefit of judging related questions together. The estimated $0.17 cost and proposed $0.50 ceiling do not override the requirement for a clean free test before requesting authorization. [Paid reading and verdict:262](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:262) · [Verdict calculation:772](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:772) · [Singleton publication:782](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:782) · [Cost estimate:32](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:32) · [Authorization condition:380](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:380)\n    21\t\n    22\t   **The smallest writer change I would take forward after the ruling** is to make the writer choose between the existing “forecast” and “can’t tell” answers *before* it assigns a discount. At that choice, it must identify what makes one side more likely under its own assumptions. If it can explain only why sales might slip, it can report that gap directly. This is a proposed repair. D742 addressed a different problem involving quarterly guidance. D745 is the round that directly tried and failed to fix unsupported discounts. Automatically publishing management’s number whenever it sits on the cutoff would change the product and would require Alex’s separate decision. [Existing answer choices:287](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/schema.json:287) · [D742:11397](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11397) · [D745:11513](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11513) · [Recurring failures:85](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:85)codex-r62.md\n     1\t**Run with the listed changes.** The current tally can declare agreement when the readers give opposite explanations. I verified that with invented votes in memory. No file was edited, and no D747 packet was built or read.\n     2\t\n     3\t1. **Serious — Alex’s new sentence is faithful, but the surrounding instructions still permit the old reading.** Alex allowed a forecast when the writer explains why the comparison fits the companies, period and quantity, and why its own assumptions favor one side. The other side can remain possible. The new sentence says that accurately. The preceding sentence still says the passages *must* support the size or a bound. The first-reader brief offers the comparison as an alternative, while adjudicators receive the two sentences without that clarification. Make the shared definition say explicitly that an explained comparison can satisfy the support requirement. D741 still requires a writer using a total for a narrower amount to state its deduction or give a supported bound. D745’s unresolved clause is still right. A reader should judge the gap the writer identifies after considering the strongest evidence. The mere availability of a defensible forecast does not disqualify a defensible “can’t tell.” [Alex’s question and chosen option:11575](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11575) · [D741:11359](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11359) · [D745:11515](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11515) · [Shared definition:15](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_rules.py:15) · [First-reader brief:241](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind4.py:241) · [Adjudicator brief:23](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_adjudicate.py:23)\n     4\t\n     5\t2. **Minor — Keep the four fixed expectations.** Each has a reason in the original answer and request.\n     6\t\n     7\t   | Answer | Expected | Decisive evidence |\n     8\t   |---|---|---|\n     9\t   | U01 | **No** | “The outlook for the second half is uncertain” never says what prevents a forecast of annual data-center revenue. The request includes reported results and next-quarter guidance. [Answer:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/controls/K-unresolved-unjustified.json:1) · [Request:25](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/P-v8/user.txt:25) |\n    10\t   | U03 | **No** | The writer reduces the $20B starting figure to $17B and doubles it to $34B. Customer interest and competition explain why sales might miss. The answer gives no comparison that explains the 15% cut that puts it below $40B. [Original answer:178](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/codex.json:178) · [Request:27](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/user.txt:27) |\n    11\t   | U05 | **No** | The writer takes 90% of $40B and acknowledges that it has neither order sizes nor shipment pace. Calling the $4B shortfall “less than half an average quarter” restates the estimate without supporting it. [Answer:94](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json:94) · [Request:33](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/user.txt:33) |\n    12\t   | T15 | **Yes** | The writer explains why announced adoption favors growth above the roughly 50% market pace and why execution risk favors growth below management’s doubling. On its stated Vera base, 85% growth gives $37B. This supports the selected band without treating market growth as a guaranteed floor. [Answer:131](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/claude.json:131) · [Request:13](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/user.txt:13) · [Vera-specific guidance:27](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/user.txt:27) |\n    13\t\n    14\t3. **Serious — I would fix U02 at “no,” retain U04, and register what the other readings must get right.** This is my recommendation before the read, not an outcome from it. [Current expectations:398](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:398)\n    15\t\n    16\t   | Answer | Recommendation | Why |\n    17\t   |---|---|---|\n    18\t   | U02 | **Fix at no** | The writer explicitly calls more than $730B a *floor* for data-center spending, then forecasts $705.6B of data-center spending and $720B of total spending. All three numbers happen to point to the same band. They cannot all hold under the writer’s own explanation. Calling the $730B figure a directional comparison would repair the answer on the writer’s behalf. [Answer:390](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json:390) · [What the $730B measures:29](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:29) |\n    19\t   | U04 | **Keep open. I lean yes.** | It identifies the roughly 94% share needed to cross $700B and explains the mismatch between four firms’ AI spending and five firms’ data-center spending. It also retains the original writer’s 90% estimate and $670.5B calculation after the control removed their links to the answer. A reader can reasonably question that tension. Keep it as the disputed abstention, with no fixed vote. [Stored control:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/controls/K-unresolved-justified.json:1) · [How it was constructed:96](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind4.py:96) · [Five-firm passage:128](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:128) |\n    20\t   | U08 | **Keep open** | At $745B of total spending, up to $45B can fall outside data centers before the forecast drops below $700B. The writer deducts $25B, uses Meta as a comparison and acknowledges that some AI spending may fall outside data centers. Whether that makes a deduction below $45B more likely is precisely the judgment Alex authorized. [Answer:226](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json:226) · [Meta passage:45](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:45) |\n    21\t   | T10 | **Keep open for the spending argument** | Its $679–745B range crosses $700B. The writer cites raised plans and higher tracking estimates to favor the upper portion. Readers should decide whether those comparisons support the 95–100% data-center share. Its range crossing the line is permitted, and its old quotation defect is already settled. [Answer:276](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/answers/claude.json:276) · [Four-firm passage:83](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/user.txt:83) · [Settled defect:11491](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11491) |\n    22\t   | U06 | **Keep open, with the arithmetic fixed** | At the writer’s 90% Vera share, its lower growth comparison gives $20B × 1.5 × 90% = **$27B**, below the selected band. Its preferred 1.8× gives **$32.4B**, inside it. The writer acknowledges the sensitivity. Readers may disagree about which outcome it justified. A “yes” based on the whole range staying above $30B cannot count as applying Alex’s rule. [Answer:162](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json:162) · [Bands:3](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/user.txt:3) |\n    23\t\n    24\t4. **Serious — Two older answers get the wrong format rule, and U03 gets the wrong original request.** The shuffle assigns nine fresh W identifiers and keeps the key outside the packets. For T10 and T15, both the initial and adjudication builders correctly select the third run’s system text, user text and schema descriptions. But both append *today’s* list of allowed qualifiers. I checked both stored schemas. Neither allowed `estimated`. T10’s source says “an estimated $750 billion,” exactly where that difference mattered. Read the allowed values from the stored schema in `case_dir`. Separately, I verified that U03 is the unchanged third-run E answer. The builder pairs it with the fourth run’s system text, which added the very instruction about sizing discounts under examination. Use the third-run request for U03. Describe U01 and U04 as constructed controls evaluated under their designated request. **Minor blinding issue:** all three controls are displayed as single-line JSON while the original replies are multiline. Give all nine the same display formatting while preserving their content. [Shuffle and packet assembly:256](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind4.py:256) · [Adjudication paths:64](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_adjudicate.py:64) · [D747 dispatch:119](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_adjudicate.py:119) · [Qualifier source:60](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_rules.py:60) · [Old allowed values:180](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/schema.json:180) · [Control provenance:104](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind4.py:104) · [Changed writer instruction:353](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:353) · [Control formatting:132](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind4.py:132)\n    25\t\n    26\t5. **Serious — Make the final verdict depend on the registered reading of the reasons.** I exercised the tally in memory with all nine labels, matching votes, and mutually incompatible reasons about whether AI spending supplies a floor. It returned **pass**. An empty key also returned **pass**. The ordinary vote rules otherwise match the registration. A settled wrong fixed vote fails, a continuing support split is inconclusive unless another case already fails, and a confirmed writer defect stops the run. T10 and the three controls are correctly excluded from defect stops. A one-reader defect on those excluded items can still delay completion by requesting adjudication. [Registered reason check and stops:407](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:407) · [Vote handling:288](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind4.py:288) · [Adjudication and exclusions:317](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind4.py:317)\n    27\t\n    28\t   Require the exact nine answers and both valid readings per answer. Keep the vote tally provisional until Claude records whether the deciding reasons are compatible and apply Alex’s rule, including after adjudication. Two readers who both insist on a guaranteed floor have made the same mistake. Give them the opportunity to settle a disagreement in reasons even when their votes match. If it persists, record **inconclusive**. The decisive pre-run check is that nine matching votes with opposing explanations cannot produce a final pass. [Round 61b decision condition:16](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r61b.md:16) · [Current final verdict:328](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind4.py:328)\n    29\t\n    30\t**Verdict — run with the listed changes.** A clean result can move the council to the E writer repair. A continuing disagreement should return to council with the exact passage and the two reasons. D745’s failure stands either way, and the paid re-send remains on hold until the writer repair passes its own free test. [Decision sequence:11560](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11560)","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '382,450p' && nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '11557,11600p;11258,11283p'\"","aggregated_output":"   382\t  - any failure goes back to council;\n   383\t  - a clean result supports asking Alex to authorize the 36-request paid re-send.\n   384\t\n   385\t**[D747] The re-read under Alex's comparison ruling (Alex: \"可以发布，要讲清楚\"; council rounds 61–61b), registered before any packet is\n   386\tbuilt.**\n   387\t- **The one change.** The shared definitions (`blind_rules.py`, \"supported\") add Alex's ruling. The band-deciding assumption must\n   388\t  rest on the passages in one of three ways:\n   389\t  - its size;\n   390\t  - a bound that justifies the rung;\n   391\t  - or, when the passages do not give the asked quantity, a related figure that the answer explains.\n   392\t\n   393\t  An explained related figure satisfies this even though the other side of the line remains possible. It is explained when the answer\n   394\t  says why the comparison applies to the companies, the period and the quantity asked, and why, under the answer's own stated\n   395\t  assumptions, the chosen side of the line is more likely. An answer that does not explain this is not supported.\n   396\t  - The first readers' step 2 asks, for a forecast, whether the passages support the deciding assumption's size or a bound, or whether\n   397\t    the answer explains a related figure as the definitions require.\n   398\t  - Adjudicators get the same definitions.\n   399\t  - The requests and the answers are unchanged. Each packet carries its writer's own request.\n   400\t- **What is re-read.** Nine stored answers under a fresh shuffle, W01–W09 (`blind4.py reread`). The key is in `reread-key.json`\n   401\t  beside, never in a packet.\n   402\t\n   403\t  | Answer | Expected reading |\n   404\t  |---|---|\n   405\t  | blind4 U01: P-v8, vague unresolved control | not supported |\n   406\t  | blind4 U02: S Claude, 98% share. It calls \"more than $730B\" a floor for data-center spending, then forecasts $705.6B of data-center spending on $720B of total spending, so its own assumptions cannot all hold (council 62) | not supported |\n   407\t  | blind4 U03: E, unsized 15% cut control | not supported |\n   408\t  | blind4 U05: E Codex, unsized 10% cut | not supported |\n   409\t  | blind3 T15: E Claude, a bracket between the market pace and the guidance, with Vera as all CPU revenue | supported |\n   410\t  | blind4 U08 (S Codex, $25B deduction) and U04 (S justified unresolved); blind3 T10 (S Claude, read for its spending argument only: its settled quotation error stands, and its defect marks are not scored); blind4 U06 (E Claude, 1.8x with a 90% Vera share, judged with its own share) | must settle either way: both first readers agree, or both adjudicators do |\n   411\t- **Changes from council round 62** (`codex-r62.md`), made before any packet was built:\n   412\t  - The definition says outright that an explained related figure satisfies the support requirement, even though the other side of\n   413\t    the line stays possible. Readers and adjudicators get the same words.\n   414\t  - Each packet carries the request its answer was written for. U03, T10 and T15 get the third run's request. The constructed\n   415\t    controls U01 and U04 are read under the request they were built for.\n   416\t  - The qualifier list shown comes from each packet's own schema, so the third run's packets do not list \"estimated\".\n   417\t  - All nine answers are shown in one layout, parsed and printed with one indent, so a control cannot be told apart by its layout.\n   418\t  - The tally refuses a key that is not exactly the nine answers, or a reading without a valid vote. A settled vote is final only\n   419\t    after Claude records its two reasons as compatible and applying Alex's rule (`reread-reasons.json`).\n   420\t    - Matching votes whose reasons are incompatible go to adjudication.\n   421\t    - An adjudicated agreement with incompatible reasons is a split.\n   422\t    - A \"yes\" on U06 that rests on the whole range staying at $30B or more does not apply the rule, since $20B × 1.5 × 90% = $27B.\n   423\t  - Checked on a scratch copy:\n   424\t    - without recorded reasons, the tally gives no verdict;\n   425\t    - with incompatible reasons, it asks for adjudication;\n   426\t    - with compatible reasons, it passes;\n   427\t    - a fixed \"no\" read as yes fails.\n   428\t- **Readers.** Fresh readers: one Claude subagent and one read-only Codex session per packet. Where they disagree, two adjudicators\n   429\t  read the packet, one per family.\n   430\t- **Scoring** (`blind4.py reread-tally`):\n   431\t  - It passes when the five fixed readings come out as expected and the four others settle.\n   432\t  - Matching votes whose stated reasons contradict each other on the comparison are recorded as unsettled. Claude reads them by hand.\n   433\t  - A settled defect on a writer's reply other than T10 is a stop.\n   434\t- **What the result decides:**\n   435\t  - Pass: the reading standard under D747 is settled. The next step is the E writer repair, with the council before any send, then a\n   436\t    $0 writer test, then Alex for the paid re-send.\n   437\t  - A split: Alex's words do not yet fix the reading. The split answers go back to the council, and to Alex as boundary cases.\n   438\t  - A failed fixed reading goes back to the council.\n   439\t  - A clean re-read does not turn D745 into a pass.\n 11258\t## D738 · 2026-11-22 · v13s: code refuses a named defect only where the calculation's structure decides it; readers mark the five defects on every reply (council rounds 52–53)\n 11259\t\n 11260\t- **Rounds 52 and 53** (`research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r52.md`, `codex-r53.md`) reviewed the small format within D737's\n 11261\t  scope. Each closing rule written for an indirect form of a named defect produced new counterexamples in both directions:\n 11262\t  - \"uses the part\" lineage refuses a correct sum of two Data Center quarters, each computed as a share of company revenue;\n 11263\t  - \"one figure per quantity\" refuses two outside estimates averaged into one starting point;\n 11264\t  - multiplying by 1, relabelling a basis, or routing through an estimate's judgement still passes.\n 11265\t- **Settled (how D737's rule is built; D737 says code refuses only for the five defects, not that code catches every instance):**\n 11266\t  - Code refuses a named defect where the calculation's own structure decides it without reading intent:\n 11267\t    - **S8:** a sum whose inputs include the part's quantity and the whole's quantity themselves.\n 11268\t    - **S4:** two figures with basis forecast for one quantity and period, or a figure of a listed shared quantity used by an outcome\n 11269\t      on a path that bypasses its forecast figure.\n 11270\t    - **S3:** a part above its whole, compared with paired ends when the part is computed from the whole and lowest against highest\n 11271\t      otherwise, on recomputed values. A share takes 0 to 100 percent.\n 11272\t    - **S5:** an estimate or forecast at or beneath the marked reference.\n 11273\t    - **S1, S2, S6, S7** as written.\n 11274\t  - Indirect forms (a multiplier of one, a relabelled basis, an estimate's judgement) are recorded for readers. They are never\n 11275\t    refused.\n 11276\t  - **The readers** in the blind test and the gate mark the five defects on every reply. In the blind test, an accepted reply with\n 11277\t    a reader-marked defect is a stop, so whether real writers use these forms is measured, not argued.\n 11278\t  - **Round 53's false refusals and test conflicts are fixed in the design:**\n 11279\t    - DC Q3 + Q4;\n 11280\t    - averaged outside inputs;\n 11281\t    - cycles through `rests_on`;\n 11282\t    - a figure used twice taking one value in range arithmetic;\n 11283\t    - the \"to\", \"+/-\" and \"±\" spellings;\n 11557\t  - **The recommendation.** Ask Alex one general question (below), with a lean to allow the forecast when the answer explains why the\n 11558\t    comparison applies to the companies, the period and the quantity asked, and why, under its own assumptions, that side of the line\n 11559\t    is more likely.\n 11560\t  - **The one test after his ruling.** A $0 blinded re-read of nine stored answers under his words, added to the shared definitions:\n 11561\t    - the answers: U01–U06, U08, the third run's T10 (for its spending argument only), and T15 (the accepted E bracket);\n 11562\t    - fresh readers, one per family;\n 11563\t    - what his choice implies for each answer is recorded before reading.\n 11564\t    - It moves on to a writer repair only if both families apply his words alike. A continuing split leaves the publication question\n 11565\t      open in practice. A clean re-read does not turn D745 into a pass.\n 11566\t  - **The paid re-send stays on hold** until an E writer repair passes a clean $0 test. The proposed repair: the writer chooses between\n 11567\t    a forecast and \"can't tell\" before it assigns a discount, naming what makes one side more likely under its own assumptions. It is a\n 11568\t    new arm and goes to council before any send. Publishing management's figure whenever it sits on a cutoff would change the product,\n 11569\t    and needs Alex's own decision.\n 11570\t- **The question to Alex** (pending): \"资料没直接给出所问的数。用一个相关数字作比较，能解释为什么结果更可能在线的这一边，但另一边仍有可能。这时可以发布预测，还是要等到能从资料算出结果落在线的哪一边？\"\n 11571\t- **Not done.** Nothing was paid. Nothing was tuned on the 36 stored answers. The request was not changed after the registration.\n 11572\t\n 11573\t## D747 · 2026-11-23 · Alex rules that a forecast may rest on an explained indirect comparison\n 11574\t\n 11575\t- **The question** (after D746): \"资料没直接给出题目问的那个数，只能拿一个相关的数字来比。比如问四大云厂商今年的数据中心开支能不能过 7000 亿，资料只说“这四家 AI 基础设施开支超过 7300 亿”。这种比较能说明结果更可能落在线的某一边，但另一边也还有可能。这时系统可以发布预测吗，还是要等资料能直接算出落在哪边？\"\n 11576\t  - Options:\n 11577\t    - \"可以发布，要讲清楚 (推荐)\": the answer must explain why the comparison applies to the companies, the year and the quantity\n 11578\t      asked, and why, under its own assumptions, that side is more likely; an answer that cannot explain it is not supported;\n 11579\t    - \"先说“还判断不了”\";\n 11580\t    - \"先停在这里\".\n 11581\t- **Alex's answer** (2026-11-23): \"可以发布，要讲清楚 (推荐)\".\n 11582\t- **Decided (Alex).** When the passages do not give the asked quantity, a forecast may rest on a related figure. It is supported\n 11583\t  only when the answer explains two things: why the comparison applies to the companies, the period and the quantity asked; and why,\n 11584\t  under the answer's own stated assumptions, the chosen side of the line is more likely. An answer that cannot explain this is not\n 11585\t  supported.\n 11586\t  - D741 ruling 3 still holds: a total used for a narrower quantity states the deduction as its own figure, or gives a supported\n 11587\t    bound.\n 11588\t  - D745's ruling still holds: a justified unresolved answer counts as usable.\n 11589\t- **Next (D746).** The ruling's words go into the shared reader definitions (`blind_rules.py`). Then one $0 blinded re-read of nine\n 11590\t  stored answers, registered with its expected readings and reviewed by the council before any packet is read.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23 && for f in reread-adjudication/*.json reread-packets/W0{3,6,7}.*json; do echo $f; nl -ba $f; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23 && for f in reread-adjudication/*.json reread-packets/W0{3,6,7}.*json; do echo $f; nl -ba $f; done'","aggregated_output":"reread-adjudication/W03.claude.json\n     1\t{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"why\": \"No passage gives data-center capex for these four companies, so the step that decides the band is F13, a 95-100% data-center share of their capital spending. That makes it a related-figure step, and the answer explains it. First, the four companies' 2026 capital spending is put at 'roughly $720 billion to $745 billion' (e5014), 'near $745 billion' (e1856) and '$760 billion' (e2458). Second, outside sources describe 2026 spending of the same size as data-center spending: 'AI data center infrastructure spending at around $750 billion this year' (e4082), and the big five spending 'nearly $800 billion on data center capital expenditures during 2026' (e5376). The answer says these are 'the same totals', notes that the $800bn covers five companies 'of which these four are the bulk', and ties both to 2026 and to data-center capex. That covers the companies, the period and the quantity. It also says why rung 4 is more likely under its own assumptions: F7 715-745 x 95-100% = 679.25-745 (arithmetic checks), the midpoint is about $712bn, and the range drops below $700bn only when the low guidance ends meet the lowest share. The definition accepts an explained related figure 'even though the other side of the line remains possible'. Reader 1's objections do not reopen the gap. The answer itself notes that e5376 covers five companies. e1947 ('more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year' for the same four companies) points the same way as the near-total share, not against it, so leaving it as a repeat does not undercut the forecast.\"}},\n     2\t \"defects\": []}\nreread-adjudication/W03.codex.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v6\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"no\",\n     5\t      \"why\": \"At F7’s $730 billion midpoint, the four companies reach $700 billion in data-center spending only if they spend at most $30 billion elsewhere, a data-center share of about 95.9%. For those four companies, e5014 says only that “a large portion” goes to AI infrastructure. The closest comparison, e1947, says “more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year,” but the answer treats it as a repeat without explaining how much qualifies as data-center capex. The $750 billion estimates in e1416 and e4082 do not identify these four companies, and e5376 covers the “big five” without sizing the fifth company’s contribution. F14’s $712 billion midpoint follows from assuming a 95–100% share. It supplies no independent reason to expect the four companies to clear $700 billion.\"\n     6\t    }\n     7\t  },\n     8\t  \"defects\": []\n     9\t}reread-adjudication/W06.claude.json\n     1\t{\"members\": {\"v41\": {\"supported\": \"no\", \"why\": \"The band is decided by the 85% growth estimate (F7): the rung 2/3 line on the $20 billion FY2027 base (F1, e3660) sits at exactly 100% growth ($40 billion). The only passages that size NVIDIA's own FY2028 Vera/CPU revenue put it at or above that line: 'expects that number to more than double in fiscal 2028' (e3660), 'the potential to more than double that number in fiscal year 2028' (e4856), 'Huang expects CPU revenue to more than double' (e4764), 'expects its Vera CPU revenue to double next year' (e4691). So the deciding assumption does not rest on the passages by its size, and no passage gives a bound that keeps FY2028 below $40 billion. The remaining route is a related figure the answer explains. The related figures are market-wide multi-year rates: 'the server CPU market's revenue could increase at an annual rate of 50% through 2030' (e4687) and 'the CPU market will grow fivefold between this year and 2030, from $35 billion to $170 billion' (e4857). The answer says only that a doubling 'requires large share gains'. It does not explain why a market-average rate applies to one company's newly ramping product, or how rates averaged out to 2030 apply to FY2028. It also does not explain why, under its own assumptions, falling short of doubling is more likely than reaching it. Its own reasons point the other way: adoption 'supports a pace well above the market', and the AMD benchmarks are 'weighed lightly'. The 85% is an unweighted blend ('Weighting the company target most heavily, tempered toward the market pace, gives 85%'). The system text does allow discounting a target for time and execution, but asks that the discount be sized by 'how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies'. The answer gives no such sizing beyond 'the whole of FY2028 is still to be sold'. Under the ruling ('an answer that does not explain this is not supported'), the side below $40 billion is not supported by the passages.\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nreread-adjudication/W06.codex.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v41\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n     5\t      \"why\": \"On NVIDIA’s $20 billion FY2027 outlook (e3660), Vera must grow 100% to reach $40 billion in FY2028. AMD projects server CPU revenue growth at an “annual rate of 50% through 2030” (e4687), a period that includes FY2028. At that pace, doubling Vera revenue would require NVIDIA’s market share to rise by roughly one-third in a year. Expected deployments by “every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM” (e4689) support substantial share gains. Anthropic is still “evaluating” Vera (e5044), and AMD reports a competing chip with “1.2 times the per-core performance” (e5400). Those passages explain why the writer expects Vera to outgrow the market while falling short of management’s doubling outlook. The writer identifies 85% as an estimate. Applied to $20 billion, it gives $37 billion, within rung 3.\"\n     6\t    }\n     7\t  },\n     8\t  \"defects\": []\n     9\t}reread-adjudication/W07.claude.json\n     1\t{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"why\": \"The band is decided by the 98% data-center share (F14) applied to the $720bn total (F11). At 98%, rung 4 needs a total of at least about $714bn. At $720bn, it needs a share of at least about 97.2%. The share rests on a related figure that the answer explains, which the definition allows. e1947 names exactly these four companies ('Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet') and this period ('this year'), with 'more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure'. The answer explains why this figure applies to the quantity asked. It treats this spending as data-center spending ('data-center (AI infrastructure) spending'). It notes that the figure is at or above the four-company capex tally (e5014 'roughly $720 billion to $745 billion'), so nearly all capex is data-center spending. It keeps a 2% residual because 'No passage sizes non-data-center spending'. The answer also says why the upper side is more likely: the repeated guidance raises (e1254, e1549, e1649), and the four-company tallies of $720-760bn (e5014, e1856, e2458) all exceed the roughly $714bn total that 98% needs. The e1947 figure itself lies in rung 4. The answer names the rung 3 risk: Microsoft or the share could come in lower, and the 96% end of its bound gives $691bn. Under the definition, 'an explained related figure satisfies this requirement even though the other side of the line remains possible'. Rung 5 is excluded on the passages, because the ~$800bn figures cover five companies (e2759 'top 5 hyperscalers'; e5055 includes 'Oracle').\"}}, \"defects\": [{\"reader\": \"Reader 1\", \"type\": \"part above or added into its whole\", \"figures\": [\"F12\", \"F11\", \"F15\"], \"decision\": \"uphold\", \"why\": \"The answer itself treats F12 as the segment. F14 defines the share as 'the share of the four companies' 2026 capital expenditure that is data-center (AI infrastructure) spending', so it makes AI-infrastructure spending part of total capex. The reasons then call e1947's 'more than $730 billion' (F12) 'the supported floor' of the outcome. The family therefore holds a figure for the part above $730bn, while its forecast of the whole, total capex F11, is $720bn and its outcome F15 is $705.6bn. The system text says 'a part cannot exceed its whole', and the definition covers 'a segment is larger than the total it belongs to'. F14's basis concedes the point: the AI-infrastructure total 'is at or above the total-capex tally'. This inconsistency does not change the rung: the figure that exceeds the total is itself in rung 4.\"}]}\nreread-adjudication/W07.codex.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v6\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"no\",\n     5\t      \"why\": \"With $720 billion of total capex, rung 4 requires the four companies to spend at most $20 billion on everything outside data centers. e1947 covers the same four companies and year, but its “more than $730 billion” measures AI infrastructure. The answer does not explain how much qualifies as data-center capex. e5014 says only “a large portion” goes to AI infrastructure. Meta’s $130 billion covers one company, Amazon’s memory costs explain its increase, and the $750 billion estimates do not isolate these four. The answer’s own 96% share gives $691.2 billion. It gives no grounded reason to favor 98% over 96% at the $700 billion boundary.\"\n     6\t    }\n     7\t  },\n     8\t  \"defects\": [\n     9\t    {\n    10\t      \"reader\": \"Reader 1\",\n    11\t      \"type\": \"part above or added into its whole\",\n    12\t      \"figures\": [\"F12\", \"F11\", \"F15\"],\n    13\t      \"decision\": \"uphold\",\n    14\t      \"why\": \"The answer calls F12 “the supported floor from e1947” for the data-center forecast. Using more than $730 billion as that floor puts the data-center portion above F11’s $720 billion total and puts F15’s $705.6 billion outcome below its own floor. The governing rule says “a part cannot exceed its whole.”\"\n    15\t    }\n    16\t  ]\n    17\t}reread-packets/W03.claude.json\n     1\t{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"Combined capital expenditure of $715-745bn (F7) is the sum of three companies' guidance and one estimate: Alphabet $195-205bn (e1254), Meta $125-145bn (e1549), Amazon $220bn (e1649), and Microsoft about $175bn (F6). Microsoft's figure is sized by an accounting identity: the four-company 2026 tallies (e5014 $720-745bn, e1856 near $745bn, e2458 $760bn) minus the other three companies' guidance of $540-570bn. It is cross-checked against e922 ($30.88bn in the January-March quarter, up 84%) and the e4405 backlog. The decisive step is the data-center share of 95-100% (F13), a related figure the answer explains: e4082 and e1416 describe the same roughly $750bn 2026 totals as data-center spending, and e5376/e2759 put the top five's 2026 data-center capex near $800bn, of which these four are the bulk. The answer says why these figures apply to these companies, to 2026 and to data-center capex. It also says why the resulting $679-745bn range sits mostly in rung 4: the midpoint is about $712bn, and the range drops below $700bn only when the low guidance ends are combined with the lowest share, a cut no passage indicates.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nreread-packets/W03.codex.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v6\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"no\",\n     5\t      \"ground\": \"F13 assumes data centers account for 95–100% of the four companies’ CY2026 capital spending. At F7’s $730 billion midpoint, about 95.9% must go to data centers to reach rung 4.\",\n     6\t      \"why_not\": \"e5014 calls AI infrastructure a large portion of spending. The $750 billion comparisons in e1416 and e4082 do not isolate these four companies, and e5376 includes a fifth. The closest comparison, e1947, puts the same four companies’ CY2026 AI infrastructure spending above $730 billion. The answer treats it as a repeat without explaining how much of that spending counts as data-center capex. Taking the midpoint of F14 therefore does not explain why spending of at least $700 billion is more likely.\"\n     7\t    }\n     8\t  },\n     9\t  \"defects\": []\n    10\t}reread-packets/W06.claude.json\n     1\t{\"members\": {\"v41\": {\"supported\": \"no\", \"ground\": \"F7, the writer's own estimate of 85% FY2028 growth applied to the $20 billion FY2027 base (F1, e3660), gives F8 = $37 billion. F7 discounts management's 'more than double' (F2, e4856; repeated in e4691 and e4764) toward outside CPU-market growth of about 50% a year (F4, e4687; F5 and F6, e4857). It cites AMD's benchmark claims (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404) as a sign of contested share and adoption breadth (e4689, e5044, e4692) as a push the other way.\", \"why_not\": \"The rung 2/3 line sits at exactly 100% growth on the $20 billion base. The only passages about NVIDIA's own FY2028 CPU revenue put it above that line: the CFO's and CEO's 'more than double', and 'double' in e4691. No passage gives the size of a cut to 85%, or any bound that keeps FY2028 below $40 billion. The 85% is a blend with no stated weights: 'weighting the company target most heavily, tempered toward the market pace'. The related figures are market-wide average growth rates, for the server CPU market (AMD) and the CPU market (Bank of America), averaged over years out to 2030. The answer says only that doubling 'requires large share gains'. It does not explain why a market average should cap a product that is still ramping and is NVIDIA's alone, or how those multi-year rates line up with FY2028. It also does not explain why share gains are more likely to fall short of doubling than to reach it. In fact it concedes that adoption 'supports a pace well above the market' and that the AMD benchmarks are 'weighed lightly'. Its time-and-execution discount is the default for a product with no shipments, yet the passages expect $20 billion of revenue in FY2027. The answer does no volume, price or selling-quarter check, and it takes the FY2027 base, itself unreported guidance, at full value. Its move condition ('rung 2 if FY2027 lands at or above $20 billion') is the very base it already assumes. So nothing in the passages supports the choice of the side below $40 billion.\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nreread-packets/W06.codex.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v41\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n     5\t      \"ground\": \"On the $20 billion FY2027 base in e3660, 50% growth would put Vera at $30 billion and doubling would put it at $40 billion. The CPU market projections in e4687 and e4857 imply roughly 50% annual growth over a period that includes FY2028. Expected deployments across major buyers (e4689) support Vera gaining share and growing faster than that market. Anthropic is still evaluating Vera (e5044), and AMD reports competitive performance (e5399–e5402, e5404), giving the writer grounds to discount NVIDIA’s doubling outlook (e4691, e4856). The writer’s 85% growth assumption yields $37 billion, within rung 3.\",\n     6\t      \"why_not\": \"\"\n     7\t    }\n     8\t  },\n     9\t  \"defects\": []\n    10\t}reread-packets/W07.claude.json\n     1\t{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"Total capex of the four is about $720bn (F11). It is the three guided companies' $540-570bn (e1254, e1549, e1649) plus Microsoft at $165bn, the answer's estimate (F10). That estimate comes from the $30.88bn Jan-Mar quarter, up 84% (e922), and from the four-company tally (e5014) less the three guided companies, $150-205bn (F9). The 98% data-center share (F14) is explained by e1947: the same four companies, for CY2026, indicated more than $730bn of AI-infrastructure spending, at or above the total-capex tallies. Meta's $130bn of AI capex sitting inside its guidance (e3686) also supports it. Result: $705.6bn, rung 4. The answer also bounds the rung from the passages. The e1947 floor of more than $730bn and the four-company tallies of $720-760bn (e5014, e1856, e2458) all lie in rung 4. The ~$800bn figures cover five companies, Oracle among them (e2759, e5055), so rung 5 is not reached. Caveats: the point estimate is only $5.6bn above the $700bn line, and it sits below the e1947 floor the answer itself cites. The Microsoft run-rate alone ($156bn), or the 96% end of the stated share bound, would give rung 3. The answer names this risk and explains why the chosen side is more likely (repeated guidance raises, the e1947 floor).\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nreread-packets/W07.codex.json\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v6\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"no\",\n     5\t      \"ground\": \"F14 assigns 98% of the $720 billion total in F11 to data centers, giving $705.6 billion. The main comparison is e1947, which covers the same four companies and year but measures AI-infrastructure spending.\",\n     6\t      \"why_not\": \"At $720 billion, rung 4 requires at least $700 billion of data-center capex, leaving at most $20 billion for everything else. The answer does not explain why enough of e1947's AI-infrastructure spending counts as data-center capex to make that outcome more likely. Meta's spending mix (e3686, e1549) covers one company, Amazon's memory costs (e1649) explain only its increase, and the $750 billion estimates (e1416, e4082) do not isolate these four companies. Even the answer's claimed 96% lower bound would give $691.2 billion, in rung 3.\"\n     7\t    }\n     8\t  },\n     9\t  \"defects\": [\n    10\t    {\n    11\t      \"type\": \"part above or added into its whole\",\n    12\t      \"figures\": [\"F12\", \"F11\", \"F15\"],\n    13\t      \"detail\": \"The answer explicitly treats F12's more than $730 billion as a floor for the four companies' data-center capex. That floor exceeds both its $720 billion forecast for their total capex and its $705.6 billion forecast for the data-center portion.\"\n    14\t    }\n    15\t  ]\n    16\t}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
