{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23 && cat -n reread-key.json REREAD-TALLY.json reread-reasons.json && nl -ba S/answers/claude.json | sed -n '195,292p;378,404p' && nl -ba S/system.txt && nl -ba S/user.txt | sed -n '1,9p;25,32p;44,47p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t{\n     2\t \"W01\": {\n     3\t  \"label\": \"blind4 U01\",\n     4\t  \"case\": \"P-v8\",\n     5\t  \"writer\": \"K-unresolved-unjustified\",\n     6\t  \"answer\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/controls/K-unresolved-unjustified.json\",\n     7\t  \"case_dir\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/P-v8\"\n     8\t },\n     9\t \"W02\": {\n    10\t  \"label\": \"blind4 U06\",\n    11\t  \"case\": \"E\",\n    12\t  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n    13\t  \"answer\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json\",\n    14\t  \"case_dir\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E\"\n    15\t },\n    16\t \"W03\": {\n    17\t  \"label\": \"blind3 T10\",\n    18\t  \"case\": \"S\",\n    19\t  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n    20\t  \"answer\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/answers/claude.json\",\n    21\t  \"case_dir\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S\"\n    22\t },\n    23\t \"W04\": {\n    24\t  \"label\": \"blind4 U05\",\n    25\t  \"case\": \"E\",\n    26\t  \"writer\": \"codex\",\n    27\t  \"answer\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json\",\n    28\t  \"case_dir\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E\"\n    29\t },\n    30\t \"W05\": {\n    31\t  \"label\": \"blind4 U04\",\n    32\t  \"case\": \"S\",\n    33\t  \"writer\": \"K-unresolved-justified\",\n    34\t  \"answer\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/controls/K-unresolved-justified.json\",\n    35\t  \"case_dir\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S\"\n    36\t },\n    37\t \"W06\": {\n    38\t  \"label\": \"blind3 T15\",\n    39\t  \"case\": \"E\",\n    40\t  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n    41\t  \"answer\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/claude.json\",\n    42\t  \"case_dir\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E\"\n    43\t },\n    44\t \"W07\": {\n    45\t  \"label\": \"blind4 U02\",\n    46\t  \"case\": \"S\",\n    47\t  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n    48\t  \"answer\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json\",\n    49\t  \"case_dir\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S\"\n    50\t },\n    51\t \"W08\": {\n    52\t  \"label\": \"blind4 U08\",\n    53\t  \"case\": \"S\",\n    54\t  \"writer\": \"codex\",\n    55\t  \"answer\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json\",\n    56\t  \"case_dir\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S\"\n    57\t },\n    58\t \"W09\": {\n    59\t  \"label\": \"blind4 U03\",\n    60\t  \"case\": \"E\",\n    61\t  \"writer\": \"K-unsupported\",\n    62\t  \"answer\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/controls/K-unsupported.json\",\n    63\t  \"case_dir\": \"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E\"\n    64\t }\n    65\t}     1\t{\n     2\t \"verdict\": \"fail\",\n     3\t \"lines\": [\n     4\t  \"W01 blind4 U01  P-v8  K-unresolved-unjustified  v8 unresolved settled=no     votes=['no', 'no'] [expects no] ok\",\n     5\t  \"W02 blind4 U06  E     claude                    v41 forecast   settled=no     votes=['no', 'no'] [must settle] ok\",\n     6\t  \"W03 blind3 T10  S     claude                    v6 forecast   settled=split  votes=['no', 'yes', 'no', 'yes'] [must settle] MISS\",\n     7\t  \"W04 blind4 U05  E     codex                     v41 forecast   settled=no     votes=['no', 'no'] [expects no] ok\",\n     8\t  \"W05 blind4 U04  S     K-unresolved-justified    v6 unresolved settled=yes    votes=['yes', 'yes'] [must settle] ok\",\n     9\t  \"W06 blind3 T15  E     claude                    v41 forecast   settled=split  votes=['yes', 'no', 'yes', 'no'] [expects yes] MISS\",\n    10\t  \"W07 blind4 U02  S     claude                    v6 forecast   settled=split  votes=['no', 'yes', 'no', 'yes'] [expects no] MISS\",\n    11\t  \"W08 blind4 U08  S     codex                     v6 forecast   settled=yes    votes=['yes', 'yes'] [must settle] ok\",\n    12\t  \"W09 blind4 U03  E     K-unsupported             v41 forecast   settled=no     votes=['no', 'no'] [expects no] ok\"\n    13\t ],\n    14\t \"defects\": [\n    15\t  [\n    16\t   \"W07\",\n    17\t   \"blind4 U02\",\n    18\t   \"part above or added into its whole\",\n    19\t   [\n    20\t    \"F11\",\n    21\t    \"F12\",\n    22\t    \"F15\"\n    23\t   ],\n    24\t   [\n    25\t    \"uphold\",\n    26\t    \"uphold\"\n    27\t   ]\n    28\t  ]\n    29\t ],\n    30\t \"stops\": [\n    31\t  [\n    32\t   \"W07\",\n    33\t   \"blind4 U02\",\n    34\t   \"part above or added into its whole\",\n    35\t   [\n    36\t    \"F11\",\n    37\t    \"F12\",\n    38\t    \"F15\"\n    39\t   ],\n    40\t   [\n    41\t    \"uphold\",\n    42\t    \"uphold\"\n    43\t   ]\n    44\t  ]\n    45\t ]\n    46\t}     1\t{\n     2\t \"W01\": {\"first\": \"compatible\", \"note\": \"Both: the reason names no gap; the passages place the outcome in rung 2 (Codex: no reason another band is as likely; Claude: the answer's own calculation and outside figures give rung 2).\"},\n     3\t \"W02\": {\"first\": \"compatible\", \"note\": \"Both reject on the band-deciding assumption left unexplained: Codex, nothing explains why non-Vera CPU sales stay at or under $6B at the 90% share; Claude, no size for the shortfall below 2x and an unsupported 90% share, which at the 1.5x anchor gives $27B. Neither rests a yes on the whole range staying at $30B or more.\"},\n     4\t \"W04\": {\"first\": \"compatible\", \"note\": \"Both: the 10% shortfall has no size or explained comparison; the cited passages show risk, not why revenue lands below $40B but at or above $30B.\"},\n     5\t \"W05\": {\"first\": \"compatible\", \"note\": \"Both: the gap holds; e1947 does not separate data-center spending from the four companies' total (Codex: no data-center breakdown; Claude: the total under another label), e5376 covers five companies, and the share needed (~94%) is not placed by the passages (Claude: Meta's 90-104% straddles it).\"},\n     6\t \"W08\": {\"first\": \"compatible\", \"note\": \"Both apply Alex's rule: e1947 (same four companies, same year, AI infrastructure close to total) and Meta's AI share explain why a deduction under $45B is more likely; Codex notes they do not measure it directly, which the rule allows.\"},\n     7\t \"W09\": {\"first\": \"compatible\", \"note\": \"Both: the 15% cut has no size or explained comparison; the passages explain why sales could fall short, not why the shortfall is small enough to stay at or above $30B.\"}\n     8\t}\n   195\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"four largest AI hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"capital expenditure\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\n   196\t      \"kind\": \"computed\",\n   197\t      \"basis\": \"forecast\",\n   198\t      \"value\": 720,\n   199\t      \"low\": null,\n   200\t      \"high\": null,\n   201\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   202\t      \"evidence_id\": null,\n   203\t      \"quote_number\": null,\n   204\t      \"qualifier\": null,\n   205\t      \"inputs\": [\"F4\", \"F10\"],\n   206\t      \"operation\": \"sum\",\n   207\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n   208\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   209\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   210\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   211\t    },\n   212\t    {\n   213\t      \"id\": \"F12\",\n   214\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft and Alphabet\", \"measure\": \"AI infrastructure spending\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\n   215\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n   216\t      \"basis\": \"guidance\",\n   217\t      \"value\": 730,\n   218\t      \"low\": null,\n   219\t      \"high\": null,\n   220\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   221\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e1947\",\n   222\t      \"quote_number\": \"$730 billion\",\n   223\t      \"qualifier\": \"more than\",\n   224\t      \"inputs\": null,\n   225\t      \"operation\": null,\n   226\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n   227\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   228\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   229\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   230\t    },\n   231\t    {\n   232\t      \"id\": \"F13\",\n   233\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"Meta Platforms\", \"measure\": \"AI capital expenditure\", \"scope\": \"total\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\n   234\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n   235\t      \"basis\": \"third_party\",\n   236\t      \"value\": 130,\n   237\t      \"low\": null,\n   238\t      \"high\": null,\n   239\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   240\t      \"evidence_id\": \"e3686\",\n   241\t      \"quote_number\": \"$130 billion\",\n   242\t      \"qualifier\": \"none\",\n   243\t      \"inputs\": null,\n   244\t      \"operation\": null,\n   245\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n   246\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   247\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   248\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   249\t    },\n   250\t    {\n   251\t      \"id\": \"F14\",\n   252\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"four largest AI hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"data-center share of capital expenditure\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\n   253\t      \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n   254\t      \"basis\": \"forecast\",\n   255\t      \"value\": 98,\n   256\t      \"low\": null,\n   257\t      \"high\": null,\n   258\t      \"unit\": \"%\",\n   259\t      \"evidence_id\": null,\n   260\t      \"quote_number\": null,\n   261\t      \"qualifier\": null,\n   262\t      \"inputs\": null,\n   263\t      \"operation\": null,\n   264\t      \"basis_text\": \"My estimate of the share of the four companies' 2026 capital expenditure that is data-center (AI infrastructure) spending. The companies' indicated AI-infrastructure total, more than $730bn (F12), is at or above the total-capex tally of $720-745bn (F8), which puts the share near 100%; Meta's planned AI capex of $130bn (F13) sits inside its $125-145bn total guidance (F2), about 96% of the midpoint; Amazon's raise to $220bn was mostly memory-chip costs, i.e. server hardware (e1649); outside estimates put spending on data centers at about $750bn (e1416, e4082). No passage sizes non-data-center spending such as offices or logistics, so I keep a 2% residual; the passages bound the share between about 96% and 100%.\",\n   265\t      \"rests_on\": [\"F12\", \"F8\", \"F13\", \"F2\", \"e1649\", \"e1416\", \"e4082\"],\n   266\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   267\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   268\t    },\n   269\t    {\n   270\t      \"id\": \"F15\",\n   271\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"four largest AI hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"data-center capex\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\n   272\t      \"kind\": \"computed\",\n   273\t      \"basis\": \"forecast\",\n   274\t      \"value\": 705.6,\n   275\t      \"low\": null,\n   276\t      \"high\": null,\n   277\t      \"unit\": \"USD bn\",\n   278\t      \"evidence_id\": null,\n   279\t      \"quote_number\": null,\n   280\t      \"qualifier\": null,\n   281\t      \"inputs\": [\"F11\", \"F14\"],\n   282\t      \"operation\": \"share\",\n   283\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n   284\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   285\t      \"outcome_for\": [\"v6\"],\n   286\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   287\t    },\n   288\t    {\n   289\t      \"id\": \"F16\",\n   290\t      \"what\": {\"population\": \"four largest hyperscalers\", \"measure\": \"capital expenditure\", \"scope\": \"combined\", \"period\": \"CY2026\"},\n   291\t      \"kind\": \"source\",\n   292\t      \"basis\": \"third_party\",\n   378\t      \"basis_text\": null,\n   379\t      \"rests_on\": null,\n   380\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   381\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   382\t    }\n   383\t  ],\n   384\t  \"answers\": [\n   385\t    {\n   386\t      \"view_id\": \"v6\",\n   387\t      \"shape\": \"forecast\",\n   388\t      \"rung\": 4,\n   389\t      \"reference_unavailable\": null,\n   390\t      \"reasons\": \"Reference: outside tallies put the four companies' combined 2026 capital spending at roughly $720-745bn (Zacks, e5014; about $745bn tracking, e1856), inside rung 4 ($700-800bn). Build: the three companies with full-year guidance add to $540-570bn (F4): Alphabet $195-205bn after raising from $180-190bn (e1254), Meta $125-145bn after a raise (e1549), Amazon $220bn after raising from $200bn (e1649). Microsoft gives no calendar-year guidance in the evidence; its January-March 2026 quarter was $30.88bn, up 84% (e922), and a continued ramp of about 16% a quarter gives about $156bn, while the tally less the other three implies $150-205bn; I use $165bn (F10, my estimate). Total capital expenditure is therefore about $720bn (F11; $705-735bn across the guidance ranges). The question asks for data-center spending, which is narrower than total capex, so I apply a 98% data-center share (F14, my estimate): the companies indicated more than $730bn of AI-infrastructure spending (e1947), at or above the total tally; Meta's $130bn of AI capex (e3686) sits inside its $125-145bn guidance; Amazon's raise was mostly memory chips; outside estimates put spending on data centers near $750bn (e1416, e4082). The share is bounded at about 96-100% by these passages, giving data-center capex of about $706bn (F15), rung 4; the supported floor from e1947 (more than $730bn of AI infrastructure) also lies in rung 4. Rung 5 is not supported: the $795-800bn figures (NVIDIA, e2759; BofA, e5055) cover five companies, Oracle among them in BofA's list, so the four sit below $800bn, and the highest four-company tally is $760bn (e2458). Pushing up: three mid-year guidance raises, rising memory costs that lift the dollar cost of the same build (e1649), and strong cloud demand (Azure backlog $678bn, e4405; Google Cloud +82%, e4406; AWS's best quarter in four years, e4404). Pushing down: about $130bn of US data-center projects blocked or delayed this year (e122, not specific to these four), and grid-connection and electrician constraints (e5292, e1545); the memory shortage (e4566) limits volume but raises price, so it cuts both ways. The forecast sits close to the $700bn edge, so the main risk is rung 3 if Microsoft's ramp or the data-center share comes in lower; the repeated raises make an undershoot of guidance less likely. The many reprints of NVIDIA's top-five figure count as one claim (e4564's '$1.3 billion' for 2027 is a misprint of $1.3 trillion).\",\n   391\t      \"why\": null,\n   392\t      \"cites\": [\"e5014\", \"e1856\", \"e1254\", \"e1549\", \"e1649\", \"e922\", \"e4405\", \"e1947\", \"e3686\", \"e1416\", \"e4082\", \"e2458\", \"e2759\", \"e5055\", \"e4406\", \"e4404\", \"e122\", \"e5292\", \"e1545\", \"e4566\"],\n   393\t      \"pushes\": [\n   394\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e1254\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   395\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e1549\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   396\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e1649\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   397\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e922\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   398\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e5014\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   399\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e1856\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   400\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e1947\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   401\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e2458\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   402\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e1416\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   403\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e4082\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n   404\t        {\"evidence_id\": \"e2759\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"},\n     1\tYou are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging the current rungs of a family of related number theses: one company's quantities that overlap, such as a total and its segment, a level and its growth, or a later year that rests on an earlier year's base. Use only the supplied evidence and themes in force. Each rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n     2\t\n     3\t- Judge every member from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently) and the themes in force, as if for the first time. Earlier forecasts are not shown: whether a conclusion changed is compared afterwards.\n     4\t- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate whose pace you forecast, informed by guidance, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted subject to the evidence requirement below; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. Build the family's figures once and read every member's outcome from them: a part cannot exceed its whole, a figure that spans several periods or products gives a question only its share, and a quantity the family shares has one forecast. Compare each outcome with its rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n     5\t- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n     6\t- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n     7\t- \"Themes in force\" lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on a member and report it in that member's theme_pushes with the direction it pushes that member (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \"updated since this thesis was last judged\" may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n     8\t- Your conclusions must be consistent with each other and with the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open.\n     9\t- Return the family's figures and one answer per member, as the JSON schema defines them. Every number an answer rests on is a figure; a number you judge rather than read or compute is an estimate, stated as yours. If the chosen rung depends on a share, deduction or discount, show what in the passages supports its size or gives a bound that justifies the rung. If the evidence cannot support a forecast, answer unresolved and explain the gap. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n     1\t[Family] members:\n     2\tThesis v6 (ai-capex, theme, focus \"Combined data-center capital expenditures of the four largest AI hyperscalers\", 2026, due 2027-02-28): How much will the four largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026?\n     3\tLadder: rung 1 Lowest range: Combined spending is below $500 billion. / rung 2 Lower-middle range: Combined spending is at least $500 billion and below $600 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: Combined spending is at least $600 billion and below $700 billion. / rung 4 Upper-middle range: Combined spending is at least $700 billion and below $800 billion. / rung 5 Highest range: Combined spending is at least $800 billion.\n     4\t\n     5\tRelations: none.\n     6\tShared quantities, one forecast figure each; on every arithmetic path from an outcome, through rates and multiples too, the first figure of that quantity reached is its forecast: none.\n     7\tOutcome keys (population | measure | scope | period), in USD bn, to be used exactly: v6 = four largest AI hyperscalers | data-center capex | combined | CY2026.\n     8\t\n     9\t[Evidence in view for the family, 50 items]\n    25\t    Quote: Amazon (NASDAQ: AMZN) is doing the same, going from an estimated $200 billion to a new projection of $220 billion, although, in fairness, the increase was mostly due to higher memory chip costs.\n    26\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4567; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    27\t- e1856 [third_party_view; 247Wallst.com, by Gerelyn Terzo; original source 247Wallst.com, by Gerelyn Terzo ; from gnews:61ba718265df905f5dcc9dad 2026-08-11] Combined 2026 capital spending by the four largest hyperscalers was tracking near $745 billion.\n    28\t    Quote: Combined 2026 capital spending across the four largest hyperscalers is tracking near $745 billion\n    29\t- e1947 [party_statement; fool.com (Patrick Sanders); original source Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet ; from gnews:c8ac167e2356a75994ebc26f 2026-08-13] Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure in calendar 2026.\n    30\t    Quote: hyperscalers Amazon , Meta Platforms , Microsoft , and Alphabet have indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year\n    31\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1738, e2079; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    32\t- e2079 [party_statement; uk.finance.yahoo.com; original source Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers ; from gnews:1219126db2e6631aa1264142 2026-08-16] Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers guided combined 2026 capital spending of roughly $720 billion to $745 billion, about 77% above the prior year.\n    44\t    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3139; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    45\t- e3686 [third_party_view; Michael Parekh; original source Meta ; from gnews:8e2fa4cb6d1ac1bfdd389e09 2026-08-30] Meta plans $130 billion of AI capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n    46\t    Quote: Meta plans $130 billion of AI capex this year.\n    47\t- e4082 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) ; from gnews:15f1ea8f70a1fc52417f55b5 2026-09-03] The article cites estimates of about $750 billion in AI data-center infrastructure spending in calendar 2026 and more than $1 trillion in calendar 2027.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"D=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers; rg -n 'part|whole|share|def publish' \"'$D/calc_small.py | tail -65 && ls poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 && nl -ba $D/BLIND3-RESULT.md | tail -78 && nl -ba $D/BLIND4-RESULT.md | head -63'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"D=docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers; rg -n 'part|whole|share|def publish' \"'$D/calc_small.py | tail -65 && ls poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 && nl -ba $D/BLIND3-RESULT.md | tail -78 && nl -ba $D/BLIND4-RESULT.md | head -63'","aggregated_output":"2:the five named defects (S1 a number misread, S2 wrong arithmetic, S3/S8 a part above its whole or added into it, S4 two forecasts of one\n14:BASES = {'reported', 'guidance', 'consensus', 'third_party', 'forecast'}\n15:EXTERNAL = {'consensus', 'guidance', 'third_party'}\n17:ARITY = {'sum': (2, None), 'product': (2, None), 'difference': (2, 2), 'ratio': (2, 2), 'share': (2, 2), 'growth': (2, 2),\n32:_BODIES = [   # [54] the complete numeric expressions, longest forms first; a quote_number must be one of them, whole\n129:    year, part = m.group(1) + m.group(2), m.group(3)\n130:    if part is None:\n132:    elif part.startswith('H'):\n133:        quarters = {1, 2} if part == 'H1' else {3, 4}\n135:        a, _, b = part.partition('-')\n144:    \"\"\"[54] Two periods overlap: the same label, or parsed fiscal periods of one year that share a quarter.\"\"\"\n215:        if money + pct > 1:                                  # never USD bn × % (use share), never two amounts or two percentages\n221:    if op == 'share':\n222:        return units[0] if units[1] == '%' else None         # [54] a share of a percentage is a percentage\n239:    if op == 'share':\n304:    def fits(self, part, whole):\n305:        \"\"\"[54] The part can be at most the whole: for every combination of the leaves they share, some value of each one's own leaves\n306:        puts the part at or below the whole (independent ranges: the part's low end at most the whole's high end).\"\"\"\n307:        lp, lw = self.leaves(part), self.leaves(whole)\n308:        shared = lp & lw\n311:        for s in self.assignments(shared):\n312:            low_part = min(self.point(part, dict(s, **a)) for a in self.assignments(lp - shared))\n313:            high_whole = max(self.point(whole, dict(s, **a)) for a in self.assignments(lw - shared))\n314:            if low_part > high_whole + 1e-9 * max(1.0, abs(high_whole)):\n467:            errors.append(('S1', f'{fid}: \"{qn}\" is part of the longer expression \"{inside[0]}\" in {ev}; copy it whole' if inside else\n508:            hint = ' (use share for a whole times a percentage)' if op == 'product' else ''\n522:        if op == 'share' and not (0 <= got[ins[1]][0] and got[ins[1]][1] <= 100):\n523:            errors.append(('S2', f'{fid}: a share takes a percentage from 0 to 100'))\n585:            else:                                          # [D741] a past period's reported share or multiple carried to another period\n615:def _shared(family, figs, outcomes):\n628:    for want in family.get('shared', []):\n655:def _part_whole(family, figs, calc, outcomes):\n659:        if rel.get('type') != 'part_of' or rel['part'] not in outcomes or rel['whole'] not in outcomes:\n661:        p_what, w_what = members[rel['part']]['what'], members[rel['whole']]['what']\n662:        pairs = [(outcomes[rel['part']], outcomes[rel['whole']], 'outcome')]\n668:            for p in _tops(outcomes[rel['part']], p_key, figs):\n669:                for w in _tops(outcomes[rel['whole']], w_key, figs):\n671:        for part, whole, label in pairs:\n672:            pv, wv = calc.interval(part), calc.interval(whole)\n675:            if not calc.fits(part, whole):\n676:                errors.append(('S3', f'{label}: the part {part} ({pv[0]:g}–{pv[1]:g}) exceeds the whole {whole} ({wv[0]:g}–{wv[1]:g})'))\n682:    scopes = family.get('part_scopes', [])\n689:            parts = [i for i in same if i['what']['scope'] == ps['part']]\n690:            wholes = [i for i in same if i['what']['scope'] == ps['whole']]\n691:            if not parts or not wholes:\n694:                if any(_overlap(p['what']['period'], w['what']['period']) for p in parts for w in wholes):\n695:                    errors.append(('S8', f'{fid}: adds {ps[\"part\"]} into {ps[\"whole\"]} for the same period, which already includes it'))\n696:            elif not (ins[0]['what']['scope'] == ps['whole'] and ins[1]['what']['scope'] == ps['part']\n698:                      and f['what']['scope'] not in (ps['part'], ps['whole'])):\n699:                errors.append(('S8', f'{fid}: a difference may take {ps[\"part\"]} from {ps[\"whole\"]} of the same period only to give the rest'))\n718:        *parts_p, out = parsed\n722:            total = [q for p in parts_p for q in p[1]]\n725:            ok = parts_p[1][1] <= parts_p[0][1] and parts_p[0][1] - parts_p[1][1] == out[1]\n734:    scopes = family.get('part_scopes', [])\n740:            if any({ps['part'], ps['whole']} <= rested for ps in scopes):\n741:                recorded.append({'type': 'part and whole in one estimate', 'figure': fid})\n744:            if any(_quantity(i['what']) != _quantity(f['what']) for i in money) and f['operation'] != 'share':\n748:            if f['operation'] == 'sum' and any({ps['part'], ps['whole']} <= {i['what']['scope'] for i in money} for ps in scopes):\n749:                recorded.append({'type': 'part and whole added for different periods', 'figure': fid})\n777:    errors = answer_errors + _shared(family, figs, outcomes) + _part_whole(family, figs, engine, outcomes) + _sums(family, figs)\n782:def publish_decision(family, calc, result):\n789:        ends = {rel.get('part'), rel.get('whole'), rel.get('later'), rel.get('earlier')} - {None}\nBUILD.txt\nCHECKED.json\nE\nO\nP\nP-v4\nP-v8\nREREAD-TALLY.json\nREREAD2-TALLY.json\nS\nTALLY.json\nadjudication\ncodex-writers.log\nreader-key.json\nreader-packets\nreread-adjudication\nreread-key.json\nreread-packets\nreread2-adjudication\nreread2-controls\nreread2-key.json\nreread2-packets\n    60\t  12 with a request contradiction (run 2), to 12 of 12 with no refusal (run 3). D742's rule was followed by all six writers it applies\n    61\t  to.\n    62\t- **The stop this time is in the measuring instrument.** The reader brief's definition is narrower than the format the writer was\n    63\t  given. Both adjudicators corrected it from the format rule.\n    64\t- **The readers did their job on substance.** They found the one reply whose decisive assumption is unsupported (R07). That is the\n    65\t  kind of error the paid re-send is meant to measure in gpt-6-luna.\n    66\t- **Council round 57 found two more points** (`codex-r57.md`):\n    67\t  - R01, the only usable S reply, marks Zacks's $720–745B of *total* capex as the market reference for *data-center* capex. Claude's\n    68\t    reader noticed it in its ground but filed no named defect.\n    69\t  - R11's prose offers \"$138 billion in Q4\" as reaching rung 1, but it gives $428.32B against the $431.2B edge. The forecast itself\n    70\t    sits in rung 2.\n    71\t- **Open disclosures:**\n    72\t  - O's $18B reading (split three runs running);\n    73\t  - wrong numbers in prose, which this run's adjudications did not raise;\n    74\t  - R07's unsupported share.\n    75\t\n    76\t## 4. The blinded re-read (D743, Alex: \"补上那条，重读一遍\"; `reread-packets/`, `reread-adjudication/`, `REREAD-TALLY.json`)\n    77\t\n    78\tFresh readers re-read the same 12 replies under the corrected range rule. Two controls were mixed in, under a fresh shuffle Q01–Q14.\n    79\t\n    80\t- **The range repair works.**\n    81\t  - C-disjoint was marked broken structure by both readers.\n    82\t  - C-overlap was marked by neither.\n    83\t  - R01 (now Q07), the range across a rung edge, was not marked broken structure by either reader.\n    84\t- **STOP again, as written: Q10 (P-v8, Codex writer).**\n    85\t  - Both readers marked \"number misread\" on F6: \"$108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%\" given as its midpoint, 108.\n    86\t  - Both adjudicators rejected it. The schema the writer was given says \"'$4.5 billion, plus or minus 3%' in full, given as low and high\n    87\t    or its midpoint as value\".\n    88\t  - The same mark has been raised about 12 times across the four readings, by single readers until now. Every adjudication rejected it.\n    89\t- **The class.** The readers' packet carries the user text and the answer, but not the format rules the writer was given. The\n    90\t  adjudication packet carries them and its definitions say \"allowing what the format rules permit\". Both stops caused by the instrument\n    91\t  (the range, the midpoint) come from this one gap.\n    92\t- **C-overlap's other mark is my error.** Both readers marked \"two forecasts\" on C-overlap because my control kept E's original text\n    93\t  (\"85% … $37 billion\") beside the new range. The readers were right to flag the inconsistency. It does not touch the range rule the\n    94\t  control tests.\n    95\t- **Judgement noise:**\n    96\t  - S's Codex reply (90% share) was settled \"not supported\" by both adjudicators in the first reading, and is \"supported\" by both fresh\n    97\t    readers now.\n    98\t  - S's Claude reply (Q07) is split at both levels this time: Codex says not supported and upholds two marks; Claude rejects them.\n    99\t  - O's Claude reply (Q09) is split again.\n   100\t- **No settled defect.** Every adjudicated mark on Q09, Q10 and Q14 was rejected by both adjudicators. Q07's two marks split.\n   101\t\n   102\t## 5. The second blinded re-read (D744, Alex: \"批准再重读一次\"; `reread2-packets/`, `reread2-adjudication/`, `REREAD2-TALLY.json`)\n   103\t\n   104\tReaders now get the same rule text as adjudicators (`blind_rules.py`). There were five clean controls, under a fresh shuffle T01–T17.\n   105\t\n   106\t- **The instrument is calibrated.**\n   107\t  - All five controls passed: K-midpoint was not marked; K-misread was marked by both readers; K-overlap was not marked; K-disjoint was\n   108\t    marked by both; K-unsupported was \"not supported\" by both.\n   109\t  - On the 12 replies, no defect was marked by both readers. The class from §4 (format-permitted forms marked) did not recur.\n   110\t- **STOP on two registered lines, as written.**\n   111\t  1. **A settled defect on an accepted reply: T10 (S, Claude writer), F11.** e1416 writes \"an estimated $750 billion\". The writer\n   112\t     recorded qualifier \"none\", and both adjudicators upheld \"number misread\". But \"estimated\" is not in the qualifier list the schema\n   113\t     enumerates (`calc_small.QUALIFIERS`), so \"none\" was the only value the format allowed. The descriptions the adjudicators saw say\n   114\t     \"the qualifier the quote writes just before the number\" and do not list the allowed values. This is a format gap: the closed list\n   115\t     and the description disagree.\n   116\t  2. **A case with zero usable first replies: S.**\n   117\t     - S's Codex reply is \"not supported\" by both readers. Its 90% data-center share decides rung 3, and the passages do not size the\n   118\t       share.\n   119\t     - S's Claude reply is split at both levels. Its 95–100% share sets the range, and Codex says the passages do not support 95% as\n   120\t       the low end.\n   121\t     - Both writers met D741 ruling 3's letter (a stated deduction), but not its \"supported\" part. That is a judgement weakness of the\n   122\t       writers on this question, caught by the readers, not an instrument error.\n   123\t- **Other settled support:**\n   124\t  - E's Codex reply is now \"not supported\" by both readers. Its 15% cut from the guided $40B floor decides rung 3 without passage\n   125\t    support. Earlier readers had supported it, and the two-step brief (name the evidence for the band-setting assumption) made readers\n   126\t    stricter on such cuts.\n   127\t  - O's Codex reply is supported after adjudication; O's Claude reply is split again.\n   128\t- **Usable replies per case:**\n   129\t\n   130\t  | Case | Usable replies |\n   131\t  |---|---|\n   132\t  | P | both writers |\n   133\t  | P-v4 | both writers |\n   134\t  | P-v8 | both writers |\n   135\t  | O | Codex |\n   136\t  | E | Claude |\n   137\t  | S | none |\n     1\t# The D745 targeted test: result\n     2\t\n     3\tRun folder: `poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/`. It was registered in `SMALL-DESIGN.md` §5 [D745] and scored by\n     4\t`blind4.py tally` (`TALLY.json`).\n     5\t\n     6\t## 1. Verdict: FAIL (registered line \"every S and E reply has a settled yes\")\n     7\t\n     8\t| Packet | Case | Writer | Shape | First readers (Codex, Claude) | Adjudicators (Codex, Claude) | Settled | Line |\n     9\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n    10\t| U05 | E | codex | forecast, rung 3 | no, no | (none) | **no** | **MISS** |\n    11\t| U02 | S | claude | forecast, rung 4 | no, yes | no, yes | split | MISS (inconclusive) |\n    12\t| U08 | S | codex | forecast, rung 4 | no, yes | no, yes | split | MISS (inconclusive) |\n    13\t| U06 | E | claude | forecast, rung 3 | no, yes | no, yes | split | MISS (inconclusive) |\n    14\t| U04 | S | control: justified unresolved | unresolved | yes, no | yes, no | split | control not confirmed |\n    15\t| U07 | P-v8 | codex | forecast, rung 2 | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    16\t| U09 | P-v8 | claude | forecast, rung 2 | yes, yes | (none) | yes | ok |\n    17\t| U01 | P-v8 | control: unjustified unresolved | unresolved | no, no | (none) | no | ok |\n    18\t| U03 | E | control: unsupported (15% cut) | forecast | no, no | (none) | no | ok |\n    19\t\n    20\t- **Refusals and defects.** All 6 first replies passed the schema and the code checks and were publishable. No reader marked a defect,\n    21\t  so no defect is settled or split.\n    22\t- **Writers never withheld.** 0 of 6 replies answered unresolved, including on S and E, where the new sentence invites it.\n    23\t- **One construction change, disclosed.** The registered row for K-unresolved-justified reads \"S made unresolved with the specific\n    24\t  gap\". It was first built on the third run's S Claude reply, which the current code refuses (S1, F11 \"estimated\"). It was rebuilt on\n    25\t  the third run's S Codex reply with the same reason text, and the code accepted it. The reason's five passage citations were checked\n    26\t  against the request.\n    27\t\n    28\t## 2. What split, and along which line\n    29\t\n    30\tEvery disagreement split by model family, and the four adjudications did not settle any of them: 8 of 8 adjudicator votes went with\n    31\ttheir own family. On S and E, Codex read \"supported\" as \"a passage states the size of the band-deciding assumption, or bounds the\n    32\tsame quantity\". Claude read it as \"the passages give a checkable bound that makes the rung most likely\".\n    33\t\n    34\t- **S (data-center spending of the four largest cloud companies in 2026; the rung 3/4 edge is $700B).** Both writers forecast rung 4.\n    35\t  The Claude writer took $720B of total spending at a 98% data-center share. The Codex writer took $745B minus a $25B non-data-center\n    36\t  deduction. Every reader on both sides read the same passage as decisive: e1947, \"more than $730 billion combined on AI\n    37\t  infrastructure\", for the same four companies.\n    38\t  - Claude: AI infrastructure sits inside data-center spending, so e1947 is a floor above $700B.\n    39\t  - Codex: e1947 gives no data-center split, so it does not bound the share.\n    40\t  - The answer's own stated lower bound gives $691B, in rung 3 (Codex adjudicator, U02).\n    41\t- **E (FY2028 Vera CPU revenue; management's \"more than double\" of $20B sits on the $40B edge between rungs 2 and 3).**\n    42\t  - The Codex writer realised 90% of the $40B reference. Its basis was deployment plans, a customer evaluation and a competitor's\n    43\t    benchmarks, and it said itself that \"the passages give no order sizes or shipment pace\". Both first readers, one per family,\n    44\t    said not supported, so no adjudication was needed (corrected after council round 61, which caught \"all four readers\").\n    45\t  - The Claude writer bracketed the pace between the server-CPU market pace (about 1.5x, e4687 and e4857) and the guidance (2x), then\n    46\t    took a 90% Vera share.\n    47\t    - The Codex adjudicator showed that the bracket fails under the answer's own share: 1.5 × $20B × 90% = $27B, in rung 4.\n    48\t    - The Claude adjudicator rescued it by arguing a near-full Vera share from e4691 and e4856.\n    49\t- **The justified-unresolved control.** Codex accepted the reason \"no passage gives the share\". Claude rejected it on the same e1947\n    50\t  floor, so the control's expected reading is itself contested.\n    51\t\n    52\t## 3. What the frozen gold does on the same kind of question\n    53\t\n    54\tThese are read for reasoning only. The gold questions differ from S and E in ladder and window.\n    55\t- **q04 (the same four companies' 2026 data-center spending).** Reader B took the big four's total plans of about $650B as the\n    56\t  reference, \"the figure NVIDIA management also cites\", with no deduction, and bet $650–720B.\n    57\t- **q19 (FY2027 stand-alone Vera revenue).** Reader B discounted management's $20B to $8–18B. The reasoning was the launch timing\n    58\t  (about two selling quarters) and the units the figure implies ($5,000 per CPU means four million units), against one counter-signal.\n    59\t  No passage states the discount.\n    60\t\n    61\tThe frozen ruler's own answers are reasoned bets of the kind the Claude family accepts. D741 ruling 3 (Alex) is stricter for S: the\n    62\tdeduction must be stated.\n    63\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && nl -ba blind_rules.py | head -86 && nl -ba blind4.py | sed -n '248,295p;340,405p' && nl -ba blind_adjudicate.py | sed -n '15,51p' && nl -ba calc_small.py | sed -n '655,678p;782,799p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && nl -ba blind_rules.py | head -86 && nl -ba blind4.py | sed -n '248,295p;340,405p' && nl -ba blind_adjudicate.py | sed -n '15,51p' && nl -ba calc_small.py | sed -n '655,678p;782,799p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t\"\"\"The one source of the defect definitions and the writer's format material for judging-v13s blind readers and adjudicators (D744).\n     2\t\n     3\tReaders and adjudicators get the same text: the writer's system text, every schema description, and these definitions, so that what\n     4\tthe format rules permit is never marked as a defect by one and cleared by the other.\n     5\t\"\"\"\n     6\timport json\n     7\tfrom pathlib import Path\n     8\t\n     9\tDEFINITIONS = \"\"\"Definitions (judge each against the passages and the format rules the writer was given, shown below; what the format rules permit is\n    10\tnot a defect):\n    11\t- supported: the forecast follows from the shown passages and the answer's stated assumptions; a reader could check it against them.\n    12\t  For a forecast, the assumption that decides the band must rest on the passages in one of three ways: its size, a bound that\n    13\t  justifies the rung, or, when the passages do not give the asked quantity, a related figure that the answer explains. An explained\n    14\t  related figure satisfies this requirement even though the other side of the line remains possible. It is explained when the\n    15\t  answer says why the comparison applies to the companies, the period and the quantity asked, and why, under the answer's own stated\n    16\t  assumptions, the chosen side of the line is more likely; an answer that does not explain this is not supported (a ruling). For an unresolved\n    17\t  member, supported means its reason holds: after the strongest relevant passages, a gap remains that keeps the rung unsettled (a\n    18\t  ruling).\n    19\t- number misread: a source figure's number, scale, sign or qualifier differs from what its passage's quote writes, allowing what the\n    20\t  format rules permit.\n    21\t- wrong arithmetic: a computed figure does not equal its operation on its inputs, within the tolerance the format rules state.\n    22\t- part above or added into its whole: a segment is larger than the total it belongs to, or is added again into a total that already\n    23\t  includes it (directly or through an intermediate figure or an estimate).\n    24\t- two forecasts of one quarter: two different figures are used as the value of one quantity and period. A growth rate computed from\n    25\t  guidance and used only as a stated benchmark for an estimate is not a second value, when one figure supplies the quarter in the\n    26\t  calculation (a ruling). Look through any rate or multiple, including inside a stated assumption, for a second dollar value of the\n    27\t  quarter.\n    28\t- own estimate passed off as the market's reference: a figure marked as the market's reference is, or rests on, the writer's own\n    29\t  estimate or forecast. A reference that applies a past period's reported share or multiple to another period (a quarter's mix\n    30\t  applied to the year) is our comparison estimate, not the market's (a ruling).\n    31\t- broken structure: a link that does not resolve, a figure that depends on itself, a missing or doubled answer, an outcome that is not\n    32\t  the question's quantity, or a rung whose band does not hold the outcome. For a range outcome, the format allows \"a band it overlaps,\n    33\t  with the reason in reasons\": such a rung is not broken structure; a band the range does not reach is. A total used for a narrower\n    34\t  quantity (total capital expenditure for data-center spending) without a stated deduction or a supported bound is an outcome not of\n    35\t  the question's quantity (a ruling).\"\"\"\n    36\t\n    37\t\n    38\tdef descriptions(schema, path=''):\n    39\t    \"\"\"Every description in the schema, keyed by its field path.\"\"\"\n    40\t    found = {}\n    41\t    if isinstance(schema, dict):\n    42\t        if isinstance(schema.get('description'), str):\n    43\t            found[path.strip('/') or 'answer'] = schema['description']\n    44\t        for key, value in schema.items():\n    45\t            if key != 'description':\n    46\t                step = '' if key in ('properties', 'items', 'anyOf') else key\n    47\t                found.update(descriptions(value, f'{path}/{step}' if step else path))\n    48\t    elif isinstance(schema, list):\n    49\t        for value in schema:\n    50\t            found.update(descriptions(value, path))\n    51\t    return found\n    52\t\n    53\t\n    54\tdef allowed_qualifiers(schema):\n    55\t    \"\"\"The qualifier values the writer's own schema allowed (D747: an older run's schema, not today's list).\"\"\"\n    56\t    options = schema['properties']['figures']['items']['properties']['qualifier']['anyOf']\n    57\t    found = [o['enum'] for o in options if isinstance(o, dict) and 'enum' in o]\n    58\t    if len(found) != 1:\n    59\t        raise SystemExit('the schema does not hold exactly one qualifier list')\n    60\t    return found[0]\n    61\t\n    62\t\n    63\tdef format_material(case_folder):\n    64\t    \"\"\"The writer's system text and every schema description, as shown to readers and adjudicators.\"\"\"\n    65\t    case_folder = Path(case_folder)\n    66\t    schema = json.loads((case_folder / 'schema.json').read_text())\n    67\t    return '\\n'.join(['# The system text the writer was given', (case_folder / 'system.txt').read_text(), '',\n    68\t                      '# The format rules the writer was given (schema descriptions)',\n    69\t                      json.dumps(descriptions(schema), indent=1, ensure_ascii=False), '',\n    70\t                      'The qualifier values the format allows (a qualifier not in this list cannot be recorded): '\n    71\t                      + ', '.join(allowed_qualifiers(schema)) + '.'])\n   248\tBLIND3_REREAD2 = {'T10': ('S', 'claude'), 'T15': ('E', 'claude')}                 # SMALL-DESIGN.md §5 [D747]\n   249\tREREAD_FROM_BLIND4 = ('U01', 'U02', 'U03', 'U04', 'U05', 'U06', 'U08')\n   250\tREREAD_EXPECTED = {'blind4 U01': 'no', 'blind4 U02': 'no', 'blind4 U03': 'no', 'blind4 U05': 'no', 'blind3 T15': 'yes'}\n   251\tREREAD_LABELS = {f'blind4 {p}' for p in REREAD_FROM_BLIND4} | {f'blind3 {p}' for p in BLIND3_REREAD2}\n   252\tORIGINAL_BLIND3 = {'K-unsupported': 'E'}      # U03 is the third run's E Codex reply unchanged: it is read under the request it was written for\n   253\t\n   254\t\n   255\tdef _display(path):\n   256\t    \"\"\"Every answer shown the same way (parsed and printed with one indent), so a control is not told apart by its layout.\"\"\"\n   257\t    return json.dumps(bt.first_json(Path(path).read_text()), indent=1, ensure_ascii=False)\n   258\t\n   259\t\n   260\tdef reread(out):\n   261\t    \"\"\"[D747] Nine stored answers re-read under the shared definitions with Alex's comparison ruling: seven from this run and the third\n   262\t    run's T10 (its spending argument only) and T15 (the bracket both families accepted). Each packet carries the request its answer\n   263\t    was written for; the constructed controls U01 and U04 are read under the request they were built for.\"\"\"\n   264\t    key4 = json.loads((out / 'reader-key.json').read_text())\n   265\t    key3 = json.loads((BASE / 'reread2-key.json').read_text())\n   266\t    items = [{'label': f'blind4 {pid}', 'case': key4[pid]['case'], 'writer': key4[pid]['writer'],\n   267\t              'answer': str(out / key4[pid]['answer']),\n   268\t              'case_dir': str(BASE / ORIGINAL_BLIND3[key4[pid]['writer']] if key4[pid]['writer'] in ORIGINAL_BLIND3\n   269\t                              else out / key4[pid]['case'])} for pid in REREAD_FROM_BLIND4]\n   270\t    for pid, (case, writer) in BLIND3_REREAD2.items():\n   271\t        if (key3[pid]['case'], key3[pid]['writer']) != (case, writer):\n   272\t            raise SystemExit(f'{pid} in the second re-read key is {key3[pid]}, not {case}/{writer}')\n   273\t        items.append({'label': f'blind3 {pid}', 'case': case, 'writer': writer, 'answer': str(BASE / case / 'answers' / f'{writer}.json'),\n   274\t                      'case_dir': str(BASE / case)})\n   275\t    order = list(range(len(items)))\n   276\t    random.Random(hashlib.sha256(b'D747 ' + json.dumps(items, sort_keys=True).encode()).hexdigest()).shuffle(order)\n   277\t    folder = out / 'reread-packets'\n   278\t    folder.mkdir(exist_ok=False)\n   279\t    key = {}\n   280\t    for n, i in enumerate(order, 1):\n   281\t        item = items[i]\n   282\t        case_dir = Path(item['case_dir'])\n   283\t        pid = f'W{n:02d}'\n   284\t        key[pid] = item\n   285\t        (folder / f'{pid}.md').write_text('\\n'.join([REREAD_BRIEF, format_material(case_dir), '', '# The request as the writer saw it', '',\n   286\t                                                     (case_dir / 'user.txt').read_text(), '', '# The answer', '',\n   287\t                                                     _display(item['answer']), '']))\n   288\t        (folder / f'{pid}.codex-prompt.txt').write_text(\n   289\t            f'Read the file {folder / f\"{pid}.md\"} and nothing else (no other file, no web). Follow the brief at its top: read the request '\n   290\t            'and the answer it contains, and return only the JSON the brief asks for, with no text before or after it.\\n')\n   291\t    (out / 'reread-key.json').write_text(json.dumps(key, indent=1))\n   292\t    print(f'{len(order)} packets in {folder}; key in reread-key.json (keep from readers)')\n   293\t\n   294\t\n   295\tdef reread_tally(out):\n   340\t            ok = value == expected if expected else value in ('yes', 'no')\n   341\t            if not ok:\n   342\t                verdict = 'fail' if expected and value != 'split' else ('inconclusive' if verdict == 'pass' else verdict)\n   343\t            lines.append(f'{pid} {item[\"label\"]:11} {item[\"case\"]:5} {item[\"writer\"]:25} {view} {a[\"shape\"]:10} settled={value:6} '\n   344\t                         f'votes={votes} [{\"expects \" + expected if expected else \"must settle\"}] {\"ok\" if ok else \"MISS\"}')\n   345\t        if either and adj is None and pid not in need:\n   346\t            need.append(pid)\n   347\t        elif either and adj is not None:\n   348\t            decisions = [{_defect_key(d): d.get('decision') for d in x.get('defects', [])} for x in adj]\n   349\t            for d in sorted(either, key=str):\n   350\t                pair = [decisions[0].get(d), decisions[1].get(d)]\n   351\t                if pair != ['reject', 'reject']:\n   352\t                    defects.append([pid, item['label'], d[0], list(d[1]), pair])\n   353\t    if need:\n   354\t        print('not final; needed:', ' | '.join(sorted(set(need))))\n   355\t        return\n   356\t    stops = [d for d in defects if (d[4] == 'both readers' or d[4] == ['uphold', 'uphold']) and not d[1].startswith('blind3 T10')\n   357\t             and 'K-' not in key[d[0]]['writer']]\n   358\t    if stops:\n   359\t        verdict = 'fail'\n   360\t    result = {'verdict': verdict, 'lines': lines, 'defects': defects, 'stops': stops}\n   361\t    (out / 'REREAD-TALLY.json').write_text(json.dumps(result, indent=1, ensure_ascii=False))\n   362\t    print('\\n'.join(lines))\n   363\t    print('defects (settled or split):', defects)\n   364\t    print('stops:', stops)\n   365\t    print('VERDICT:', verdict)\n   366\t\n   367\t\n   368\tif __name__ == '__main__':\n   369\t    cmd = sys.argv[1] if len(sys.argv) == 3 else ''\n   370\t    target = Path(sys.argv[2]).resolve() if len(sys.argv) == 3 else None\n   371\t    {'build': build, 'check': check, 'packets': packets, 'tally': tally, 'reread': reread, 'reread-tally': reread_tally}.get(\n   372\t        cmd, lambda _: (_ for _ in ()).throw(SystemExit(__doc__)))(target)\n    15\timport sys\n    16\tfrom pathlib import Path\n    17\t\n    18\tsys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent))\n    19\tfrom blind_rules import DEFINITIONS, descriptions, format_material  # noqa: E402\n    20\t\n    21\tHEADER = \"\"\"# Adjudication (judging-v13s blind test)\n    22\t\n    23\tTwo readers disagreed about the answer below. Settle each point against the passages in the request and the format rules quoted\n    24\tbelow, not against either reader's threshold. Read only what is given here.\n    25\t\n    26\tFor each member, decide **supported** (yes or no). For each defect a reader marked, decide **uphold** or **reject**, quoting the\n    27\tpassage words or the format rule that decides it.\n    28\t\n    29\t\"\"\" + DEFINITIONS + \"\"\"\n    30\t\n    31\tReturn only JSON: {\"members\": {\"<view id>\": {\"supported\": \"yes|no\", \"why\": \"...\"}},\n    32\t \"defects\": [{\"reader\": \"Reader 1|Reader 2\", \"type\": \"...\", \"figures\": [...], \"decision\": \"uphold|reject\", \"why\": \"...\"}]}\n    33\t\"\"\"\n    34\t\n    35\t\n    36\tdef _json(text):\n    37\t    \"\"\"A reading: the outermost JSON object in the file.\"\"\"\n    38\t    return json.loads(text[text.find('{'):text.rfind('}') + 1])\n    39\t\n    40\t\n    41\tdef reading_lines(label, reading):\n    42\t    lines = []\n    43\t    for member, mark in sorted(reading.get('members', {}).items()):\n    44\t        line = f\"- {label} on member {member}: supported = {mark.get('supported')}. Ground: {mark.get('ground', '')}\"\n    45\t        if mark.get('why_not'):\n    46\t            line += f\" Why not: {mark['why_not']}\"\n    47\t        lines.append(line)\n    48\t    defects = reading.get('defects', [])\n    49\t    if not defects:\n    50\t        lines.append(f'- {label} marks no defect.')\n    51\t    for defect in defects:\n   655\tdef _part_whole(family, figs, calc, outcomes):\n   656\t    errors = []\n   657\t    members = family['members']\n   658\t    for rel in family.get('relations', []):\n   659\t        if rel.get('type') != 'part_of' or rel['part'] not in outcomes or rel['whole'] not in outcomes:\n   660\t            continue\n   661\t        p_what, w_what = members[rel['part']]['what'], members[rel['whole']]['what']\n   662\t        pairs = [(outcomes[rel['part']], outcomes[rel['whole']], 'outcome')]\n   663\t        for period in family.get('compare_periods', []):\n   664\t            if period == p_what['period']:\n   665\t                continue\n   666\t            p_key = (p_what['population'], p_what['measure'], p_what['scope'], period)\n   667\t            w_key = (w_what['population'], w_what['measure'], w_what['scope'], period)\n   668\t            for p in _tops(outcomes[rel['part']], p_key, figs):\n   669\t                for w in _tops(outcomes[rel['whole']], w_key, figs):\n   670\t                    pairs.append((p, w, period))\n   671\t        for part, whole, label in pairs:\n   672\t            pv, wv = calc.interval(part), calc.interval(whole)\n   673\t            if pv is None or wv is None:\n   674\t                continue\n   675\t            if not calc.fits(part, whole):\n   676\t                errors.append(('S3', f'{label}: the part {part} ({pv[0]:g}–{pv[1]:g}) exceeds the whole {whole} ({wv[0]:g}–{wv[1]:g})'))\n   677\t    return errors\n   678\t\n   782\tdef publish_decision(family, calc, result):\n   783\t    \"\"\"'refused' when a check fails; 'held' when a member is unresolved and a declared relation joins it to another member; else\n   784\t    'publish' (an unresolved member is shown as unresolved).\"\"\"\n   785\t    if result['errors']:\n   786\t        return 'refused'\n   787\t    unresolved = {a['view_id'] for a in calc['answers'] if a.get('shape') == 'unresolved'}\n   788\t    for rel in family.get('relations', []):\n   789\t        ends = {rel.get('part'), rel.get('whole'), rel.get('later'), rel.get('earlier')} - {None}\n   790\t        if ends & unresolved and ends - unresolved:\n   791\t            return 'held'\n   792\t    return 'publish'\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import json,pathlib,sys\nfor run in sorted(pathlib.Path('\\\\''poc/data'\\\\'').glob('\\\\''judging-v13s-blind[34]-*'\\\\'')):\n for f in sorted(run.glob('\\\\''*/answers/*.json'\\\\'')):\n  if f.parent.parent.name not in sys.argv[1:]: continue\n  raw=f.read_text(); x=json.loads(raw[raw.index('\\\\''{'\\\\''):raw.rindex('\\\\''}'\\\\'')+1]); print('\\\\''\\\\n'\\\\'',run.name, f.parent.parent.name, f.stem)\n  print('\\\\'' | '\\\\''.join(i['\\\\''id'\\\\'']+'\\\\'' '\\\\''+i['\\\\''what'\\\\'']['\\\\''scope'\\\\'']+'\\\\'' '\\\\''+str(i['\\\\''value'\\\\''] if i['\\\\''value'\\\\'']\"'!=None else (i['\"'\\\\''low'\\\\''],i['\\\\''high'\\\\''])) for i in x['\\\\''figures'\\\\'']))\n  for a in x['\\\\''answers'\\\\'']: print(a['\\\\''view_id'\\\\''],a.get('\\\\''reasons'\\\\'') or a.get('\\\\''why'\\\\''))\n' O P P-v4\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import json,pathlib,sys\nfor run in sorted(pathlib.Path('\\\\''poc/data'\\\\'').glob('\\\\''judging-v13s-blind[34]-*'\\\\'')):\n for f in sorted(run.glob('\\\\''*/answers/*.json'\\\\'')):\n  if f.parent.parent.name not in sys.argv[1:]: continue\n  raw=f.read_text(); x=json.loads(raw[raw.index('\\\\''{'\\\\''):raw.rindex('\\\\''}'\\\\'')+1]); print('\\\\''\\\\n'\\\\'',run.name, f.parent.parent.name, f.stem)\n  print('\\\\'' | '\\\\''.join(i['\\\\''id'\\\\'']+'\\\\'' '\\\\''+i['\\\\''what'\\\\'']['\\\\''scope'\\\\'']+'\\\\'' '\\\\''+str(i['\\\\''value'\\\\''] if i['\\\\''value'\\\\'']\"'!=None else (i['\"'\\\\''low'\\\\''],i['\\\\''high'\\\\''])) for i in x['\\\\''figures'\\\\'']))\n  for a in x['\\\\''answers'\\\\'']: print(a['\\\\''view_id'\\\\''],a.get('\\\\''reasons'\\\\'') or a.get('\\\\''why'\\\\''))\n' O P P-v4\"","aggregated_output":"\n judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 O claude\nF1 company 25 | F2 company 18 | F3 company 7 | F6 ecosystem partners 70 | F7 other companies 42 | F9 SB Energy 1.5 | F10 Lancium 1 | F11 Anthropic 10 | F12 Thinking Machines Lab 2.5 | F4 company (2, 13) | F5 company (9, 20)\nv29 Reference: there is no market forecast of the year-end balance. The status quo is NVIDIA's reported $25 billion of equity investment commitments at July 26, 2026 (F1), exactly at rung 2's lower edge. Bank of America's '$70 billion committed in equity investments' (F6) does not place the year-end balance in rung 3: it counts funded and unfunded money together. NVIDIA bought more than $42 billion of stakes in the first half (F7) and still owed $25 billion, which together come close to BofA's figure. Derivation: NVIDIA said $18 billion (F2) of its commitments is to be funded in the rest of FY2027. That is the company's own schedule for the current half and the strongest input; it leaves about $7 billion (F3) of the July book still owed at year end. To that I add commitments signed after July that are still unfunded at year end (F4, my estimate $2-13 billion). Most new deals fund at closing (Poolside $1 billion, MediaTek $3.5 billion convertible bonds, SB Energy's first $1.5 billion). What stays on the books is staged or unclosed money: SB Energy's second $1.5 billion if its IPO slips, Lancium's milestone-linked $1 billion, and at most a $10 billion Anthropic IPO anchor if signed before year end but funded after. Huang said this year's large AI-lab investments will not repeat and that the $100 billion OpenAI plan was never a commitment, which argues against another large staged commitment. Outcome F5 = $9-20 billion, entirely below the $25 billion boundary: rung 1. What would move it to rung 2: a large part of the $18 billion slipping into next year, or a new multi-year staged commitment of more than $5 billion beyond the ones named.\n\n judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 O codex\nF1 company 25 | F2 company 18 | F3 ecosystem partners 70 | F4 Lancium 3 | F5 SB Energy 1.5 | F6 Anthropic IPO 10 | F7 Thinking Machines Lab 2.5 | F8 company 17 | F9 commitments outstanding at FY2027 Q2 end 8 | F10 commitments added after FY2027 Q2 end 8 | F11 company 16\nv29 I forecast $16 billion of outstanding equity commitments at FY2027 year-end, below the $25 billion cutoff for rung 1. NVIDIA's July balance sat at the bottom of rung 2. Its stated funding schedule and first-half investment activity support an estimate that $17 billion of the July balance will be funded before year-end, leaving $8 billion. I estimate that another $8 billion of commitments added after July will still be outstanding. Announced infrastructure and equity deals and the GDT report support continued dealmaking. The Anthropic and Thinking Machines discussions add upside, with uncertain signing and funding dates. Poolside's license and MediaTek's convertible bonds are separate from the equity commitments forecast. The earlier OpenAI financing announcement lacks evidence of a fresh addition to the July balance, and Huang explicitly ruled out treating the earlier larger plan as a commitment. The AI capex themes support continued demand for ecosystem financing, with no direct read on how much NVIDIA will still owe at year-end.\n\n judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 P claude\nF1 company 81.62 | F2 company 96.2 | F3 company 177.82 | F4 Data Center 75.25 | F5 Data Center 89.0 | F6 Data Center 164.25 | F7 company (105.84, 110.16) | F8 company 3.1 | F9 company 112.5 | F10 company 12 | F11 company 12 | F12 company 126.0 | F13 company 238.5 | F14 company 416.32 | F15 Data Center 92.5 | F16 Data Center 93.0 | F17 Data Center 221.81 | F18 Data Center 386.06 | F19 company 411 | F20 Data Center 361\nv4 Reference: Wall Street consensus of $411 billion for FY2027 (F19) sits in rung 2 ($392-431.2 billion); the 87-88% consensus growth rates (e4990, e5129) on FY2026's $215.9 billion give about $405 billion, also rung 2. Outcome from the evidence: H1 is reported at $177.8 billion (Q1 $81.62 billion, Q2 $96.2 billion; F3). Q3 guidance is $108.0 billion plus or minus 2%, excluding China data center compute; given 12 straight beats of the high end (3.1% on average over the last four) and a $5.2 billion beat of the Q2 midpoint, trimmed for the stated memory, power and packaging caps, I forecast Q3 at $112.5 billion (F9). For Q4 I forecast 12% sequential growth, the pace guided for Q3, because hyperscale growth is said to reaccelerate in Q4 as Vera Rubin supply builds and supply commitments rose to $279 billion, while supply stays constrained (F11): Q4 about $126 billion, H2 $238.5 billion, FY2027 about $416 billion (F14), rung 2, above consensus but inside the same band. Sensitivity: Q3 at the guidance midpoint and only 5% Q4 growth still gives about $399 billion (rung 2); rung 1 ($431.2 billion) would need H2 of about $253 billion, e.g. Q4 near $138 billion (+20% sequential), which a supply-constrained company is not shown to deliver. The reports of '70% full-year growth' (e3280, e3890, e4628) conflict with the call transcript (e2691), which ties about 70% growth to next year (FY2028) because of supply limits; 70% on $215.9 billion ($367 billion) would also require Q4 to fall to about $81 billion, below Q1, so I read them as the FY2028 outlook. The analysts' quarterly path (90% Q3 growth, 65% Q4 growth: e5161, e5162) implies only about $398 billion and predates the Q3 guide's stated pace. Themes: China authorizing H200 purchases (v9) is judged more likely than not; Q3 guidance assumes no China data center compute, and I do not assume China at zero for Q4, but any such sales before the late-January year end are small and supply-limited, so they sit inside the Q4 pace rather than being added on top. Hyperscaler capex judged at about $760 billion for 2026 (v6) and above the market reference for 2027 (v7, updated) supports the Q4 hyperscale reacceleration. The export-control themes judged 'No' (v16, v22, v26, v27) keep the status quo already in guidance.\nv8 Reference: Morningstar's $361 billion FY2027 Data Center estimate (F20), the only quoted outside figure for the segment, sits in rung 2 ($350-400 billion). Applying a quarter's mix to the $411 billion consensus would be my own estimate, not the market's. Outcome from the family's figures: H1 Data Center revenue is reported at $164.25 billion (Q1 $75.25 billion, Q2 $89.0 billion; $164 billion per e5309). Data Center's share of revenue has risen every quarter, from 87.9% to 92.5% in Q2 (e3399). Management says Q3 growth comes primarily from the AI data center business (ACIE leading, Vera Rubin about 20% of Q3 Data Center revenue), while Edge Computing, the remainder ($7.2 billion in Q2), grew a slower 13%. I therefore take Data Center at 93.0% of the $238.5 billion H2 revenue forecast (F16), giving $221.8 billion for H2 and about $386 billion for FY2027 (F18), rung 2. That stays below the total's $416 billion, so the part does not exceed the whole. It sits above Morningstar's pre-Q2 estimate, consistent with SemiAnalysis's view that H2 Data Center revenue will beat analyst estimates by 20% (e1135). The 'just under $330 billion' run rate (e5310) annualizes H1 flat and is not a forecast. Sensitivity: H2 at the guidance midpoint with 5% Q4 growth and a 92.5% share gives about $369 billion (rung 2). Rung 1 ($400 billion) needs about $236 billion of H2 Data Center revenue, i.e. H2 company revenue near $253 billion, the same acceleration the supply constraints (e2870, e3115, e5067) argue against. Rung 3 would need H2 Data Center below about $186 billion, under even the low end of Q3 guidance carried flat. e4907's $83.7 billion Q2 figure conflicts with the company's $89.0 billion release and is disregarded. Themes: possible China H200 authorization (v9, judged more likely than not) is Data Center upside that guidance excludes; it is not assumed to be zero but is small and supply-limited within FY2027. The hyperscaler capex themes (v6, v7) support Q4 hyperscale demand. The export-control themes judged 'No' keep the status quo.\n\n judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 P codex\nF1 company 81.62 | F2 company 96.22 | F3 company 177.84 | F4 company 108 | F5 company 113.6 | F6 company 16.4 | F7 company 112 | F8 company 17 | F9 company 131.04 | F10 company 420.88 | F11 company 411 | F12 Data Center 75.25 | F13 Data Center 89.02 | F14 Data Center 164.27 | F15 Data Center 92.5 | F16 Data Center 93 | F17 Data Center 93.5 | F18 Data Center 104.16 | F19 Data Center 122.52 | F20 Data Center 390.95 | F21 Data Center 361\nv4 I forecast $420.88 billion, in rung 2. The $411 billion analyst consensus also sits in rung 2. NVIDIA reported $177.84 billion in the first half. The last quarter's guidance beat and Vera Rubin shipments support a Q3 forecast of $112 billion against the $108 billion guidance midpoint. I expect $131.04 billion in Q4 as more Vera Rubin systems reach hyperscalers. Current spending supports that ramp. The higher calendar 2027 spending view mainly supports later deliveries. Memory shortages and long equipment lead times limit the upside this fiscal year. The export-policy views reduce the risk of additional restrictions, and limited H200 authorization offers a small chance of late sales. The open Commerce investigation gives no direction for this year's shipments.\nv8 I forecast $390.95 billion, in rung 2. Morningstar's $361 billion estimate is the outside reference and also sits in rung 2. NVIDIA reported $164.27 billion in the first half. I allocate 93% of the company-wide Q3 forecast to Data Center, or $104.16 billion, as AI cloud, industrial and enterprise sales drive most of the segment's growth. I raise the share to 93.5% in Q4, or $122.52 billion, as Vera Rubin supply expands and hyperscaler sales pick up. Strong hyperscaler spending and the prospect of limited H200 authorization support the forecast, with most of the calendar 2027 spending impact falling later. The outlook for fewer new export restrictions eases downside risk, while the Commerce investigation remains open. Supply and deployment constraints make the top rung less likely.\n\n judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 P-v4 claude\nF1 company 81.62 | F2 company 96.2 | F3 company 177.82 | F4 company 17.86 | F5 company 108.0 | F6 company 91.0 | F7 company 5.71 | F8 company 65 | F9 company 68 | F10 company 4.62 | F11 company 4.5 | F12 company 112.86 | F13 company 14 | F14 company 128.66 | F15 company 419.34 | F16 company 411\nv4 Reference: the Wall Street consensus for FY2027 revenue is $411 billion (e4333), which sits in rung 2 ($392 billion to below $431.2 billion); the Zacks consensus of 87.4% growth on FY2026's $215.9 billion (about $405 billion, e4990) is also rung 2. Outcome: the first half is reported at $177.8 billion (Q1 $81.62 billion, Q2 $96.2 billion). Q3 is guided to $108.0 billion plus or minus 2%, above the $104.6 billion consensus for the quarter; NVIDIA beat its guidance midpoint by 5.7% in Q2 and 4.6% in Q4 FY2026 and has topped the high end of guidance in each of the past 12 quarters, so I forecast Q3 at about $112.9 billion (4.5% above the midpoint, trimmed because capacity is sold out and supply covers roughly 70% of demand). For Q4 I forecast about 14% sequential growth to about $128.7 billion: management says hyperscale growth reaccelerates in Q4 as Vera Rubin supply builds, Vera Rubin is in production shipments with purchase orders from every major hyperscaler and is guided to about 20% of Q3 Data Center revenue at a higher revenue per gigawatt ($40 billion versus $25 billion for Blackwell), and supply commitments rose from $119 billion to $279 billion; the pace stays below the roughly 18% realized in Q2 because supply remains constrained through FY2028. FY2027 = 177.8 + 112.9 + 128.7, about $419 billion: rung 2, the same rung as the reference, a little above consensus. Rung 1 would need Q4 above about $140.5 billion, a 24.5% sequential jump against the supply limits management states. Rung 3 would need Q3 plus Q4 below about $214 billion, for example Q3 at the guidance low end and Q4 flat near $108 billion, which contradicts the beat record and the reacceleration management describes. The '70% full-year growth' reports (e3280, e3890, e4628) are not used for FY2027: the CFO's own words (e2691) put approximately 70% growth on next year, which e5227 identifies as FY2028; applied to FY2027 it would mean about $367 billion and a Q4 near $81 billion, below Q2, inconsistent with the $108 billion Q3 guidance. Competing accelerator commitments (AMD with OpenAI and Meta, Broadcom's $115 billion AI revenue for 2027) deploy mainly in calendar 2027 and NVIDIA is supply-bound now, so they do not lower FY2027. Themes in force: limited Chinese authorization of H200 purchases (judged likely) is upside the zero-China-compute guidance excludes, small and at most in Q4, so my Q4 pace does not assume zero China; hyperscaler 2026 data-center capital expenditure near $760 billion supports demand above supply; 2027 hyperscaler spending falls mainly in FY2028. None of these moves the outcome across $431.2 billion or $392 billion.\n\n judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 P-v4 codex\nF1 company 411 | F2 company 81.62 | F3 company 96.22 | F4 company 108 | F5 company 113.6 | F6 company 112.5 | F7 company 12 | F8 company 126 | F9 company 416.34\nv4 I forecast FY2027 revenue of $416.34 billion, in rung 2. The $411 billion analyst consensus also sits in rung 2. The first two quarters are reported. For Q3, the previous guidance beat and CoreWeave's live Rubin deployment support $112.5 billion against NVIDIA's $108 billion midpoint. For Q4, the expected pickup in hyperscaler sales as Rubin supply expands supports $126 billion. Memory constraints and long channel lead times limit the volume that can ship before year-end and keep the forecast below rung 1. The calendar 2026 hyperscaler spending theme supports near-term demand, and the updated calendar 2027 theme adds support for orders entering Q4. Limited Chinese H200 authorization gives Q4 modest upside because shipments would have little time to land. The other export-control themes leave the near-term baseline broadly in place.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import json,pathlib,sys\nfor run in sorted(pathlib.Path('\\\\''poc/data'\\\\'').glob('\\\\''judging-v13s-blind[34]-*'\\\\'')):\n for f in sorted(run.glob('\\\\''*/answers/*.json'\\\\'')):\n  if f.parent.parent.name not in sys.argv[1:]: continue\n  raw=f.read_text(); x=json.loads(raw[raw.index('\\\\''{'\\\\''):raw.rindex('\\\\''}'\\\\'')+1]); print('\\\\''\\\\n'\\\\'',run.name, f.parent.parent.name, f.stem)\n  print('\\\\'' | '\\\\''.join(i['\\\\''id'\\\\'']+'\\\\'' '\\\\''+i['\\\\''what'\\\\'']['\\\\''scope'\\\\'']+'\\\\'' '\\\\''+str(i['\\\\''value'\\\\''] if i['\\\\''value'\\\\'']\"'!=None else (i['\"'\\\\''low'\\\\''],i['\\\\''high'\\\\''])) for i in x['\\\\''figures'\\\\'']))\n  for a in x['\\\\''answers'\\\\'']: print(a['\\\\''view_id'\\\\''],a.get('\\\\''reasons'\\\\'') or a.get('\\\\''why'\\\\''))\n' S E P-v8\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import json,pathlib,sys\nfor run in sorted(pathlib.Path('\\\\''poc/data'\\\\'').glob('\\\\''judging-v13s-blind[34]-*'\\\\'')):\n for f in sorted(run.glob('\\\\''*/answers/*.json'\\\\'')):\n  if f.parent.parent.name not in sys.argv[1:]: continue\n  raw=f.read_text(); x=json.loads(raw[raw.index('\\\\''{'\\\\''):raw.rindex('\\\\''}'\\\\'')+1]); print('\\\\''\\\\n'\\\\'',run.name, f.parent.parent.name, f.stem)\n  print('\\\\'' | '\\\\''.join(i['\\\\''id'\\\\'']+'\\\\'' '\\\\''+i['\\\\''what'\\\\'']['\\\\''scope'\\\\'']+'\\\\'' '\\\\''+str(i['\\\\''value'\\\\''] if i['\\\\''value'\\\\'']\"'!=None else (i['\"'\\\\''low'\\\\''],i['\\\\''high'\\\\''])) for i in x['\\\\''figures'\\\\'']))\n  for a in x['\\\\''answers'\\\\'']: print(a['\\\\''view_id'\\\\''],a.get('\\\\''reasons'\\\\'') or a.get('\\\\''why'\\\\''))\n' S E P-v8\"","aggregated_output":"\n judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 E claude\nF1 Vera CPU 20 | F2 Vera CPU 2 | F3 Vera CPU 40 | F4 server CPU 50 | F5 CPU 35 | F6 CPU 170 | F7 Vera CPU 85 | F8 Vera CPU 37\nv41 Reference: NVIDIA's own guidance. CFO Colette Kress reaffirmed about $20 billion of CPU revenue for FY2027 (F1, e3660) and said FY2028 would more than double it (F2, e4856); NVIDIA's wording ties both the base and the doubling to Vera CPU revenue (e4691, e4856). That gives more than $40 billion (F3), the bottom edge of rung 2 (Bullish, $40-50 billion). Forecast: this is a target for a full fiscal year that has not yet started, for a product line still ramping, so it is discounted for time and execution rather than taken in full; the whole of FY2028 is still to be sold. Outside benchmarks put CPU market growth near 50% a year (AMD's server CPU estimate, F4; Bank of America's path from $35 billion to $170 billion, F5 and F6), roughly half the pace NVIDIA needs, so a doubling requires large share gains. Adoption breadth supports a pace well above the market: Kress says every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and system OEM will deploy Vera (e4689), Anthropic is evaluating it for CPU-heavy agentic work (e5044), and Arm-based servers already hold a large share of data-center revenue (e4692). Against that, AMD's own benchmarks claim its Venice and EPYC 9996 parts beat Vera per core, per platform, per rack and in memory bandwidth (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404); these are a competitor's marketing tests from one article and are weighed lightly, but they show standalone CPU share will be contested. The CEO's and other outlets' repeats of the doubling (e4764, e4691, e4856) add no new fact. The 2027 hyperscaler capex theme, upgraded to the upper-middle range, supports strong AI infrastructure demand in the year that matters but does not by itself imply a doubling of CPU revenue. I forecast 85% growth (F7) on the $20 billion base, giving $37 billion (F8): rung 3 (Neutral), just below the rung-2 line. It would move to rung 2 if FY2027 lands at or above $20 billion with Vera Rubin shipping on schedule, or if NVIDIA sizes FY2028 Vera orders; a confirmed Rubin delay or an FY2027 shortfall would push it toward rung 4.\n\n judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 E codex\nF1 CPU 20 | F2 FY2028 Vera CPU revenue relative to the FY2027 CPU outlook 2 | F3 Vera CPU 40 | F4 Vera CPU revenue as a share of the stated CPU opportunity 85 | F5 Vera CPU 17 | F6 Vera CPU FY2028 versus FY2027 2 | F7 Vera CPU 34\nv41 Management's reference starts at $40 billion, the lower edge of rung 2. Its more-than-double language points above that threshold without specifying by how much. My forecast is $34 billion, in rung 3. I estimate $17 billion of FY2027 Vera revenue from the $20 billion CPU outlook and expect Vera revenue to double as deployments broaden. NVIDIA expects broad deployment, and Anthropic's evaluation is a concrete sign of interest. Neither statement gives committed purchase volumes. AMD's own tests suggest that Vera faces competition for CPU-intensive workloads, with an uncertain effect on sales. The hyperscaler spending outlook, especially the updated calendar 2027 assessment, supports the ramp, but gives no Vera allocation.\n\n judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 P-v8 claude\nF1 Data Center 75.25 | F2 Data Center 89.0 | F3 Data Center 164.25 | F4 company 96.2 | F5 company 91.0 | F6 company 5.71 | F7 company 65 | F8 company 68 | F9 company 4.62 | F10 company 108.0 | F11 company 5.0 | F12 company 113.4 | F13 company 12.0 | F14 company 127.01 | F15 company 240.41 | F16 company 92.8 | F17 Data Center 223.1 | F18 Data Center 387.35 | F19 Data Center 361 | F20 company 81.62 | F21 company 177.82 | F22 company 418.23 | F23 company 411\nv8 Reference: the only outside figure for this exact quantity is Morningstar's FY2027 Data Center estimate of $361 billion (F19, e2295), which sits in rung 2 ($350-400 billion); it was published before the Q2 report. For the whole company, analysts expect $411 billion of FY2027 revenue (F23); my company forecast is $418.2 billion (F22), slightly above, in line with NVIDIA's habit of beating guidance. Calculation: reported Data Center revenue was $75.25 billion in Q1 and $89.0 billion in Q2, so $164.25 billion in H1 (F3). Q3 company revenue is guided at $108.0 billion plus or minus 2%; NVIDIA beat the midpoint by 5.7% in Q2 and 4.6% in Q4 FY2026 and has beaten the high end for 12 straight quarters, so I forecast a 5% beat, $113.4 billion (F11, F12, my estimate). For Q4 I keep the guided 12% sequential pace (F13, my estimate): hyperscale growth is set to reaccelerate as Vera Rubin supply builds and supply commitments rose to $279 billion, but supply covers only about 70% of demand and stays tight through FY2028; that gives $127.0 billion (F14). Data Center was 92.5% of Q2 revenue and its share has risen every quarter; I assume 92.8% for H2 (F16), so H2 Data Center revenue is $223.1 billion (F17) and FY2027 Data Center revenue is $387.4 billion (F18), rung 2, the same rung as the reference but well above it, because Q2 and the Q3 guidance came in after the reference. Boundaries: falling below $350 billion would need H2 Data Center revenue under $185.8 billion, below the Q2 pace, although the Q3 guidance alone implies about $100 billion a quarter. Reaching $400 billion would need $235.8 billion, about 20% sequential Data Center growth in both quarters while supply is capped. So rung 1 is the upside risk (SemiAnalysis expects H2 Data Center revenue 20% above analyst estimates, e1135, but gives no base figure), not the most likely outcome. The reports of '70% growth for the full fiscal year' (e3280) refer to FY2028 per the earnings call (e2691); applied to FY2027 they would contradict H1 plus the Q3 guidance, so they move nothing. The H1 run-rate of just under $330 billion (e5310) annualises a past half and is not used. Competing accelerator deals (e1040, e4314) mostly deploy after FY2027 while NVIDIA sells all it can supply, so they do not move this year. Themes: China authorizing H200 purchases (judged likely) is an upside the Q3 guidance excludes; no evidence sizes it, and it is not large enough to lift the outcome past $400 billion on its own. Hyperscaler capex for 2026 sits about at the market reference, and the raised 2027 view supports the Q4 reacceleration, but supply, not demand, limits FY2027.\n\n judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 P-v8 codex\nF1 Data Center 361 | F2 Data Center 75.25 | F3 Data Center 89.02 | F4 Data Center 164.27 | F5 company 96.22 | F6 company 108 | F7 company 113.6 | F8 company 16 | F9 company 111.62 | F10 company 15 | F11 company 128.36 | F12 Data Center 93 | F13 Data Center 93.5 | F14 Data Center 103.8 | F15 Data Center 120.01 | F16 Data Center 223.81 | F17 Data Center 388.08\nv8 I forecast about $388 billion of FY2027 Data Center revenue, in rung 2. Morningstar's $361 billion segment estimate is the direct outside reference and also falls in rung 2. The reported first half contributes $164.27 billion. I forecast about $104 billion in Q3 and $120 billion in Q4. NVIDIA's Q3 company guidance anchors the pace. The prior guidance beat and initial Rubin shipments support a modest beat, and the live CoreWeave deployment and management's expectation of faster hyperscaler growth support a further Q4 step-up. Memory constraints and long delivery lead times limit how much of that demand can ship by year-end, keeping the forecast below $400 billion. The calendar 2026 spending theme supports demand this fiscal year. The higher calendar 2027 spending theme offers some late-year upside, with most of the spending window falling after NVIDIA's fiscal close. Possible H200 authorization adds limited Q4 upside because authorization may come late and shipment timing is uncertain. The other export-policy themes leave near-term shipment assumptions unchanged.\n\n judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 S claude\nF1 company total (195, 205) | F2 company total (fiscal year equals calendar year) (125, 145) | F3 company total 220 | F4 company total (fiscal Q3 FY2026, January-March 2026) 30.88 | F5 company total (fiscal Q3 FY2026, January-March 2026) 84 | F6 company total 175 | F7 combined (715, 745) | F8 combined (720, 745) | F9 combined, tracking 745 | F10 combined, planned 760 | F11 combined 750 | F12 combined 800 | F13 combined (95, 100) | F14 combined (679.25, 745)\nv6 Rung 4 (at least $700bn and below $800bn). Reference: outside tallies of the four companies' own 2026 guidance put their combined capital expenditure at roughly $720-745bn (F8, e5014), which sits in rung 4. Build: Alphabet guides $195-205bn (raised from $180-190bn, e1254), Meta $125-145bn (raised, e1549) and Amazon $220bn (raised from $200bn, mostly on memory costs, e1649), $540-570bn together. Microsoft gives no calendar-2026 total in view: its January-March 2026 capex was $30.88bn, up 84% (e922), and its $678bn Azure backlog (e4405) supports a continued ramp, so I estimate about $175bn (F6), between a run-rate build of about $155bn and the roughly $175-190bn the outside tallies imply after the other three companies' guidance. Combined capital expenditure: $715-745bn (F7). Data-center part: almost all of this is servers, networking, buildings and leases for data centers, and outside sources describe the same totals as data-center spending (about $750bn, e1416, e4082; NVIDIA's nearly $800bn data-center figure for the top five, e2759 and e5376, of which these four are the bulk). I take a 95-100% share (F13, my estimate), giving $679-745bn, midpoint about $712bn (F14). The range reaches rung 3 only at its low end, which would need a non-data-center share no source indicates; the midpoint and most of the range lie in rung 4. Upward pressure: every company raised its guidance this year, memory costs keep rising (e4567, e4566), and independent trackers sit at $745-760bn (e1856, e2458); this keeps the outcome in the upper part of the build but not near $800bn, since the $800bn figures cover five companies. Downward pressure: about $130bn of U.S. data-center projects blocked or delayed so far in 2026 (e122) is a multi-year project value across all developers, not 2026 spending by these four, and the labour, grid and financing warnings (e1545, e5292, e2081, e4807) carry no number that would cut 2026 spending below $700bn. Repeats of the same claims (e4790, e1947, e2079 and the NVIDIA restatements) are counted once.\n\n judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22 S codex\nF1 combined (720, 745) | F2 combined 760 | F3 company-wide (195, 205) | F4 company-wide 220 | F5 company-wide (125, 145) | F6 company-wide 130 | F7 company-wide 30.88 | F8 company-wide 84 | F9 combined 730 | F10 combined 800 | F11 combined 745 | F12 combined 90 | F13 combined 670.5\nv6 I forecast $670.5 billion in CY2026 data-center capex, which falls in rung 3. The closest market benchmark by company coverage is Zacks's $720 billion to $745 billion estimate for all capex, numerically in rung 4. The company spending revisions and Microsoft's reported spending growth support $745 billion of total capex. Project delays and grid constraints temper the pace, and the additional AWS GPU deployment is scheduled after CY2026. Applying my 90% data-center share to the total puts the forecast below $700 billion. The spending mix is the biggest uncertainty near that cutoff.\n\n judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23 E claude\nF1 CPU 20 | F2 CPU 2 | F3 CPU 40 | F4 all vendors 50 | F5 CPU 1.8 | F6 CPU 36 | F7 Vera CPU 90 | F8 Vera CPU 32.4\nv41 Rung 3 (Neutral, $30-40 billion). I forecast about $32 billion of Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 (F8). Reference: NVIDIA's own outlook. CFO Colette Kress reaffirmed about $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and said FY2028 should more than double it (e3660). Huang and other outlets carry the same claim (e4764, e4691, e4856), counted once. That puts the reference above $40 billion (F3), at rung 2. No consensus figure is supplied. How I got the outcome: the FY2027 base is current-year guidance, so I take it in full (F1). The FY2028 doubling is a full-year target for the next fiscal year. All four selling quarters are still ahead, and it depends on a Vera ramp that the evidence does not yet show in volume: Anthropic is still evaluating Vera (e5044), and deployments are described in the future tense (e4689). So the target is discounted for time and execution rather than taken as the answer. Bounds on the growth pace: a vendor that only held share in a server-CPU market growing about 50% a year (AMD, e4687; BofA's fivefold growth from 2026 to 2030 is a similar pace, e4857) would grow about 1.5x, giving $30 billion. The guidance floor is 2.0x, giving $40 billion. Broad deployment claims (e4689) and Arm's 45% revenue share of data-center servers (e4692) support gaining share above the market pace. AMD's benchmark claims that its EPYC parts beat Vera by 1.2x per core up to 3.4x per modeled rack (e5399-e5404; a competitor's own tests, weighed lightly as one signal) argue against taking the guidance in full. I forecast 1.8x (F5), which gives $36 billion of total CPU revenue (F6). The question asks only about Vera. Management attributes the doubling to Vera (e4691, e4856), so I put Vera at 90% of FY2028 CPU revenue (F7). The rest is prior-generation CPUs in systems still shipping early in the year. That gives $32.4 billion. Sensitivity: rung 3 holds for any Vera share from about 84% to 100% at this pace. Reaching rung 2 needs the full guided 2x pace with nearly all CPU revenue being Vera. Falling to rung 4 needs a pace below about 1.67x at a 90% share. Scope note: two outlets describe the guidance as total CPU revenue (e3660, e4764) and two as Vera CPU revenue (e4691, e4856); the share figure covers that gap. Themes: our capex judgements put hyperscaler data-center spending at about $750 billion in 2026 (v6) and above $1.1 trillion in 2027 (v7, updated upward). That supports demand and keeps the pace well above the market's own growth, but on its own it does not supply the share gain that a pace above 2x needs.\n\n judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23 E codex\nF1 CPU 20 | F2 Vera CPU 2 | F3 Vera CPU 40 | F4 Vera CPU 90 | F5 Vera CPU 36\nv41 Management's doubling outlook gives a $40 billion reference at the lower edge of rung 2. The Vera-specific reporting ties that outlook to the $20 billion FY2027 CPU baseline. Separate reports of potential to more than double describe the same outlook and leave the upside unsized. NVIDIA expects broad deployment, and rising hyperscaler spending supports demand. Anthropic is still evaluating Vera, while AMD's own benchmarks show a credible alternative for agentic workloads. Those benchmarks indicate competitive risk without quantifying lost sales. I expect most of the planned ramp to land, with a modest shortfall from deployment timing and customer choice. Realizing 90% of the reference puts my FY2028 forecast at $36 billion, within rung 3.\n\n judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23 P-v8 claude\nF1 Data Center 75.25 | F2 Data Center 89.0 | F3 company 108.0 | F4 company revenue 1.045 | F5 company 112.86 | F6 company, sequential from FY2027 Q3 12 | F7 company 126.4 | F8 company 239.26 | F9 Data Center share of company revenue 92.5 | F10 Data Center 221.32 | F11 Data Center 164.25 | F12 Data Center 385.57 | F13 Data Center 361 | F14 company 177.8 | F15 company 417.06 | F16 company 411\nv8 Reference: the only outside forecast of this exact quantity is Morningstar's $361bn for FY2027 Data Center revenue (e2295, F13), which sits in rung 2 ($350bn to below $400bn); it predates the Q2 report and the Q3 guidance. Derivation: H1 is reported, $75.25bn in Q1 (e109) plus $89.0bn in Q2 (e2650), $164.25bn (F11; the $164bn in e5309 agrees). Q3 company revenue is guided at $108.0bn plus or minus 2% (e2652, e2720); NVIDIA has beaten the high end of guidance for 12 straight quarters (e5054, e5055) and beat the Q2 midpoint by $5.2bn (e3200), so I forecast Q3 at 4.5% above the midpoint (F4), $112.86bn (F5). For Q4 I forecast 12% sequential growth (F6), the pace the Q3 guide implies, held because management expects hyperscale growth to reaccelerate in Q4 as Vera Rubin supply builds (e3406, e5061) and supply commitments more than doubled to $279bn (e2759), and not raised because supply covers only about 70% of demand and stays constrained through FY2028 (e3733, e5067, e3115): $126.40bn (F7). Data Center has taken a rising share of revenue for five quarters, reaching 92.5% in Q2 (e3399, e5116); holding 92.5% for H2 (F9) gives H2 Data Center revenue of $221.32bn (F10) and FY2027 Data Center revenue of $385.57bn (F12), inside rung 2. The implied company total, $417.06bn (F15), sits close to the $411bn analyst consensus (F16, e4333), so the build is in line with where the market stands on the whole company. Boundaries: rung 1 would need Q4 revenue to jump far faster than any recent quarter, or a clearly higher Data Center share, which the supply limits make unlikely this fiscal year; rung 3 would need H2 revenue to fall back toward H1's level, which the Q3 guidance and the expected Q4 reacceleration contradict. Upside pressure: SemiAnalysis's call that H2 Data Center revenue beats analyst estimates by 20% (e1135) and possible H200 sales to China late in the year (theme v9 judges authorization more likely than not; the Q3 guide assumes no China data-center compute revenue, e2681, and my Q4 pace is not built on a zero-China assumption) push toward rung 1, but the first states no base and predates the Q3 guide, and neither is enough on its own to cross $400bn given supply. Not used: the 70% growth reported as full-year guidance (e3280, e4628) is FY2028 growth in the CFO's own words (e2691), and read as FY2027 growth it would imply Q4 revenue far below Q3; the just-under-$330bn run rate (e5310) annualises H1 flat; the $83.7bn Q2 Data Center figure (e4907) conflicts with NVIDIA's reported $89.0bn. AMD and custom-chip commitments (e1040, e1041, e4060) mostly deliver after FY2027 and do not bind a supply-limited NVIDIA this fiscal year.\n\n judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23 P-v8 codex\nF1 Data Center 361 | F2 Data Center 75.25 | F3 Data Center 89.02 | F4 Data Center 164.27 | F5 company 108 | F6 company 113.6 | F7 company 112 | F8 Data Center 92.5 | F9 Data Center 92.5 | F10 Data Center 103.6 | F11 company 12 | F12 company 13 | F13 company 126.56 | F14 Data Center 93 | F15 Data Center 117.7008 | F16 Data Center 385.5708\nv8 I forecast $385.6 billion in FY2027 Data Center revenue, within rung 2 ($350 billion to below $400 billion). Morningstar's $361 billion segment estimate is the direct outside reference and also sits in rung 2. NVIDIA reported $164.27 billion in the first half. I forecast $103.6 billion for Q3 from $112 billion of company revenue at a 92.5% Data Center share. The prior guidance beat and live Rubin deployment support a beat this quarter, with memory and deployment constraints limiting its size. For Q4, management's forecast of faster hyperscaler growth as Rubin supply expands supports 13% sequential company growth. A 93% Data Center share of $126.56 billion gives $117.7 billion for the segment. SemiAnalysis also points to second-half upside, but the passage gives no dollar baseline for its estimate. The stronger hyperscaler spending outlook supports near-term orders, with calendar 2027 spending bearing mainly on the end of this fiscal year. Limited H200 authorization adds a small amount of Q4 upside. The other export-policy themes leave current restrictions in place for this forecast. Long lead times and the timing of customer deployments keep the annual forecast below $400 billion.\n\n judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23 S claude\nF1 total (195, 205) | F2 total (125, 145) | F3 total 220 | F4 combined (540, 570) | F5 total 30.88 | F6 year-over-year change 84 | F7 Azure cloud platform 678 | F8 combined (720, 745) | F9 implied by the four-company tally less the three guided companies (150, 205) | F10 total 165 | F11 combined 720 | F12 combined 730 | F13 total 130 | F14 combined 98 | F15 combined 705.6 | F16 combined 745 | F17 combined 760 | F18 combined 750 | F19 combined 800 | F20 combined 795\nv6 Reference: outside tallies put the four companies' combined 2026 capital spending at roughly $720-745bn (Zacks, e5014; about $745bn tracking, e1856), inside rung 4 ($700-800bn). Build: the three companies with full-year guidance add to $540-570bn (F4): Alphabet $195-205bn after raising from $180-190bn (e1254), Meta $125-145bn after a raise (e1549), Amazon $220bn after raising from $200bn (e1649). Microsoft gives no calendar-year guidance in the evidence; its January-March 2026 quarter was $30.88bn, up 84% (e922), and a continued ramp of about 16% a quarter gives about $156bn, while the tally less the other three implies $150-205bn; I use $165bn (F10, my estimate). Total capital expenditure is therefore about $720bn (F11; $705-735bn across the guidance ranges). The question asks for data-center spending, which is narrower than total capex, so I apply a 98% data-center share (F14, my estimate): the companies indicated more than $730bn of AI-infrastructure spending (e1947), at or above the total tally; Meta's $130bn of AI capex (e3686) sits inside its $125-145bn guidance; Amazon's raise was mostly memory chips; outside estimates put spending on data centers near $750bn (e1416, e4082). The share is bounded at about 96-100% by these passages, giving data-center capex of about $706bn (F15), rung 4; the supported floor from e1947 (more than $730bn of AI infrastructure) also lies in rung 4. Rung 5 is not supported: the $795-800bn figures (NVIDIA, e2759; BofA, e5055) cover five companies, Oracle among them in BofA's list, so the four sit below $800bn, and the highest four-company tally is $760bn (e2458). Pushing up: three mid-year guidance raises, rising memory costs that lift the dollar cost of the same build (e1649), and strong cloud demand (Azure backlog $678bn, e4405; Google Cloud +82%, e4406; AWS's best quarter in four years, e4404). Pushing down: about $130bn of US data-center projects blocked or delayed this year (e122, not specific to these four), and grid-connection and electrician constraints (e5292, e1545); the memory shortage (e4566) limits volume but raises price, so it cuts both ways. The forecast sits close to the $700bn edge, so the main risk is rung 3 if Microsoft's ramp or the data-center share comes in lower; the repeated raises make an undershoot of guidance less likely. The many reprints of NVIDIA's top-five figure count as one claim (e4564's '$1.3 billion' for 2027 is a misprint of $1.3 trillion).\n\n judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23 S codex\nF1 combined 730 | F2 combined total (720, 745) | F3 combined total 745 | F4 combined total 760 | F5 company total (195, 205) | F6 company total 220 | F7 company total (125, 145) | F8 company total 130 | F9 company total 30.88 | F10 combined 800 | F11 combined total 745 | F12 combined outside data centers 25 | F13 combined 720\nv6 I forecast $720 billion of calendar 2026 data-center capex, in rung 4. The closest market reference, more than $730 billion of AI infrastructure spending for the same four companies, points to rung 4 and uses a broader spending label. Raised company outlooks, Microsoft's reported spending and strong cloud demand support $745 billion of total capex. After my $25 billion deduction for spending outside data centers, the forecast stays above $700 billion. Grid constraints and delayed projects put the lower boundary at risk, but the delay tally covers U.S. projects across developers and gives no amount attributable to these four companies' 2026 capex. NVIDIA's nearly $800 billion estimate covers five companies and serves as a cross-check. The AWS GPU expansion is scheduled for 2027–2028 and gives no amount to add to 2026.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'D=poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22; for f in $D/{reader-key,reread-key,reread2-key,TALLY,REREAD-TALLY,REREAD2-TALLY}.json; do echo $f; rg -n '\"'T10|T15|R01|S +|E +|S/answers/claude|E/answers/claude|S/answers/codex' \"'$f; done; cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && nl -ba blind4.py | sed -n '\"'225,249p' && nl -ba SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '368,385p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'D=poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22; for f in $D/{reader-key,reread-key,reread2-key,TALLY,REREAD-TALLY,REREAD2-TALLY}.json; do echo $f; rg -n '\"'T10|T15|R01|S +|E +|S/answers/claude|E/answers/claude|S/answers/codex' \"'$f; done; cd docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers && nl -ba blind4.py | sed -n '\"'225,249p' && nl -ba SMALL-DESIGN.md | sed -n '368,385p'\"","aggregated_output":"poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reader-key.json\n2: \"R01\": {\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-key.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread2-key.json\n32:  \"answer\": \"S/answers/codex.json\"\n50: \"T10\": {\n53:  \"answer\": \"S/answers/claude.json\"\n77: \"T15\": {\n80:  \"answer\": \"E/answers/claude.json\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/TALLY.json\n4:   \"packet\": \"R01\",\n386:    \"R01\",\n398:    \"R01\",\n408:    \"R01\",\n422:    \"R01\",\n487:   \"R01 S/claude v6\": \"yes\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD-TALLY.json\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json\n80:   \"T10\",\n144:  \"T10 S/claude v6\": [\n169:  \"T15 E/claude v41\": [\n   225\t                         f'{\"ok\" if ok else \"MISS\"}')\n   226\t    stops = [d for d in settled_defects if d[2] not in EXPECTED]\n   227\t    refused = [k for k, v in checked.items() if v.get('decision') != 'publish']\n   228\t    if stops or refused:\n   229\t        verdict = 'fail'\n   230\t    result = {'verdict': verdict, 'lines': lines, 'settled_defects': settled_defects, 'split_defects': split_defects,\n   231\t              'refused': refused}\n   232\t    (out / 'TALLY.json').write_text(json.dumps(result, indent=1, ensure_ascii=False))\n   233\t    print('\\n'.join(lines))\n   234\t    print('settled defects:', settled_defects)\n   235\t    print('split defects (disclosed):', split_defects)\n   236\t    print('refused first replies:', refused)\n   237\t    print('VERDICT:', verdict)\n   238\t\n   239\t\n   240\t_STEP2_D745 = READER_BRIEF[READER_BRIEF.index('2. For each member answered as a forecast'):READER_BRIEF.index('Return JSON:')]\n   241\tREREAD_BRIEF = READER_BRIEF.replace('targeted test, D745', 're-read under the comparison ruling, D747').replace(_STEP2_D745, (\n   242\t    '2. For each member answered as a forecast, name the evidence for the assumption that decides its band, and say whether the\\n'\n   243\t    '   passages support its size or a bound that justifies the rung, or, where the passages do not give the asked quantity, whether\\n'\n   244\t    '   the answer explains a related figure as the definitions below require. For each member answered unresolved, name the gap that\\n'\n   245\t    '   remains after the strongest relevant passages, and say whether the passages indeed cannot settle the rung.\\n\\n'))\n   246\tif 'D747' not in REREAD_BRIEF or 'An explained related figure satisfies this requirement' not in ' '.join(REREAD_BRIEF.split()):\n   247\t    raise SystemExit('the D747 re-read brief does not hold the ruling')\n   248\tBLIND3_REREAD2 = {'T10': ('S', 'claude'), 'T15': ('E', 'claude')}                 # SMALL-DESIGN.md §5 [D747]\n   249\tREREAD_FROM_BLIND4 = ('U01', 'U02', 'U03', 'U04', 'U05', 'U06', 'U08')\n   368\t- **Controls**, each checked by the code before use:\n   369\t\n   370\t  | Control | Built from | Expected reading |\n   371\t  |---|---|---|\n   372\t  | K-unsupported | the saved third-run E Codex reply, with an unsized 15% cut | not supported |\n   373\t  | K-unresolved-unjustified | P-v8 Claude made unresolved with a vague reason | not supported |\n   374\t  | K-unresolved-justified | S made unresolved with the specific gap | supported |\n   375\t- **Pass:**\n   376\t  - every S and E reply has a settled \"yes\" on its forecast or on its reason for leaving the answer unresolved;\n   377\t  - both P-v8 replies have a settled \"yes\" on a forecast;\n   378\t  - all three controls read as expected;\n   379\t  - the refusal and defect stop lines hold as before.\n   380\t- **Otherwise:**\n   381\t  - a support split that adjudication does not settle leaves the run inconclusive, and it goes back to council;\n   382\t  - any failure goes back to council;\n   383\t  - a clean result supports asking Alex to authorize the 36-request paid re-send.\n   384\t\n   385\t**[D747] The re-read under Alex's comparison ruling (Alex: \"可以发布，要讲清楚\"; council rounds 61–61b), registered before any packet is\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'run=poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22; for f in \"$run\"/{TALLY,REREAD-TALLY,REREAD2-TALLY}.json; do echo $f; rg -n '\"'S/(claude|codex)|E/claude' \\\"\"'$f\" -A5 | tail -33; done; for f in \"$run\"/{S,E}/answers/claude.json; do echo $f; rg -n '\"'\\\"basis_text\\\": \\\"|\\\"reasons\\\"' \\\"\"'$f\" | cut -c1-510; done; nl -ba \"$run\"/E/user.txt | rg '\"'e3660 |e4687 |e4857 |e4689 |e5044 |e4691 |e5400 '; nl -ba \\\"\"'$run\"/S/user.txt | rg '\"'e1416 |e4082 |e1947 |e5376 |e5014 '; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/CHECKED.json | head -75\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'run=poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22; for f in \"$run\"/{TALLY,REREAD-TALLY,REREAD2-TALLY}.json; do echo $f; rg -n '\"'S/(claude|codex)|E/claude' \\\"\"'$f\" -A5 | tail -33; done; for f in \"$run\"/{S,E}/answers/claude.json; do echo $f; rg -n '\"'\\\"basis_text\\\": \\\"|\\\"reasons\\\"' \\\"\"'$f\" | cut -c1-510; done; nl -ba \"$run\"/E/user.txt | rg '\"'e3660 |e4687 |e4857 |e4689 |e5044 |e4691 |e5400 '; nl -ba \\\"\"'$run\"/S/user.txt | rg '\"'e1416 |e4082 |e1947 |e5376 |e5014 '; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/CHECKED.json | head -75\"","aggregated_output":"poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/TALLY.json\n487:   \"R01 S/claude v6\": \"yes\",\n488-   \"R02 P/codex v4\": \"yes\",\n489-   \"R02 P/codex v8\": \"yes\",\n490-   \"R03 E/codex v41\": \"yes\",\n491-   \"R04 O/codex v29\": \"yes\",\n492-   \"R05 P-v4/codex v4\": \"yes\",\n--\n494:   \"R07 S/codex v6\": \"no\",\n495-   \"R08 P-v8/codex v8\": \"yes\",\n496-   \"R09 P-v4/claude v4\": \"yes\",\n497-   \"R10 P-v8/claude v8\": \"yes\",\n498-   \"R11 P/claude v4\": \"yes\",\n499-   \"R11 P/claude v8\": \"yes\",\n500:   \"R12 E/claude v41\": \"yes\"\n501-  }\n502- }\n503-}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD-TALLY.json\n282:   \"Q07 S/claude v6\": [\n283-    \"split\",\n284-    [\n285-     \"no\",\n286-     \"yes\",\n287-     \"no\",\n--\n291:   \"Q08 S/codex v6\": [\n292-    \"yes\",\n293-    [\n294-     \"yes\",\n295-     \"yes\"\n296-    ]\n--\n337:   \"Q14 E/claude v41\": [\n338-    \"yes\",\n339-    [\n340-     \"yes\",\n341-     \"yes\",\n342-     \"yes\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json\n123:  \"T06 S/codex v6\": [\n124-   \"no\",\n125-   [\n126-    \"no\",\n127-    \"no\"\n128-   ]\n--\n144:  \"T10 S/claude v6\": [\n145-   \"split\",\n146-   [\n147-    \"no\",\n148-    \"yes\",\n149-    \"no\",\n--\n169:  \"T15 E/claude v41\": [\n170-   \"yes\",\n171-   [\n172-    \"yes\",\n173-    \"yes\"\n174-   ]\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/answers/claude.json\n112:      \"basis_text\": \"My estimate. Microsoft states no calendar-2026 total in the evidence. Its January-March 2026 quarter was $30.88bn, up 84% year over year, and its $678bn Azure backlog supports a continuing ramp: carrying the growth pace through the year at roughly 15% sequential increases a quarter gives about $155bn on the reported quarter's basis (not the flat annualised $124bn). The outside tallies of the four companies' guided 2026 spending ($720-760bn) less the other three companies' guidance\n245:      \"basis_text\": \"My estimate. Nearly all of these companies' capital expenditure is servers, chips, networking, buildings and leases for data centers (Amazon's latest increase was mostly memory chip costs; Zacks says a large portion goes to AI infrastructure), and outside sources describe the same roughly $750-800bn totals as data-center spending. A small remainder (offices, logistics and other assets) is not data-center spending, so the share is taken at 95-100%; no company reports a separate dat\n276:      \"reasons\": \"Rung 4 (at least $700bn and below $800bn). Reference: outside tallies of the four companies' own 2026 guidance put their combined capital expenditure at roughly $720-745bn (F8, e5014), which sits in rung 4. Build: Alphabet guides $195-205bn (raised from $180-190bn, e1254), Meta $125-145bn (raised, e1549) and Amazon $220bn (raised from $200bn, mostly on memory costs, e1649), $540-570bn together. Microsoft gives no calendar-2026 total in view: its January-March 2026 capex was $30.88bn,\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/claude.json\n131:      \"basis_text\": \"My estimate of FY2028 Vera CPU revenue growth on the FY2027 base. Management's 'more than double' (F2, at least 100% growth) is the benchmark, but it is a target for a fiscal year that has not started, for a product line still ramping, so it is discounted for time and execution rather than taken in full. Outside views put CPU market growth near 50% a year (AMD, F4; Bank of America's fivefold path from F5 to F6), so a doubling needs large share gains. Broad announced adoption (ever\n162:      \"reasons\": \"Reference: NVIDIA's own guidance. CFO Colette Kress reaffirmed about $20 billion of CPU revenue for FY2027 (F1, e3660) and said FY2028 would more than double it (F2, e4856); NVIDIA's wording ties both the base and the doubling to Vera CPU revenue (e4691, e4856). That gives more than $40 billion (F3), the bottom edge of rung 2 (Bullish, $40-50 billion). Forecast: this is a target for a full fiscal year that has not yet started, for a product line still ramping, so it is discounted for\n    10\t- e3660 [party_statement; Bram Berkowitz, fool.com, reporting Colette Kress's statement; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:fd25a8ea6f60290264bfbe55 2026-08-30] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA still expects $20 billion of CPU revenue in FY2027 and expects FY2028 CPU revenue to more than double that amount.\n    13\t- e4687 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source AMD ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] AMD estimates that server CPU market revenue could grow at a 50% annual rate through 2030, reaching a $220 billion opportunity by the end of the decade.\n    16\t- e4689 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst), quoting Colette Kress; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said Vera CPU deployments would include every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM.\n    19\t- e4691 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3c5c71e12ed2a7001ce14f1a 2026-09-10] NVIDIA expects Vera CPU revenue in FY2028 to be double its FY2027 level.\n    30\t- e4857 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source Bank of America analysts ; from gnews:c70bc12967b9b53426b5fb10 2026-09-12] Bank of America analysts forecast that the CPU market would grow fivefold, from $35 billion in 2026 to $170 billion in 2030.\n    33\t- e5044 [party_statement; Insider Monkey; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a1af1bb8840fa4ec9e52bcb0 2026-09-15] NVIDIA said Anthropic is evaluating NVIDIA’s Vera CPU for CPU-intensive agentic workloads.\n    37\t- e5400 [party_statement; wccftech.com; original source AMD ; from gnews:83ffa482f76b03da59ac2674 2026-09-19] AMD reported that its EPYC 9996 delivered 1.2 times Vera-based platform per-core performance and 2.24 times its platform-level performance in SPECrate 2026 Integer testing.\n    17\t- e1416 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-29; from gnews:d67077a64d61e214e5d9ac06 2026-07-29] The author estimates that tech giants will spend $750 billion on data centers in calendar 2026 and says their spending could increase in calendar 2027.\n    29\t- e1947 [party_statement; fool.com (Patrick Sanders); original source Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet ; from gnews:c8ac167e2356a75994ebc26f 2026-08-13] Amazon, Meta Platforms, Microsoft, and Alphabet indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure in calendar 2026.\n    47\t- e4082 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) ; from gnews:15f1ea8f70a1fc52417f55b5 2026-09-03] The article cites estimates of about $750 billion in AI data-center infrastructure spending in calendar 2026 and more than $1 trillion in calendar 2027.\n    83\t- e5014 [third_party_view; Zacks.com; original source Zacks.com 2026-09-14; from gnews:1d3bc1ba4c2df5e998884bdb 2026-09-14] Zacks estimated that Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta would spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026, with a large portion directed to AI infrastructure.\n   128\t- e5376 [party_statement; fool.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4ee5d8f73b7bcfc2f2111ad1 2026-09-19] NVIDIA forecasts that the five largest AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n     1\t[\n     2\t {\n     3\t  \"case\": \"S\",\n     4\t  \"writer\": \"claude\",\n     5\t  \"sha256\": \"d81bc24f57330d34e985b9e4069fd84d8e231b8f981bb02ac291de8b61bfc221\",\n     6\t  \"stage\": \"checked\",\n     7\t  \"errors\": [],\n     8\t  \"recorded\": [\n     9\t   {\n    10\t    \"type\": \"conversion between quantities\",\n    11\t    \"figure\": \"F4\"\n    12\t   },\n    13\t   {\n    14\t    \"type\": \"qualified source\",\n    15\t    \"figure\": \"F8\",\n    16\t    \"qualifier\": \"roughly\"\n    17\t   },\n    18\t   {\n    19\t    \"type\": \"conversion between quantities\",\n    20\t    \"figure\": \"F9\"\n    21\t   },\n    22\t   {\n    23\t    \"type\": \"estimate\",\n    24\t    \"figure\": \"F10\",\n    25\t    \"assumption\": \"My estimate of Microsoft's calendar-2026 capital expenditure; no calendar-year guidance is shown. The January-March 2026 quarter (fiscal Q3) was $30.88bn, up 84% from a year earlier (F5, F6). A continued sequential rise of about 16% a quarter, the pace that compounds to roughly 84% over a year and is supported by the $678bn Azure backlog (F7), gives about 30.9 + 35.8 + 41.6 + 48.2 = $156bn; the four-company tally less the three guided companies implies $150-205bn (F9). $165bn follows the run-rate path and moves partway into the tally-implied range, staying in its lower part because the run-rate is the only Microsoft-specific number.\"\n    26\t   },\n    27\t   {\n    28\t    \"type\": \"conversion between quantities\",\n    29\t    \"figure\": \"F11\"\n    30\t   },\n    31\t   {\n    32\t    \"type\": \"qualified source\",\n    33\t    \"figure\": \"F12\",\n    34\t    \"qualifier\": \"more than\"\n    35\t   },\n    36\t   {\n    37\t    \"type\": \"estimate\",\n    38\t    \"figure\": \"F14\",\n    39\t    \"assumption\": \"My estimate of the share of the four companies' 2026 capital expenditure that is data-center (AI infrastructure) spending. The companies' indicated AI-infrastructure total, more than $730bn (F12), is at or above the total-capex tally of $720-745bn (F8), which puts the share near 100%; Meta's planned AI capex of $130bn (F13) sits inside its $125-145bn total guidance (F2), about 96% of the midpoint; Amazon's raise to $220bn was mostly memory-chip costs, i.e. server hardware (e1649); outside estimates put spending on data centers at about $750bn (e1416, e4082). No passage sizes non-data-center spending such as offices or logistics, so I keep a 2% residual; the passages bound the share between about 96% and 100%.\"\n    40\t   },\n    41\t   {\n    42\t    \"type\": \"qualified source\",\n    43\t    \"figure\": \"F16\",\n    44\t    \"qualifier\": \"near\"\n    45\t   },\n    46\t   {\n    47\t    \"type\": \"qualified source\",\n    48\t    \"figure\": \"F18\",\n    49\t    \"qualifier\": \"estimated\"\n    50\t   },\n    51\t   {\n    52\t    \"type\": \"qualified source\",\n    53\t    \"figure\": \"F19\",\n    54\t    \"qualifier\": \"nearly\"\n    55\t   },\n    56\t   {\n    57\t    \"type\": \"qualified source\",\n    58\t    \"figure\": \"F20\",\n    59\t    \"qualifier\": \"roughly\"\n    60\t   }\n    61\t  ],\n    62\t  \"published\": {\n    63\t   \"v6\": [\n    64\t    690.9,\n    65\t    720.3,\n    66\t    null\n    67\t   ]\n    68\t  },\n    69\t  \"decision\": \"publish\",\n    70\t  \"shapes\": {\n    71\t   \"v6\": \"forecast\"\n    72\t  }\n    73\t },\n    74\t {\n    75\t  \"case\": \"S\",\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"agent_message","text":"W07’s failure holds even with the code’s full range. Its highest calculated data-center figure is $720.3B, below the answer’s claimed floor of more than $730B. I found one clear instance in the 18 original replies. I’m checking what the earlier votes establish about the three disagreements."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23 && for f in reread-packets/W0{1,2,4,5,8,9}.*json; do echo $f; cut -c1-1050 \"$f\"; done; nl -ba reread-packets/W06.md | head -68; nl -ba adjudication/U02.codex.json; nl -ba adjudication/U02.claude.json'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cd poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23 && for f in reread-packets/W0{1,2,4,5,8,9}.*json; do echo $f; cut -c1-1050 \"$f\"; done; nl -ba reread-packets/W06.md | head -68; nl -ba adjudication/U02.codex.json; nl -ba adjudication/U02.claude.json'","aggregated_output":"reread-packets/W01.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v8\": {\"supported\": \"no\",\n  \"ground\": \"Unresolved with the stated reason 'The outlook for the second half is uncertain.' Strongest relevant passages: reported H1 FY2027 Data Center revenue $75.25bn + $89.0bn = $164.25bn (e109, e2650; e5309 $164bn); Q3 guidance $108.0bn plus or minus 2% (e2652), i.e. $105.84-110.16bn (e3346); Data Center mix 92.5% and rising (e3399), giving Q3 Data Center about $99.9bn (e3401); hyperscale growth reaccelerating in Q4 as Vera Rubin supply builds (e3406, e5061); supply about 70% of demand and constrained through FY2028 (e3733, e5067).\",\n  \"why_not\": \"The reason names no gap; it is a statement of confidence, and the passages do settle the rung. H1 plus a guided Q3 puts FY2027 Data Center at about $262-266bn through Q3. Rung 3 or lower (< $350bn) would need Q4 Data Center below about $86bn: below Q2's reported $89.0bn and about 14% under the Q3 level. No passage points to a fall, and management says Q4 reaccelerates. Rung 1 (>= $400bn) would need Q4 Data Center of about $136bn, roughly 36% sequential growth. Even with a beat to about $113.6bn total (e5056), Q4 would need about 24% growth, against 18% delivered in Q2, 12% guided for Q3 and a binding supply limit (e3733, e5067). The answer's own calculation reaches $387.35bn (F18, rung 2), and the outside figures agree: Morningstar $361bn (e2295), and consensus total $411bn (e4333) at about 92.5% mix is about $380bn. The answer does not cite the outlets that read the 70% growth as FY2027 (e3280, e3890, e4628). That reading would imply a Q4 total of about $81bn after H1's $177.82bn and the $108bn Q3 guidance, which contra\n \"defects\": []}\nreread-packets/W01.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v8\": {\n      \"supported\": \"no\",\n      \"ground\": \"NVIDIA reported $164.25 billion in first-half Data Center revenue (e109, e2650). Its $108 billion Q3 company guidance and history of beating guidance support the answer’s $113.4 billion Q3 forecast (e2652, e5054, e5055). The answer assumes 12% growth in Q4 and a 92.8% second-half Data Center share, citing the guided growth pace, expected Q4 hyperscale acceleration and recent segment shares (e5005, e3406, e3399). Those assumptions yield $387.35 billion for the year, in rung 2. Morningstar’s $361 billion estimate for the same segment and year also falls in rung 2 (e2295).\",\n      \"why_not\": \"The exact Q4 growth rate and second-half segment share remain uncertain. The answer explains its estimates for both, then gives no reason that the uncertainty makes another band as likely as rung 2.\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": []\n}reread-packets/W02.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v41\": {\"supported\": \"no\", \"ground\": \"F5, the writer's own estimate of 1.8x FY2028 growth over the FY2027 base of $20 billion (F1, e3660). It discounts the reaffirmed 'more than double' (F2; e3660, e4764, e4691, e4856), using AMD's 50% server-CPU market pace as the lower anchor (F4, e4687; e4857) and the 2.0x guidance floor as the upper anchor. F7 then takes an unsized 90% Vera share of total CPU revenue. Outcome F8 = 20 x 1.8 x 0.9 = $32.4 billion, in rung 3.\", \"why_not\": \"The reference sits at rung 2: management says FY2028 will 'more than double' $20 billion, which is above $40 billion. The forecast drops below the $40 billion line only by assuming NVIDIA misses its own reaffirmed floor (1.8x) and by taking a further 10% deduction. No passage gives a size for that shortfall or a bound that keeps the pace under 2.0x. The negatives the answer cites carry no number for a miss. Anthropic is evaluating Vera (e5044), which is pipeline and not a cut. The deployment claim (e4689) is future tense but is itself a bullish stateme\nreread-packets/W02.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v41\": {\n      \"supported\": \"no\",\n      \"ground\": \"F5 uses e3660 and e4687 to forecast 1.8× growth, giving $36 billion of FY2028 NVIDIA CPU revenue. F7 assigns Vera 90% of that total, citing e4691, e4856 and e4689. That gives $32.4 billion in rung 3.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"At $36 billion total, Vera reaches the $30 billion cutoff only if other CPUs contribute $6 billion or less. e4691 and e4856 describe Vera’s expected growth, and e4689 names the kinds of customers expected to deploy it. None explains why other CPU sales are likely to stay within $6 billion. Vera could account for most of NVIDIA’s CPU revenue and still fall below rung 3.\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": []\n}reread-packets/W04.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v41\": {\"supported\": \"no\", \"ground\": \"F4: the writer's estimate that NVIDIA realizes 90% of the $40 billion doubling reference (F3 = F1 $20 billion FY2027 CPU revenue from e3660 x F2 'double' from e4691), resting on e4689, e5044 and e5403; F5 = $36 billion, rung 3.\", \"why_not\": \"The reference sits exactly at the $40 billion lower edge of rung 2, so the band is decided only by whether there is any shortfall against guidance; the 10% haircut is what moves the forecast into rung 3. No passage gives that shortfall's size or a bound for it. Three passages (e3660, e4764, e4856) say management expects CPU revenue to 'more than double', which points to $40 billion or more, not less. The answer calls that upside unsized but then cuts below plain doubling without explaining why the company's own guidance would be missed. The cited reasons for the cut do not size a loss: e5044 says Anthropic is evaluating Vera, which is a prospective customer, not a lost sale, delay or cancellation. e5403 is AMD's own benchmark claim with no sales e\nreread-packets/W04.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v41\": {\n      \"supported\": \"no\",\n      \"ground\": \"The $40 billion reference rests on e3660, e4691 and e4856. F4 assumes NVIDIA realizes 90% of it, putting FY2028 Vera CPU revenue at $36 billion.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"The deployment plans in e4689 give no order volumes. Anthropic’s evaluation in e5044 and AMD’s benchmark in e5403 identify sales risks but give no shipment delay or lost order. Calling the $4 billion shortfall less than half an average quarter restates its size. Nothing cited shows why revenue is likely to fall below $40 billion while remaining at or above $30 billion.\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": []\n}reread-packets/W05.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"Unresolved. The gap is that no passage gives the four companies' data-center share of their total capital expenditure, and the $700 billion line sits at about 94% of the total (700/745; about 92% of 760, 97% of 720). Total capex for the four is $720-745bn (e5014), near $745bn (e1856) or $760bn (e2458). The passages that call this spending 'AI infrastructure' or 'data center capex' do not settle the split. e1947 (more than $730bn on AI infrastructure) is the same claim as e2079's total capital spending, so it is the total under another label, and Zacks (e5014) says of that same $720-745bn only that 'a large portion' goes to AI infrastructure. e5376 (nearly $800bn data-center capex) covers five companies, the fifth is not named, and it restates NVIDIA's total-capex figure (e2759). Meta's $130bn AI capex (e3686) against its $125-145bn guidance (e1549) is 90-104%, which straddles the 94% line. So the passages cannot place the four-company data-center figure on either side of $700bn, and t\nreread-packets/W05.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v6\": {\n      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n      \"ground\": \"At the $745 billion total estimated in F11, the four companies reach $700 billion in data-center capex only if they spend about 94% of the total on data centers. F13 correctly computes $670.5 billion at the assumed 90% share. Neither e5014’s “large portion” nor Meta’s AI spending in e3686 establishes which share is more likely for all four. e1947 covers the right four companies but gives no data-center breakdown of their AI infrastructure spending. e5376 covers data-center capex but gives no separate amount for the fifth company. The $700 billion threshold therefore remains unresolved.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": []\n}reread-packets/W08.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"Total CY2026 capex for the same four companies of $745bn (F11), resting on the passage tallies e1856 (near $745bn), e5014/e2079 ($720-745bn), e2458 ($760bn) and e1947 (more than $730bn on AI infrastructure), less a stated $25bn non-data-center deduction (F12) sized from e1947's AI-infrastructure tally sitting close to total capex (implying roughly $15bn outside it) and Meta's $130bn AI capex (e3686) against the $135bn midpoint of its $125-145bn guidance (e1549). The answer explains why the related figure (total capex) applies: same four companies, same year, AI spending nearly all of capex, so a small deduction keeps the outcome ($720bn) above the $700bn line; e1947's more-than-$730bn for AI infrastructure alone also bounds data-center spending near or above the line.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\nreread-packets/W08.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v6\": {\n      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n      \"ground\": \"e1856 and e5014 support the $745 billion total-capex forecast. e1947 puts AI infrastructure spending above $730 billion for the same four companies in 2026, and Meta's $130 billion AI capex plan is close to the $135 billion midpoint of its total guidance (e3686, e1549). The answer uses those comparisons to favor a small deduction for spending outside data centers. Its estimated $25 billion deduction leaves $720 billion in rung 4. A deduction above $45 billion would put spending below $700 billion. The comparisons explain why the answer favors the smaller deduction, although they do not measure it directly.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": []\n}reread-packets/W09.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v41\": {\"supported\": \"no\", \"ground\": \"F4, the writer's own estimate that Vera realizes 85% of the $20 billion FY2027 CPU outlook (e3660), which gives F5 = $17 billion. F6 then doubles that base exactly (2x), where e4856/e4764 say 'more than double'. Together they give F7 = $34 billion, in rung 3.\", \"why_not\": \"The band is decided by the 15% cut in F4, and no passage gives the size of that cut or a bound for it. The passages give the company's FY2027 expectation of $20 billion (e3660, 'still expects') and FY2028 Vera CPU revenue of more than double that (e4856, e4691, e4764). That puts management's floor at $40 billion, the lower edge of rung 2. No passage splits the $20 billion into Vera and non-Vera CPU revenue, and none sizes an execution shortfall. The basis text justifies the 85% only by saying 'the evidence gives no sales volumes'. That is missing confirmation, not evidence of a smaller figure. The cited passages e4689 (broad deployment) and e5044 (Anthropic evaluating) point up, not down. The AMD benchmark claims (e\nreread-packets/W09.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v41\": {\n      \"supported\": \"no\",\n      \"ground\": \"F4 assumes Vera realizes 85% of the $20 billion FY2027 CPU outlook in e3660. That gives $17 billion, which F6 doubles to $34 billion using e4691 as its benchmark.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"At the assumed doubling pace, rung 3 requires FY2027 Vera revenue of at least $15 billion and less than $20 billion. Expected deployment across major customers (e4689) and Anthropic’s evaluation (e5044) give no purchase volumes or Vera revenue share. AMD’s benchmarks (e5400, e5403) give no estimate of lost sales. These passages explain why sales could fall short, but do not explain why a shortfall small enough to keep FY2028 revenue at or above $30 billion is most likely.\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"defects\": []\n}     1\t# Blind reading (judging-v13s blind test, re-read under the comparison ruling, D747)\n     2\t\n     3\tYou are given one forecasting request (the questions, their ladders, and every passage the writer was shown), the system text and the\n     4\tformat rules the writer was given, and one answer written in that format. You are not told who wrote the answer or whether any check\n     5\taccepted it. Read only what is given here.\n     6\t\n     7\tJudge in two steps:\n     8\t1. Check each source figure against its passage's quote and each computed figure against its inputs, using the format rules below.\n     9\t   What the format rules permit is not a defect.\n    10\t2. For each member answered as a forecast, name the evidence for the assumption that decides its band, and say whether the\n    11\t   passages support its size or a bound that justifies the rung, or, where the passages do not give the asked quantity, whether\n    12\t   the answer explains a related figure as the definitions below require. For each member answered unresolved, name the gap that\n    13\t   remains after the strongest relevant passages, and say whether the passages indeed cannot settle the rung.\n    14\t\n    15\tReturn JSON:\n    16\t{\"members\": {\"<view id>\": {\"supported\": \"yes|no\", \"ground\": \"<the passage ids or the stated assumption that put the forecast in its band>\",\n    17\t                           \"why_not\": \"<if no: what is missing or wrong>\"}},\n    18\t \"defects\": [{\"type\": \"<one of: number misread | wrong arithmetic | part above or added into its whole | two forecasts of one quarter |\n    19\t               own estimate passed off as the market's reference | broken structure>\", \"figures\": [\"<figure ids>\"], \"detail\": \"...\"}]}\n    20\t\n    21\tDefinitions (judge each against the passages and the format rules the writer was given, shown below; what the format rules permit is\n    22\tnot a defect):\n    23\t- supported: the forecast follows from the shown passages and the answer's stated assumptions; a reader could check it against them.\n    24\t  For a forecast, the assumption that decides the band must rest on the passages in one of three ways: its size, a bound that\n    25\t  justifies the rung, or, when the passages do not give the asked quantity, a related figure that the answer explains. An explained\n    26\t  related figure satisfies this requirement even though the other side of the line remains possible. It is explained when the\n    27\t  answer says why the comparison applies to the companies, the period and the quantity asked, and why, under the answer's own stated\n    28\t  assumptions, the chosen side of the line is more likely; an answer that does not explain this is not supported (a ruling). For an unresolved\n    29\t  member, supported means its reason holds: after the strongest relevant passages, a gap remains that keeps the rung unsettled (a\n    30\t  ruling).\n    31\t- number misread: a source figure's number, scale, sign or qualifier differs from what its passage's quote writes, allowing what the\n    32\t  format rules permit.\n    33\t- wrong arithmetic: a computed figure does not equal its operation on its inputs, within the tolerance the format rules state.\n    34\t- part above or added into its whole: a segment is larger than the total it belongs to, or is added again into a total that already\n    35\t  includes it (directly or through an intermediate figure or an estimate).\n    36\t- two forecasts of one quarter: two different figures are used as the value of one quantity and period. A growth rate computed from\n    37\t  guidance and used only as a stated benchmark for an estimate is not a second value, when one figure supplies the quarter in the\n    38\t  calculation (a ruling). Look through any rate or multiple, including inside a stated assumption, for a second dollar value of the\n    39\t  quarter.\n    40\t- own estimate passed off as the market's reference: a figure marked as the market's reference is, or rests on, the writer's own\n    41\t  estimate or forecast. A reference that applies a past period's reported share or multiple to another period (a quarter's mix\n    42\t  applied to the year) is our comparison estimate, not the market's (a ruling).\n    43\t- broken structure: a link that does not resolve, a figure that depends on itself, a missing or doubled answer, an outcome that is not\n    44\t  the question's quantity, or a rung whose band does not hold the outcome. For a range outcome, the format allows \"a band it overlaps,\n    45\t  with the reason in reasons\": such a rung is not broken structure; a band the range does not reach is. A total used for a narrower\n    46\t  quantity (total capital expenditure for data-center spending) without a stated deduction or a supported bound is an outcome not of\n    47\t  the question's quantity (a ruling).\n    48\tReturn an empty \"defects\" list when you find none. Return only JSON.\n    49\t\n    50\t# The system text the writer was given\n    51\tYou are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging the current rungs of a family of related number theses: one company's quantities that overlap, such as a total and its segment, a level and its growth, or a later year that rests on an earlier year's base. Use only the supplied evidence and themes in force. Each rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n    52\t\n    53\t- Judge every member from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently) and the themes in force, as if for the first time. Earlier forecasts are not shown: whether a conclusion changed is compared afterwards.\n    54\t- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate whose pace you forecast, informed by guidance, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted by default; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. Build the family's figures once and read every member's outcome from them: a part cannot exceed its whole, a figure that spans several periods or products gives a question only its share, and a quantity the family shares has one forecast. Compare each outcome with its rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n    55\t- Weigh by tier and by novelty. Only fact can establish that a rung's outcome already holds; \"not established\" is a statement of confidence, never a reason to stay. Guidance for the current or next quarter is the strongest forecast input. A management target for a product not yet shipping, or an ambition spanning years, is an input to be discounted for time and execution (how many selling quarters remain, what volume and price the figure implies, what the company's own choices say), not an answer; consensus is where the market stands, not the answer either. third_party_view and rumour move the forecast in proportion to their specificity (a number, a date, a named party): a research firm's shipment or revenue tracking with a number counts. A commitment or deal that is announced but not sized is sized from its disclosed comparable (the same programme's other deal, scaled by capacity or units); a figure no one states is derived from the accounting identities in the evidence when they allow it. A repeat, another outlet for the same claim, or an opinion that adds no new fact or calculation moves nothing. Missing confirmation alone is not grounds for the lowest or highest rung: an event not being confirmed is not confirmation that it did not happen.\n    56\t- An evidence item that replaces another includes its predecessor for context, such as guidance revised from 12% to 25% growth. The predecessor is no longer evidence in force.\n    57\t- \"Themes in force\" lists the library's open theme theses (policy stages, shared quantities) with their current conclusions. They are our own judgements, never evidence: do not cite them in cites. Weigh each one that bears on a member and report it in that member's theme_pushes with the direction it pushes that member (toward_first, toward_last or none); leave out a theme that does not bear on it. A theme marked \"updated since this thesis was last judged\" may change the conclusion; one that is not marked already stood at the previous judgement. When no themes are listed, theme_pushes is empty.\n    58\t- Your conclusions must be consistent with each other and with the themes in force as shown: a share cannot be judged at zero while the policy that gates it is judged likely to open.\n    59\t- Return the family's figures and one answer per member, as the JSON schema defines them. Every number an answer rests on is a figure; a number you judge rather than read or compute is an estimate, stated as yours. When the evidence cannot support a member's forecast, answer it unresolved and say why. Write explanations in English; preserve IDs and any quoted source text.\n    60\t\n    61\t\n    62\t# The format rules the writer was given (schema descriptions)\n    63\t{\n    64\t \"figures\": \"The family's calculation. Use one forecast figure for each quantity and period, as a point or a range, wherever that quantity is needed (outside figures beneath it, or a point a final range rests on, are fine). Guidance may inform a growth pace: record the pace you forecast as an estimate, say why it applies, name the benchmark in rests_on, and use that estimate in the calculation. A rate computed from a shared quantity's guidance or outside figure is never itself an input of the calculation. To turn a total into a narrower quantity (data-center spending out of total capital expenditure), write the deduction or share as its own figure, or give a supported bound; never relabel a total as the narrower quantity. Figures name each other by id; no figure may depend on itself.\",\n    65\t \"figures/id\": \"Short and unique, such as F1; never a passage id.\",\n    66\t \"figures/what\": \"What the number measures. Where the request lists keys (outcome keys, shared quantities), copy them exactly.\",\n    67\t \"figures/what/measure\": \"The quantity, such as revenue or capital expenditure; never its basis.\",\n    68\t \"figures/what/period\": \"For example FY2027, FY2027 Q3, FY2027 H1, FY2027 Q1-Q3, CY2026, end of FY2027.\",\n     1\t{\n     2\t  \"members\": {\n     3\t    \"v6\": {\n     4\t      \"supported\": \"no\",\n     5\t      \"why\": \"At $720 billion of total capex, rung 4 requires a data-center share of about 97.2%. The answer assumes 98%, but its own claimed lower bound of 96% gives $691.2 billion, in rung 3. The format requires the passages to support the share's size or give a bound that justifies the rung. e1947 says “more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure,” and e5014 says “a large portion directed toward AI infrastructure.” Neither passage quantifies data-center capex for the four companies. Meta's AI capex and Amazon's memory-driven spending increase do not establish the combined share. The $750 billion estimates do not identify the same four companies, and NVIDIA's nearly $800 billion data-center estimate covers five companies without breaking out the fifth. Microsoft's reported 84% year-over-year increase and Azure backlog also do not establish the assumed 16% quarterly spending growth.\"\n     6\t    }\n     7\t  },\n     8\t  \"defects\": []\n     9\t}     1\t{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"why\": \"Rung 4 ($700-800bn) is set by an assumption that rests on bounds in the passages, not on the precision of the point. (1) Four-company total: every tally for exactly these four is at or above $720bn: e5014 'roughly $720 billion to $745 billion', e1856 'tracking near $745 billion', e2458 '$760 billion in capex in 2026', e2079 'guided roughly $720 billion ... to $745 billion'. The answer's own total, $720bn (F11 = F4 $540-570bn guided + F10 $165bn), sits at the low end of these. Its Microsoft estimate falls inside the tally-implied $150-205bn (F9), and it follows the system text's instruction to forecast a run-rate pace (16% a quarter compounds to the reported 84%, e922) rather than annualise one quarter flat. (2) Data-center share: F14 is written as its own figure, as the format requires ('write the deduction or share as its own figure, or give a supported bound'). The passages put the share near 100%. e1947 names the same four companies and the same year: 'have indicated they will spend more than $730 billion combined on AI infrastructure this year', which is at or above their total-capex tally. e5376 and e4766 call NVIDIA's top-five figure 'data center capital expenditures' ('nearly $800 billion'), the same figure e2759 calls 'CapEx by the top 5 hyperscalers'. So the passages treat hyperscaler capex as data-center spending, and any share from 97.2% to 100% on $720bn gives rung 4. The answer also states e1947's floor directly ('the supported floor from e1947 ... also lies in rung 4'), and >$730bn is inside rung 4. e5014's 'a large portion directed toward AI infrastructure' does not cap the data-center share, because non-AI cloud spending in data centers is still data-center capex. (3) Ceiling: the near-$800bn figures cover five companies (e5055 lists Oracle), so the four sit below $800bn, and the highest four-company tally is $760bn. Reader 1 is right that the 2% residual has no passage behind its size, and that the answer's stated 96% lower share gives $691.2bn. But the 96% comes from Meta's AI-only capex (e3686), which is narrower than data-center capex. The rung rests on the passage bounds above, which a reader can check, so the forecast is supported.\"}}, \"defects\": []}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'A=poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22; B=poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23; for f in $A/reread2-packets/T{10,15}.*json $A/reread-packets/Q{07,14}.*json $A/reader-packets/R{01,12}.*json; do echo \"$f\"; cut -c1-550 \"$f\" | head -8; done; rg -n '\"'\\\"reasons\\\"' \"'$B/S/answers/codex.json $A/P/answers/claude.json $A/S/answers/codex.json | cut -c1-190; nl -ba $B/TALLY.json | head -26; nl -ba $A/E/answers/claude.json | sed -n '\"'128,134p'; nl -ba \"'$A/S/answers/claude.json | sed -n '\"'243,247p'; nl -ba \"'$B/reread-packets/W07.md | head -36; rg -n '\"'single-reader|conjunction over|before any prompt change' AGENTS.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'A=poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22; B=poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23; for f in $A/reread2-packets/T{10,15}.*json $A/reread-packets/Q{07,14}.*json $A/reader-packets/R{01,12}.*json; do echo \"$f\"; cut -c1-550 \"$f\" | head -8; done; rg -n '\"'\\\"reasons\\\"' \"'$B/S/answers/codex.json $A/P/answers/claude.json $A/S/answers/codex.json | cut -c1-190; nl -ba $B/TALLY.json | head -26; nl -ba $A/E/answers/claude.json | sed -n '\"'128,134p'; nl -ba \"'$A/S/answers/claude.json | sed -n '\"'243,247p'; nl -ba \"'$B/reread-packets/W07.md | head -36; rg -n '\"'single-reader|conjunction over|before any prompt change' AGENTS.md\"","aggregated_output":"poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread2-packets/T10.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"Build of the four companies' 2026 capital expenditure from the shown guidance: Alphabet $195-205bn (e1254, F1), Meta $125-145bn (e1549, F2), Amazon $220bn (e1649, F3), plus a stated estimate for Microsoft of $175bn (F6), set between a run-rate build from its January-March 2026 quarter of $30.88bn up 84% (e922; 15% sequential growth gives about $154bn, checked) and the residual of the outside four-company tallies (e5014 $720-745bn, e1856 $745bn, e2458 $760bn) less the other three companies' guid\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread2-packets/T10.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v6\": {\n      \"supported\": \"no\",\n      \"ground\": \"F6 estimates $175 billion in Microsoft capital expenditure using e922, e4405 and outside estimates of the four companies’ total spending. F13 assumes 95–100% of combined capital expenditure goes to data centers, citing e1649, e5014, e1416, e4082 and e5376. That yields $679.25–745 billion, with a midpoint of $712.125 billion in rung 4.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"The passages support substantial data-center spending, but they do not establish that other spending accounts for at most 5% of the four companies’ combined capital expenditure. e1649 explains Amazon’s increase, and e5014 says a large portion goes to AI infrastructure without sizing it. The data-center estimates cover a broader or unspecified group. F8 is an outside estimate of total capital expenditure, so it does not independently show what the market expects for the data-center portion.\"\n    }\n  },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread2-packets/T15.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v41\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"F8 = apply_growth(F1 $20bn FY2027 CPU revenue from e3660, F7 85%) = 37, rung 3 ($30-40bn). The band rests on F7's stated assumption: management's 'more than double' (F2, e4856; repeated in e4691, e4764) is discounted for time and execution because it is a full next-fiscal-year target for a product still ramping, with no volume shown in hand (e4689 says Vera 'will be deployed'; e5044 says Anthropic is only evaluating it). The size of the discount is argued from the outside market pace of about \npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread2-packets/T15.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v41\": {\n      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n      \"ground\": \"The forecast applies an estimated 85% growth rate to the $20 billion FY2027 base (e3660, e4856), yielding $37 billion in rung 3. NVIDIA's doubling outlook (e4691, e4856) anchors the estimate. Broad planned deployments (e4689) support growth above the outside market benchmarks (e4687, e4857). Anthropic's evaluation (e5044) gives no order volume, and AMD's benchmark claims (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404) support allowing for competition as Vera ramps.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"\"\n    }\n  },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q07.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"F14 = F7 x F13. F7 ($715-745bn) is the sum of Alphabet guidance F1 $195-205bn (e1254), Meta guidance F2 $125-145bn (e1549), Amazon projection F3 $220bn (e1649) and the stated Microsoft estimate F6 $175bn (from e922's $30.88bn quarter up 84%, e4405's backlog, and the outside tallies). F13 is a stated 95-100% data-center share, reasoned from outlets that describe the same totals as data-center spending (e1416, e4082, e5376). The result is $679.25-745bn, midpoint about $712bn. The range overlaps r\n \"defects\": [{\"type\": \"two forecasts of one quarter\", \"figures\": [\"F7\", \"F6\", \"F8\", \"F9\", \"F10\"], \"detail\": \"The four companies' combined CY2026 capital expenditure gets two different values on the outcome's path. F7 computes it as $715-745bn. Inside F6's stated assumption, the outside tallies F8 ($720-745bn), F9 ($745bn) and F10 ($760bn) are used as the dollar value of that same quantity: tally minus the other three companies' $540-570bn is used to back out Microsoft ('roughly $175-190bn at their centres'; F10's centre would actually give abou\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q07.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v6\": {\n      \"supported\": \"no\",\n      \"ground\": \"e1254, e1549 and e1649 supply three companies' total-capex guidance. F6 assumes $175 billion for Microsoft using e922, e4405, e5014, e1856 and e2458. F13 assumes 95–100% of the resulting total goes to data centers, giving F14 a range of $679.25–745 billion and a midpoint in rung 4.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"The arithmetic from the assumed share to rung 4 is checkable, but the passages do not substantiate the 95–100% share. e5014 gives total capex and says a large portion goes to AI infrastructure. e1649 describes what drove Amazon's increase. e1416 and e4082 do not identify the same four companies, and e5376 covers five. The $720–745 billion reference is for total capex, so it does not independently ground the data-center forecast.\"\n    }\n  },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q14.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v41\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"e3660 gives the FY2027 base of $20 billion (F1); e4856 and e4691 tie that base and the FY2028 'more than double' to Vera CPU revenue (F2, giving the reference F3 = 20 x 2 = $40 billion, rung 2's bottom edge). The forecast rests on the stated assumption F7: 85% growth, the management doubling discounted for time and execution and tempered toward the ~50% market pace (e4687 via F4; e4857 via F5/F6), with adoption breadth (e4689, e5044, e4692) for and AMD's benchmark claims (e5399, e5400, e5401, \npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread-packets/Q14.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v41\": {\n      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n      \"ground\": \"The $20 billion FY2027 base comes from e3660 and e4856. The answer assumes 85% FY2028 growth, discounting management's doubling benchmark (e4691, e4856) for execution risk and AMD competition (e5399–e5402, e5404), while weighing Vera adoption (e4689, e5044) and CPU market growth (e4687, e4857). $20 billion × 1.85 = $37 billion, inside rung 3's $30 billion to under $40 billion band.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"\"\n    }\n  },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reader-packets/R01.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"Sourced guidance: Alphabet $195-205bn (e1254, F1), Meta $125-145bn (e1549, F2) and Amazon $220bn (e1649, F3). The writer's own Microsoft estimate of $175bn (F6) rests on e922 ($30.88bn for Jan-Mar 2026, up 84%), the e4405 backlog, and the outside four-company tallies less the other three companies. These sum to $715-745bn (F7; the arithmetic checks). A stated 95-100% data-center share (F13), which leans on e1416, e4082, e4766 and e5376 calling the ~$750-800bn totals data-center spending, gives \npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reader-packets/R01.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v6\": {\n      \"supported\": \"no\",\n      \"ground\": \"e1254, e1549 and e1649 supply three company capex figures. The answer adds its $175 billion Microsoft estimate and assumes that 95–100% of combined capex goes to data centers. That yields $679.25–745 billion, with a midpoint of $712.125 billion.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"The midpoint is in rung 4, but the stated forecast extends into rung 3. The answer's own assumptions allow that lower result. e5014 gives total capex, and the cited data-center estimates cover different or unspecified groups, so they do not establish the assumed 95–100% share for these four companies.\"\n    }\n  },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reader-packets/R12.claude.json\n{\"members\": {\"v41\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"F8 = F1 x (1 + F7) = 20 x 1.85 = 37, inside rung 3 (at least $30bn, below $40bn). F1 is the $20bn FY2027 CPU revenue guidance (e3660), tied to Vera CPU by e4856 and e4691. F7 is the stated 85% growth assumption: management's 'more than double' (F2, e4856) is the benchmark, discounted for time and execution toward the ~50% a year CPU-market pace (F4 from e4687; F5 to F6 from e4857, about 48% a year) and AMD's competing benchmark claims (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404), weighed against broad \npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reader-packets/R12.codex.json\n{\n  \"members\": {\n    \"v41\": {\n      \"supported\": \"yes\",\n      \"ground\": \"The FY2027 base is $20 billion (e3660, e4856). The writer assumes 85% FY2028 growth, discounting NVIDIA’s more-than-double outlook for execution risk and competition while allowing for broad Vera adoption (e4856, e4687, e4857, e4689, e5044, e5399–e5404). Applying 85% growth to $20 billion gives $37 billion, within rung 3.\",\n      \"why_not\": \"\"\n    }\n  },\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/answers/codex.json:257:      \"reasons\": \"I forecast $670.5 billion in CY2026 data-center capex, which falls in rung 3. The closest market benchmark \npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json:257:      \"reasons\": \"I forecast $720 billion of calendar 2026 data-center capex, in rung 4. The closest market reference, more t\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/P/answers/claude.json:390:      \"reasons\": \"Reference: Wall Street consensus of $411 billion for FY2027 (F19) sits in rung 2 ($392-431.2 billion); the\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/P/answers/claude.json:433:      \"reasons\": \"Reference: Morningstar's $361 billion FY2027 Data Center estimate (F20), the only quoted outside figure fo\n     1\t{\n     2\t \"verdict\": \"fail\",\n     3\t \"lines\": [\n     4\t  \"U01 P-v8  K-unresolved-unjustified  v8 unresolved settled=no     votes=['no', 'no'] [control expects no] ok\",\n     5\t  \"U02 S     claude                    v6 forecast   settled=split  votes=['no', 'yes', 'no', 'yes'] [a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer] MISS\",\n     6\t  \"U03 E     K-unsupported             v41 forecast   settled=no     votes=['no', 'no'] [control expects no] ok\",\n     7\t  \"U04 S     K-unresolved-justified    v6 unresolved settled=split  votes=['yes', 'no', 'yes', 'no'] [control expects yes] MISS\",\n     8\t  \"U05 E     codex                     v41 forecast   settled=no     votes=['no', 'no'] [a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer] MISS\",\n     9\t  \"U06 E     claude                    v41 forecast   settled=split  votes=['no', 'yes', 'no', 'yes'] [a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer] MISS\",\n    10\t  \"U07 P-v8  codex                     v8 forecast   settled=yes    votes=['yes', 'yes'] [a supported forecast] ok\",\n    11\t  \"U08 S     codex                     v6 forecast   settled=split  votes=['no', 'yes', 'no', 'yes'] [a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer] MISS\",\n    12\t  \"U09 P-v8  claude                    v8 forecast   settled=yes    votes=['yes', 'yes'] [a supported forecast] ok\"\n    13\t ],\n    14\t \"settled_defects\": [],\n    15\t \"split_defects\": [],\n    16\t \"refused\": []\n    17\t}   128\t      \"qualifier\": null,\n   129\t      \"inputs\": null,\n   130\t      \"operation\": null,\n   131\t      \"basis_text\": \"My estimate of FY2028 Vera CPU revenue growth on the FY2027 base. Management's 'more than double' (F2, at least 100% growth) is the benchmark, but it is a target for a fiscal year that has not started, for a product line still ramping, so it is discounted for time and execution rather than taken in full. Outside views put CPU market growth near 50% a year (AMD, F4; Bank of America's fivefold path from F5 to F6), so a doubling needs large share gains. Broad announced adoption (every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and OEM, e4689; Anthropic evaluating Vera, e5044; Arm servers' large revenue share, e4692) supports a pace well above the market, while AMD's own benchmark claims against Vera (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404) show standalone CPU share will be contested. Weighting the company target most heavily, tempered toward the market pace, gives 85%.\",\n   132\t      \"rests_on\": [\"F2\", \"F4\", \"F5\", \"F6\", \"e4689\", \"e5044\", \"e4692\", \"e5399\", \"e5400\", \"e5401\", \"e5402\", \"e5404\"],\n   133\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   134\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   243\t      \"inputs\": null,\n   244\t      \"operation\": null,\n   245\t      \"basis_text\": \"My estimate. Nearly all of these companies' capital expenditure is servers, chips, networking, buildings and leases for data centers (Amazon's latest increase was mostly memory chip costs; Zacks says a large portion goes to AI infrastructure), and outside sources describe the same roughly $750-800bn totals as data-center spending. A small remainder (offices, logistics and other assets) is not data-center spending, so the share is taken at 95-100%; no company reports a separate data-center line, so reported capital expenditure is how the outcome will be measured in practice.\",\n   246\t      \"rests_on\": [\"F11\", \"F12\", \"e4082\", \"e5014\", \"e1649\"],\n   247\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n     1\t# Blind reading (judging-v13s blind test, re-read under the comparison ruling, D747)\n     2\t\n     3\tYou are given one forecasting request (the questions, their ladders, and every passage the writer was shown), the system text and the\n     4\tformat rules the writer was given, and one answer written in that format. You are not told who wrote the answer or whether any check\n     5\taccepted it. Read only what is given here.\n     6\t\n     7\tJudge in two steps:\n     8\t1. Check each source figure against its passage's quote and each computed figure against its inputs, using the format rules below.\n     9\t   What the format rules permit is not a defect.\n    10\t2. For each member answered as a forecast, name the evidence for the assumption that decides its band, and say whether the\n    11\t   passages support its size or a bound that justifies the rung, or, where the passages do not give the asked quantity, whether\n    12\t   the answer explains a related figure as the definitions below require. For each member answered unresolved, name the gap that\n    13\t   remains after the strongest relevant passages, and say whether the passages indeed cannot settle the rung.\n    14\t\n    15\tReturn JSON:\n    16\t{\"members\": {\"<view id>\": {\"supported\": \"yes|no\", \"ground\": \"<the passage ids or the stated assumption that put the forecast in its band>\",\n    17\t                           \"why_not\": \"<if no: what is missing or wrong>\"}},\n    18\t \"defects\": [{\"type\": \"<one of: number misread | wrong arithmetic | part above or added into its whole | two forecasts of one quarter |\n    19\t               own estimate passed off as the market's reference | broken structure>\", \"figures\": [\"<figure ids>\"], \"detail\": \"...\"}]}\n    20\t\n    21\tDefinitions (judge each against the passages and the format rules the writer was given, shown below; what the format rules permit is\n    22\tnot a defect):\n    23\t- supported: the forecast follows from the shown passages and the answer's stated assumptions; a reader could check it against them.\n    24\t  For a forecast, the assumption that decides the band must rest on the passages in one of three ways: its size, a bound that\n    25\t  justifies the rung, or, when the passages do not give the asked quantity, a related figure that the answer explains. An explained\n    26\t  related figure satisfies this requirement even though the other side of the line remains possible. It is explained when the\n    27\t  answer says why the comparison applies to the companies, the period and the quantity asked, and why, under the answer's own stated\n    28\t  assumptions, the chosen side of the line is more likely; an answer that does not explain this is not supported (a ruling). For an unresolved\n    29\t  member, supported means its reason holds: after the strongest relevant passages, a gap remains that keeps the rung unsettled (a\n    30\t  ruling).\n    31\t- number misread: a source figure's number, scale, sign or qualifier differs from what its passage's quote writes, allowing what the\n    32\t  format rules permit.\n    33\t- wrong arithmetic: a computed figure does not equal its operation on its inputs, within the tolerance the format rules state.\n    34\t- part above or added into its whole: a segment is larger than the total it belongs to, or is added again into a total that already\n    35\t  includes it (directly or through an intermediate figure or an estimate).\n    36\t- two forecasts of one quarter: two different figures are used as the value of one quantity and period. A growth rate computed from\n200:- **The prompt audit runs after every measurement and before any prompt change** (`.claude/skills/prompt-audit/SKILL.md`). The whole\n289:- **Measure the stick before using it:** control against a second control, the criterion's null estimated (a conjunction over many items\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"R=poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23; O=poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22; rg -n '\\\"reasons\\\"|basis_text.*broader' \\\"\"'$R\"/S/answers/codex.json \"$O\"/P/answers/claude.json | cut -c1-240; nl -ba \"$R\"/reread-packets/W08.claude.json; rg -n '\"'\\\"decision\\\": \\\"publish\\\"' \\\"\"'$O/CHECKED.json\" \"$R/CHECKED.json\"; nl -ba \"$R/TALLY.json\" | head -28; nl -ba \"$R/S/answers/codex.json\" | sed -n '\"'222,230p'; rg -n 'D725|no data|hallucin' docs/DECISIONS-log.md | tail -8 | cut -c1-240\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"R=poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23; O=poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22; rg -n '\\\"reasons\\\"|basis_text.*broader' \\\"\"'$R\"/S/answers/codex.json \"$O\"/P/answers/claude.json | cut -c1-240; nl -ba \"$R\"/reread-packets/W08.claude.json; rg -n '\"'\\\"decision\\\": \\\"publish\\\"' \\\"\"'$O/CHECKED.json\" \"$R/CHECKED.json\"; nl -ba \"$R/TALLY.json\" | head -28; nl -ba \"$R/S/answers/codex.json\" | sed -n '\"'222,230p'; rg -n 'D725|no data|hallucin' docs/DECISIONS-log.md | tail -8 | cut -c1-240\"","aggregated_output":"poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json:257:      \"reasons\": \"I forecast $720 billion of calendar 2026 data-center capex, in rung 4. The closest market reference, more than $730 billion of AI infrastructure spending for\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/P/answers/claude.json:390:      \"reasons\": \"Reference: Wall Street consensus of $411 billion for FY2027 (F19) sits in rung 2 ($392-431.2 billion); the 87-88% consensus growth rates (e4990, e5129) on F\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/P/answers/claude.json:433:      \"reasons\": \"Reference: Morningstar's $361 billion FY2027 Data Center estimate (F20), the only quoted outside figure for the segment, sits in rung 2 ($350-400 billion). \n     1\t{\"members\": {\"v6\": {\"supported\": \"yes\", \"ground\": \"Total CY2026 capex for the same four companies of $745bn (F11), resting on the passage tallies e1856 (near $745bn), e5014/e2079 ($720-745bn), e2458 ($760bn) and e1947 (more than $730bn on AI infrastructure), less a stated $25bn non-data-center deduction (F12) sized from e1947's AI-infrastructure tally sitting close to total capex (implying roughly $15bn outside it) and Meta's $130bn AI capex (e3686) against the $135bn midpoint of its $125-145bn guidance (e1549). The answer explains why the related figure (total capex) applies: same four companies, same year, AI spending nearly all of capex, so a small deduction keeps the outcome ($720bn) above the $700bn line; e1947's more-than-$730bn for AI infrastructure alone also bounds data-center spending near or above the line.\", \"why_not\": \"\"}}, \"defects\": []}\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/CHECKED.json:69:  \"decision\": \"publish\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/CHECKED.json:139:  \"decision\": \"publish\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/CHECKED.json:196:  \"decision\": \"publish\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/CHECKED.json:243:  \"decision\": \"publish\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/CHECKED.json:296:  \"decision\": \"publish\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/CHECKED.json:360:  \"decision\": \"publish\",\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/CHECKED.json:59:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/CHECKED.json:129:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/CHECKED.json:214:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/CHECKED.json:265:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/CHECKED.json:355:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/CHECKED.json:424:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/CHECKED.json:484:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/CHECKED.json:541:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/CHECKED.json:586:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/CHECKED.json:641:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/CHECKED.json:676:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\npoc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/CHECKED.json:720:  \"decision\": \"publish\"\n     1\t{\n     2\t \"verdict\": \"fail\",\n     3\t \"lines\": [\n     4\t  \"U01 P-v8  K-unresolved-unjustified  v8 unresolved settled=no     votes=['no', 'no'] [control expects no] ok\",\n     5\t  \"U02 S     claude                    v6 forecast   settled=split  votes=['no', 'yes', 'no', 'yes'] [a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer] MISS\",\n     6\t  \"U03 E     K-unsupported             v41 forecast   settled=no     votes=['no', 'no'] [control expects no] ok\",\n     7\t  \"U04 S     K-unresolved-justified    v6 unresolved settled=split  votes=['yes', 'no', 'yes', 'no'] [control expects yes] MISS\",\n     8\t  \"U05 E     codex                     v41 forecast   settled=no     votes=['no', 'no'] [a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer] MISS\",\n     9\t  \"U06 E     claude                    v41 forecast   settled=split  votes=['no', 'yes', 'no', 'yes'] [a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer] MISS\",\n    10\t  \"U07 P-v8  codex                     v8 forecast   settled=yes    votes=['yes', 'yes'] [a supported forecast] ok\",\n    11\t  \"U08 S     codex                     v6 forecast   settled=split  votes=['no', 'yes', 'no', 'yes'] [a supported forecast or a justified unresolved answer] MISS\",\n    12\t  \"U09 P-v8  claude                    v8 forecast   settled=yes    votes=['yes', 'yes'] [a supported forecast] ok\"\n    13\t ],\n    14\t \"settled_defects\": [],\n    15\t \"split_defects\": [],\n    16\t \"refused\": []\n    17\t}   222\t      \"quote_number\": null,\n   223\t      \"qualifier\": null,\n   224\t      \"inputs\": null,\n   225\t      \"operation\": null,\n   226\t      \"basis_text\": \"I estimate a $25 billion deduction for spending outside data centers. The four-company AI infrastructure tally is close to my total-capex forecast, and Meta's AI capex estimate is close to the midpoint of its companywide guidance. Those comparisons support a small deduction. I allow room for different spending mixes across the companies and for some AI infrastructure spending to fall outside data centers. The passages do not give a company-by-company split.\",\n   227\t      \"rests_on\": [\"F1\", \"F7\", \"F8\", \"F11\"],\n   228\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   229\t      \"reference_for\": []\n   230\t    },\n11126:- **Registered result (D725's lines, scored as written, D680):** all 72 answers refused by `calc_check.py`, none published; the joint\n11129:- **What it measures:** not D725's question (whether one call over a family gives better numbers), only that the answer format, as\n11163:    - Only then is a re-send of the same 36 requests asked for, under a new decision with D725's lines unchanged.\n11180:  - A clean result goes back to Alex as a request to re-send the same 36 requests under a new decision, with D725's lines\n11194:  - Codex answered that the six drop C8's guard against adding a segment into its total, a defect D725 named. Shrinking changes the\n11255:    D725's lines.\n11501:  - Claude agrees: the weakness is unsupported precision in a number that decides the rung, which is the goal's own failure (D725).\n11552:    - Claude withdrew two reasons for the lean. D725 does not promise that code catches every invented figure. AGENTS §8's \"no data to\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"agent_message","text":"**The FAIL is real.** W07 forecasts $705.6B of data-center spending, puts total spending at $720B, and calls **more than $730B** the floor for data centers. Both adjudicators upheld that contradiction. The three split votes affect what the council can conclude about reader agreement. They do not affect the stop. [W07 answer:390](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json:390) · [Recorded stop:30](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/REREAD-TALLY.json:30)\n\n1. **Serious — The arithmetic passed because the writer used the $730B figure in two different ways.** The source says the four companies indicated more than $730B of **AI infrastructure** spending. The writer recorded that figure correctly and cited it to justify a 98% share. The calculation then took 98% of total spending. The word *floor* appears in the explanation, where it makes a stronger claim about a different quantity. Even the top of the code’s recomputed data-center range, $720.3B, falls below that floor. The request declares no part–whole relation for this single question, so the code has no pair to compare. **Merely linking the source figure would not fix this.** It is already linked to the 98% estimate. What is missing is the claim that it imposes a minimum on the outcome. D738 deliberately leaves that interpretation to readers. [Source and request:29](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:29) · [Declared relations:5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:5) · [Existing link:264](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/claude.json:264) · [Code comparison:655](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:655) · [Published range:62](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/CHECKED.json:62) · [D738:11265](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11265)\n\n   I compared the figures and explanations in all **18 original writer replies**. I found **0 of 12** in the third run and **1 of 6** in the targeted test with an explicit floor for a part above the writer’s own total. W08 is the closest counterexample. It uses the same $730B source, forecasts $720B out of $745B, and expressly allows some AI infrastructure spending to fall outside data centers. The third run also has a wrong $138B quarterly example in prose, a different arithmetic error. The narrow defect occurred once in this set. These replies cannot establish its production frequency. [Third-run replies:487](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/TALLY.json:487) · [Targeted replies:5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:5) · [W08’s distinction:226](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/answers/codex.json:226) · [Quarterly example:390](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/P/answers/claude.json:390)\n\n2. **Serious — The three splits have different explanations.** Alex allowed a related figure when the writer explains why it fits the companies, period and quantity, and why the writer’s assumptions favor the chosen side. The other side may remain possible. My readings are as follows. [D747:11582](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11582)\n\n   | Answer | My reading | What the earlier readings establish |\n   |---|---|---|\n   | **T10, four-company spending** | **No. Codex applies D747 better.** At $730B total, crossing $700B requires about 95.9% to go to data centers. The $750B estimates do not identify the four companies. The nearly $800B estimate covers five. The writer calls these “the same totals” and treats the passage naming the four as a repeat. Higher tracking estimates help explain *total* spending. The midpoint of a range built from the assumed share does little to explain the share itself. | Spending support was initially accepted, then split in the same direction in two earlier readings and again here. That is a recurring disagreement about company and spending coverage. T10’s earlier quotation defect was excluded from this vote. [Writer:245](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/S/answers/claude.json:245) · [First result:487](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/TALLY.json:487) · [First re-read:282](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD-TALLY.json:282) · [Second re-read:144](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json:144) · [Current disagreement:5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reread-adjudication/W03.codex.json:5) |\n   | **T15, Vera revenue** | **Yes on the $30–40B band. Codex applies D747 better, narrowly.** On the writer’s stated $20B Vera base, market-rate growth gives about $30B and doubling gives $40B. The writer explains why broad adoption favors gaining share and why deployment still under evaluation and competing products temper the doubling forecast. Claude identifies a real weakness. A market average through 2030 says little by itself about one product in FY2028. The writer supplies a reason for using the comparison, though, and D747 permits that judgment. The evidence does not establish 85% growth to that precision. | This answer was accepted in all three earlier readings, including by both families in the last one. It is the strongest evidence of reader variation. The instructions also changed, so the reversal cannot measure a random disagreement rate. [Writer:131](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/claude.json:131) · [Earlier results:500](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/TALLY.json:500) · [First re-read:337](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD-TALLY.json:337) · [Second re-read:169](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json:169) · [Claude’s objection:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reread-adjudication/W06.claude.json:1) |\n   | **U02, support for the $705.6B forecast** | **No. Codex applies D747 better.** Claude upheld the impossible floor but still counted it toward support because both dollar amounts landed in the same band. D747 asks whether the explanation works under the writer’s *own* assumptions. Removing the floor would leave a different argument. At $720B total, 98% clears the line and the writer’s own 96% gives $691.2B. | The same answer already split Codex no, Claude yes, at both levels. The new finding is that both adjudicators now recognize the contradiction. The support vote stayed split. [Earlier votes:5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:5) · [Claude’s yes and upheld defect:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reread-adjudication/W07.claude.json:1) · [Codex’s reasoning:5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/reread-adjudication/W07.codex.json:5) |\n\n3. **Minor — The registration demanded more agreement than the evidence justified.** Six of nine answers settled. All three controls came out as expected. Three of the four earlier targeted-test splits settled, which is genuine progress. All **six** adjudicator support votes on the three remaining splits still followed their own family’s first vote. Which family was stricter changed with the question. T10 and T15 expose judgment about the words *explains why* and *more likely*. U02 exposes a more specific difference between accepting a plausible band and accepting the writer’s stated route to it. D747 already gives a reason to reject U02. I see no missing general clause that another read of these answers would settle. Equally, the record does not support Claude’s conclusion that all three splits are random reader variation. Requiring every disputed answer to settle was a demanding, uncalibrated test of agreement. Its registered outcome still stands. [Votes:4](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/REREAD-TALLY.json:4) · [Registered conditions:428](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:428) · [Claude’s conclusion:9](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/claude-r63.md:9)\n\n4. **Serious — The weakest proposal is to pass a writer test on stable controls and confirmed defects alone.** The product question is whether a **fresh E answer** invents the adjustment that moves Vera below $40B. U05 shows why that outcome must count. Both readers rejected its $36B forecast, yet the test recorded zero named defects. Claude’s proposed scoring could let a repeat through. A second weakness is the proposed code change. W08 shows why an indirect comparison must be allowed to influence a forecast without becoming a binding minimum. One contradictory floor warrants a case in the test. It does not establish a need to change the calculation format. Finally, D746 made writer repair conditional on agreement, and the registration sends persistent splits back to Alex. Moving forward now needs his decision. [U05 and empty defect list:8](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:8) · [Claude’s proposed scoring:44](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/claude-r63.md:44) · [D746 condition:11564](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11564) · [Registered split outcome:434](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:434)\n\n**One recommendation.** Ask Alex to authorize the E writer repair despite the three disclosed splits. Have the writer decide whether the evidence favors one side of $40B **before** choosing a discount. If it can explain only why sales *might* fall short, it should say what remains unknown. Carry W07 into verification and defer a change to the calculation format. Another vote on the same nine answers would leave the writer untouched. The paid re-send stays on hold. [D746’s proposed repair:11566](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11566)\n\n**One test.** After approval and council review of the changed request, run one $0 blind test with fresh Codex and Claude writers on E, S and the previously supported data-center revenue question, P-v8. Mix in saved U05 and W07 to verify readers catch an unsupported cut and an impossible floor. To move forward, **each fresh E and S answer must earn agreement for its forecast or its specific reason for saying “can’t tell.”** Both P-v8 answers must still produce supported forecasts. The code must accept the fresh replies, and no confirmed defect may survive. A settled unsupported answer or contradictory floor fails. A persistent support split is inconclusive and leaves spending on hold. Only a clean result supports asking Alex to authorize the paid re-send. [Existing writer-test conditions:375](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:375)"}}
